\par\par \b \uc2 Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 :Dlr Shaky Vs Majors; \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Fears Support  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Miyako Takebe  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 725 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Dezember 2003\par 06:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The dollar was shaky against the yen and the euro in Asia Tuesday, Tokyo's last trading day of the year, though it held above overnight lows partly due to fears of Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Dollar bearishness, stemming broadly from concern over the U.S. twin fiscal and current account deficits, continued to dominate the market, keeping the euro afloat near $1.2500 and the yen hovering near Y107 for most of the session.  \par \par \uc2 "It's not really an issue now of who's selling, or who's buying (other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 )," said Katsuhiro Kaneko, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Mitsubishi Securities. "The dollar's just weak."  \par \par \uc2 Still, the threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese authorities helped support the dollar against the yen and keep sell-stops lined up near Y106.80 from being triggered. "There's a fear that if we sell into any fall, we'll get burned," Kaneko said.  \par \par \uc2 While Tokyo markets will be closed from Wednesday until Jan. 5, traders expect Japanese Finance Ministry officials to continue to closely monitor the market, making \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  possible at any time during the holidays.  \par \par \uc2 "Japanese authorities have been dropping none-too-subtle hints through both the market and the media," said one Tokyo-based trader, citing some suspect price moves earlier Tuesday that helped bring the dollar back toward Y107.  \par \par \uc2 Satoshi Tate, vice president of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at UFJ Bank, said "it's best not to forget the 1999/2000 New Year's holidays," when Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Jan. 4 hoisted the dollar off lows in the mid-Y101s by some Y1.5 in one day. That \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was well-timed and succeeded in bringing the dollar back up to near Y106 by the end of the same week, he added.  \par \par \uc2 "If it looks like speculative yen-buying might accelerate over the holidays this year, Japanese exporters might receive a modest New Year's present from the authorities," Tate said.  \par \par \uc2 At 0515 GMT, the dollar was quoted at Y107.02, above Y107.00 late Monday in New York. The dollar was at Y106.97 late Monday in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 The euro was at $1.2499, higher than $1.2485 late Monday in New York. The euro was at $1.2460 in Tokyo Tuesday. Against the yen, the euro was quoted at Y133.76, up from Y133.59 in New York late Monday. The euro was at Y133.28 in Tokyo late Monday.  \par \par \uc2 Sterling was at $1.7747, up from $1.7735 in New York but lower than $1.7756 in Tokyo Monday.  \par \par \uc2 Still, some traders remain skeptical of just how long Japan can fight the dollar's decline, despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's plan to add some Y61 trillion to its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  arsenal early next year. The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has also agreed to provide up to Y10 trillion in emergency funding for the MOF if needed.  \par \par \uc2 Mitsubishi's Kaneko said that even taking into account the fact that the MOF's aim is to smooth the dollar's fall - not target a specific level to push the dollar up to, as in the past - "those placing bids have got to be feeling how massive and heavy the selling pressure they're up against is."  \par \par \uc2 Against the euro, the dollar is expected to extend its gains during and after the holidays, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could rise to as high as $1.2700 in early January as U.S. and European long-term investors continue to move "real money" into euro-zone assets, said a trader at a major Japanese bank.  \par \par \uc2 "The euro could renew its record high almost every day" in the week starting Jan. 5, he said. But he added that the dollar would likely remain within an established range against the yen over the same period given \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Miyako Takebe, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; miyako.takebe@dowjones.com   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Hugh Lawson  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The chart of late Tokyo \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates that accompanied Asia \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been replaced with an enhanced \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fixture that is updated hourly.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Subscribers can find this table:  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -On Telerate pages 4046 and 1087.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -On Dow Jones Newswires by searching the code N/IFX  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -On Reuters by entering FRX or INT  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -On Bloomberg by entering NI FRX  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1074  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031230dzcu0003q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
