\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar plumbs new depths beyond $1.25 per euro.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By John Parry  \par \uc2 659 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Dezember 2003\par 15:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Dec 29 (Reuters) - The ailing dollar on Monday hit record lows against the euro beyond the key $1.25 level and shed half a percent versus the yen on security worries and nagging concerns over the U.S. current account deficit.  \par \par \uc2 Technically driven trade predominated, analysts said.  \par \par \uc2 "The main negative is momentum (which) remains bearish," said Peter Frank, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with ABN Amro bank in Chicago.  \par \par \uc2 "There is no key impulse from data ... nor anything going on in the stock market: it seems to be technically driven and momentum-driven. The dollar is getting hammered against all the major cross rates," Frank added.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said weak U.S. economic data late last week and a rise in Japan's Nikkei share average to seven-week highs, which was helping the yen, added to dollar woes.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In thin holiday trade, the euro  climbed as far as $1.2511, breaking\par through an important psychological level at $1.2500. The dollar also\par hit an 11-year low against sterling        and came within sight of\par last week's seven-year low versus the Swiss franc       .\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Early Monday morning in New York, the euro was up 0.6 percent on the day at $1.2499, up more than 19 percent since the start of the year. Against the yen , the dollar was down 0.5 percent at 106.89 yen, just above a three-year trough around 106.75 yen, with the market wary that Japanese authorities could \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to check further dollar weakness.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders suspect the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Friday when the dollar fell to a low of 106.93 yen. Some said the BOJ may have stepped in earlier on Monday, when the greenback fell to that level again.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Against the Swiss franc  the dollar was at 1.2477 francs, down 0.5\par percent on the day. The pound        was up 0.2 percent at $1.7753,\par after hitting 11-year highs above $1.7762 earlier in illiquid holiday\par season trade.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The market remains thin, so moves are exaggerated. But you can't get away from the underlying problems that are surrounding the dollar, particularly the current account deficit," said Paul Robson, international economist at Bank One.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. current account deficit, around 5 percent of gross domestic product, is proving one of the biggest weights on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Poor economic data last week and geopolitical concerns are also undermining the dollar," Robson added.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro's move higher against the greenback gathered pace after the German government said it was not worried about euro strength, adding there was no reason to consider taking measures to try to counter it.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This contrasted with a report in Monday's Financial Times that quoted a senior European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  official as saying the decline in the dollar had been relatively sharp and the euro's performance over the next four to eight weeks could influence the outlook on interest rates.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The discovery of mad cow disease in the United States encouraged traders to shun the greenback. U.S. officials on Sunday expanded the recall of more than 10,000 pounds of beef to eight states and Guam.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts added that expectations the U.S. Federal Reserve would keep interest rates on hold for some time continued to undermine the greenback.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Once again the market is concerned about U.S. growth prospects. We had weak durable goods data, and then on Friday comments from (Dallas Federal Reserve Bank President Robert) McTeer that the U.S. is in no rush to hike rates," said Steven Saywell, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Citibank.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 There was no major economic data out of the United States on Monday, but later in the week, consumer confidence, Chicago PMI and the Institute for Supply Management reports could all influence dollar sentiment, analysts said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Additional reporting by Dhara Ranasinghe and Christina Fincher in London).   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031229dzct002yq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
