\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar extends slide beyond $1.25 per euro.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Dhara Ranasinghe  \par \uc2 685 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Dezember 2003\par 14:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Dec 29 (Reuters) - The sickly dollar on Monday hit record lows against the euro beyond the key $1.25 level and shed half a percent versus the yen on security worries and nagging concerns over the U.S. current account deficit.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said that weak U.S. economic data late last week and a rise in the Nikkei share average to seven-week highs, which was helping the yen, added to dollar woes.  \par \par \uc2 In thin holiday trade, the euro rose as far as $1.2511, breaking through a psychological level. The dollar also hit an 11-year low against sterling and came within sight of last week's seven-year low versus the Swiss franc.  \par \par \uc2 "The market remains thin so moves are exaggerated but you can't get away from the underlying problems that are surrounding the dollar, particularly the current account deficit," said Paul Robson, international economist at Bank One.  \par \par \uc2 "Poor economic data last week and geopolitical concerns are also undermining the dollar."  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was holding near its lowest level since early December at 106.92 yen at 1255 GMT, just above a three-year trough around 106.75, with the market wary that Japanese authorities could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent further dollar weakness.  \par \par \uc2 "Price action is dollar-driven and in a holiday-thinned market there has been nothing to turn dollar bearish sentiment around," said Aziz McMahon, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN Amro.  \par \par \uc2 The euro has gained more than 19 percent against the dollar since the start of the year as doubts have grown over the United States' ability to fund its current account deficit, which runs at around five percent of gross domestic product.  \par \par \uc2 GERMANY NOT WORRIED ABOUT EURO  \par \par \uc2 The euro's move higher against the greenback gathered pace after the German government said it was not worried about euro strength, adding there was no reason to consider taking measures to try to counter it.  \par \par \uc2 This contrasted with a report in Monday's Financial Times which quoted a senior European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official as saying the decline in the dollar had been relatively sharp and the euro's performance over the next four to eight weeks could influence the outlook on interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 The discovery of mad cow disease in the United States encouraged traders to shun the greenback. U.S. officials on Sunday expanded the recall of more than 10,000 pounds of beef to eight most western states and Guam.  \par \par \uc2 "Mad cow disease is another reason to sell dollars," said one trader in London. "Everyone is long euros and they would be a fool not to be."  \par \par \uc2 Analysts added that expectations the U.S. Federal Reserve would keep interest rates on hold for some time were also undermining the greenback.  \par \par \uc2 "Once again the market is concerned about U.S. growth prospects. We had weak durable goods data and then on Friday, comments from McTeer that the U.S. is in no rush to hike rates," said Steven Saywell, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Citibank.  \par \par \uc2 Dallas Fed President Robert McTeer said on Friday that U.S. inflation was still dormant and until it became a clear danger the Fed could maintain its current loose monetary policy.  \par \par \uc2 "Interest rate hike expectations continue to decline and this is weighing on the dollar," Saywell added.  \par \par \uc2 DATA VACUUM  \par \par \uc2 There is no major economic data out of the United States on Monday, with only a handful of indicators this week including the Chicago PMI and consumer confidence.  \par \par \uc2 Boosted by hopes that a good start for Wall Street next year will also boost Tokyo stock prices, the benchmark Nikkei average closed at 10,500.62, the highest close since November 10.  \par \par \uc2 Players suspect the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday when the dollar fell to a low of 106.93 yen. Some said the BOJ may have stepped in earlier on Monday when the greenback fell to that level again.  \par \par \uc2 On Friday, Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said that Japan would act on exchange rates if the market strayed from economic fundamentals. (Additional reporting by Christina Fincher).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031229dzct002qa\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
