\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar totters towards lows on euro, yen.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Charlotte Cooper  \par \uc2 571 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Dezember 2003\par 09:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  LONDON, Dec 29 (Reuters) - The dollar fell towards recent lows against the yen and euro on Monday as traders returning from holiday sold the greenback on concerns about U.S. security and current account deficit issues.   \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there were no fresh factors behind Monday's dollar-selling although some said a rise in the Nikkei share average above 10,500 was prompting some yen-buying.   \par \par \uc2  "Sentiment for the dollar remains poor across the board," said Tim Fox, market strategist at National Australia Bank in London.   \par \par \uc2  "There are ongoing terrorist concerns and the mad cow scare from last week is still an excuse to sell dollars as well."   \par \par \uc2  By 0830 GMT the euro was within spitting distance of last week's record high around $1.2470.   \par \par \uc2  The dollar was holding near its lowest level since early December at 106.98 yen, just above a three-year trough around 106.75, with the market wary that Japanese authorities could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent further dollar weakness.   \par \par \uc2  DATA VACUUM   \par \par \uc2  There is no major economic data out of the United States on Monday, with only a handful of indicators this week including the Chicago PMI and consumer confidence.   \par \par \uc2  "The dollar-selling trend is unchanged," said Katsunori Kitakura, manager of the treasury department at Chuo Mitsui Trust and Banking.   \par \par \uc2  "The market's focus is on the U.S. twin deficits and as long as that does not change, I don't think the dollar will be bought."   \par \par \uc2  Boosted by hopes that a good start for Wall Street next year will also push up Tokyo stock prices, the benchmark Nikkei average closed at a seven-week high of 10,500.62, the highest close since November 10.   \par \par \uc2  Players suspect the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday when the dollar fell to a low of 106.93 yen. Some said the BOJ may have stepped in earlier on Monday when the greenback fell to that level again.   \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will almost certainly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  so I don't think many players will want to push dollar/yen down," said Toru Umemoto, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Morgan Stanley in Tokyo.   \par \par \uc2  On Friday, Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said that Japan would act on exchange rates if the market strayed from economic fundamentals.   \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  also said on Friday that the BOJ had agreed to a government request for short-term funding to soothe worries it may soon run out of funds to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.   \par \par \uc2  The BOJ policy board agreed to issue three-month repo pacts for up to 10 trillion yen ($93 billion).   \par \par \uc2  Looking ahead, Morgan Stanley's Umemoto said an upcoming meeting of Group of Seven (G7) finance ministers due early next year could become a key event for the market.   \par \par \uc2  "I don't think EU finance ministers are very pleased about the euro's recent strength," he said, adding that they could voice such concerns at the next G7 meeting.   \par \par \uc2  A report in Monday's Financial Times quoted a senior European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official as saying the decline in the dollar had been relatively sharp and the euro's performance over the next four to eight weeks could influence 