\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar slips as US deficit worries linger.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Kazunori Takada  \par \uc2 455 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Dezember 2003\par 07:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Dec 29 (Reuters) - The dollar fell towards recent lows against the euro and the yen on Monday as traders returned from Christmas holiday and sold the greenback on mounting geopolitical worries and the yawning U.S. current account deficit.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said there were no fresh factors behind Monday's dollar-selling although some said a rise in the Nikkei share average above 10,500 was prompting some yen-buying.  \par \par \uc2 "The dollar-selling trend is unchanged," said Katsunori Kitakura, manager of the treasury department at Chuo Mitsui Trust and Banking.  \par \par \uc2 "The market's focus is on the U.S. twin deficit and as long as that does not change, I don't think the dollar will be bought."  \par \par \uc2 Boosted by hopes that a good start for Wall Street next year will also push up Tokyo stock prices, the benchmark Nikkei average closed at a seven-week high of 10,500.62, the highest close since November 10.  \par \par \uc2 As of 0623 GMT, the euro was pushing up to $1.2454/59 just short of a record high around $1.2470 hit last week. It fetched 1.2425/31 in late U.S. trade on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar also matched a two-week low of 106.93 yen drifting close to a three-year trough of 106.74 hit earlier in the month.  \par \par \uc2 But dealers said fears of possible Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  were halting further falls for now.  \par \par \uc2 Players suspect the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday when the dollar fell to a low of 106.93 yen. Some said the BOJ may have stepped in earlier on Monday when the greenback fell to that level again.  \par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will almost certainly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  so I don't think many players will want to push dollar/yen down," said Toru Umemoto, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Morgan Stanley in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 On Friday, Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said that Japan would act on exchange rates if the market strayed from economic fundamentals.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  also said on Friday that the BOJ had agreed to a government request for short-term funding to soothe worries it may soon run out of funds to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.  \par \par \uc2 The BOJ policy board agreed to issue three-month repo pacts for up to 10 trillion yen ($93 billion).  \par \par \uc2 Looking ahead, Morgan Stanley's Umemoto said an upcoming meeting of Group of Seven (G7) finance ministers due early next year could become a key event for the market.  \par \par \uc2 "I don't think EU finance ministers are very pleased about the euro's recent strength," he said, adding that they could voice such concerns at the next G7 meeting. ($1=107.26 Yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031229dzct001pa\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
