\par\par \b \uc2 BOJ Watch: Deal With MOF May Help BOJ Buy Time On Easing  \b0\par\par\uc2 704 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Dezember 2003\par 07:22\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Hiroshi Inoue \par  \par    A Dow Jones Newswires Column \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The Bank of Japan's decision last week to lend funds to the government for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  might have a surprising consequence for the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's monetary policy.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 It might allow the BOJ to hold off from easing policy further for now by keeping short-term rates from rising too sharply, people familiar with BOJ thinking say.  \par \par \uc2 That's because Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  can get the funds it needs for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by using the new temporary framework with the BOJ, rather than by issuing more financing bills as it usually does when it needs money for yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Floating more FBs could cause short-term rates to shoot up because the supply of the bills will increase, at a time of the year when those rates tend to rise in any case ahead of the fiscal year-end on March 31.  \par \par \uc2 But if the new MOF-BOJ deal means the ministry will issue fewer FBs than it has to, that would likely limit the rise in short-term rates, meaning Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would have one lesson reason for being forced to ease policy.  \par \par \uc2 Because of Friday's deal, "sharp rises in FB and market rates won't happen," said a senior money trader at a major brokerage. The average accepted rate for FBs at the tender on Dec. 24 was 0.0032%.  \par \par \uc2 The ministry had been expected in coming months to increase its weekly FB issuance amount from the current Y4.2 trillion, partly because it needs funds to balance the government's books between March and May, but also because it needed more money for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "Judging from current conditions, (weekly) FB issues would have risen to Y5 trillion or more" eventually, but for now the ministry won't be boosting FB issuance because it can rely on the deal with the BOJ to get funds for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the senior money trader said.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Yen Rise May Force BOJ To Act \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Under the framework announced Friday, the BOJ said it would supply up to Y10 trillion to the government as \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  funds by buying U.S. Treasury bonds on a repurchase basis if the need arose.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The deal was put into place because after repeated massive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market this year to keep the yen from rising, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was approaching its limit for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  funds, and needed a stopgap source of funds before parliament could approve increases in the limit early next year.  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has spent Y17.8 trillion in yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the first 11 months of the year, bringing the cumulative \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  total to about Y72 trillion, close to the current limit of Y79 trillion.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Even so, Tokyo hasn't had much luck in halting the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's rise. The dollar dropped below Y107.00 Monday, compared with about Y119.90 at the end of last year.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Cooperation with the government on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  front might also give the BOJ breathing space on monetary policy if it keeps the yen from rising too sharply.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some analysts think that a further surge in the yen might force the BOJ to loosen policy to offset the possible damage on the economy from decreased exports.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On the other hand, if as a result of Tokyo's continued aggressive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  the yen doesn't strengthen too much and the economy isn't hurt, the BOJ might not need to ease policy more.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The BOJ now floods the market with cash as part of its super-easy policy, and aims to have between Y27 trillion to Y32 trillion in its account for banks.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, BOJ officials are hesitant to ease policy further partly because they feel that the economy is recovering and deflation is at least not worsening now.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Hiroshi Inoue, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-9203; hiroshi.inoue@dowjones.com   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Hiroshi Inoue, a correspondent for Dow Jones Newswires in Tokyo, has covered the Bank of Japan for a dozen years and also looks at credit markets.)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Hugh Lawson  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1042 4131  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031229dzct00048\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
