\par\par \b \uc2 Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 : Dollar Drifts Lower Against Euro And Yen  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Lisa Twaronite  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 714 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Dezember 2003\par 06:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The dollar drifted lower against the yen and the euro Monday in the waning days of 2003, as market participants pondered whether Japanese or European financial authorities would act to stem \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar couldn't maintain its early Y107.40 high hit around 2200 GMT, as U.S. funds and a few Japanese exporters steadily sold the greenback for yen, pushing the pair just under 107 by mid-afternoon.  \par \par \uc2 But speculators itching to test the dollar/yen downside during the long Japanese new year holiday period should remember the 1999-2000 new year holiday, warned Toshihiko Masaki, Tokyo branch treasurer at China Construction Bank.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  kicked off 2000 with \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the yen's rise, and pushed the dollar off of lows below Y102.  \par \par \uc2 "While the Japanese are out" from Wednesday this week, he said, "overseas markets might set the mood as more dollar-bearish, but I'm sure the MOF will be watching the markets even during the \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  holiday and will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  very aggressively if needed."  \par \par \uc2 But one important difference between that period and now is that the euro was then wallowing at historical lows against the dollar, leading up to its break below parity for the first time in the initial weeks of 2000. The yen has more upward crosstrading momentum now, with the euro probing record highs against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The euro broke above $1.2450 resistance in the afternoon Monday, and some traders said the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was likely to retest its record high of $1.2470 within the global session and aim for $1.2500.  \par \par \uc2 At 0515 GMT, the dollar was quoted at Y106.97, below Y107.20 in New York and Y107.09 in Tokyo late Friday.  \par \par \uc2 The euro was at $1.2460, higher than $1.2434 in New York and $1.2458 in Tokyo. Against the yen, the euro was quoted at Y133.28, compared with Y133.29 in New York and Y133.41 in Tokyo Friday.  \par \par \uc2 Sterling was at $1.7756, compared with $1.7707 in New York and $1.7729 in Tokyo Friday.  \par \par \uc2 With the euro apparently gearing up to test new highs against the dollar, market participants wondered at what point European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policymakers would express concern about the impact of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength. The dollar has fallen about 13% against the euro since September.  \par \par \uc2 A report in Monday's Financial Times said ECB officials are "becoming increasingly uneasy about the euro's rise, fearing it could move substantially higher and undermine the fragile eurozone recovery."  \par \par \uc2 The long-looming risks of global economic imbalances and U.S.' twin budget and current account deficits "are now materializing," the report quoted an unnamed senior official as saying.  \par \par \uc2 "We are clearly not surprised by what is happening... but the decline of the dollar has been relatively sharp," the official added.  \par \par \uc2 Some Asian market participants said the key reasons for the dollar's drop were based in the U.S., not in Europe.  \par \par \uc2 "It's not the euro that's too strong, it's the dollar that's weak. The problem lies with the dollar, and the U.S.' twin deficits," said Ryohei Muramatsu, Commerzbank manager of Group Treasury Asia.  \par \par \uc2 Looking ahead, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  will release December \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  figures On Tuesday at 0900 GMT.  \par \par \uc2 Tokyo traders said the amount could be as high as Y2 trillion-Y3 trillion, as some believe the Bank of Japan sold as much as Y1 trillion to push the yen off its high of Y106.74 hit against the dollar on Dec. 9.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has spent Y17.8 trillion in yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the first eleven months of the year.  \par \par \uc2 -By Lisa Twaronite, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; lisa.twaronite@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by John Gibson and Bennett Richardson  \par \par \uc2 The chart of late Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates that accompanied Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been replaced with an enhanced \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  fixture that is updated hourly.  \par \par \uc2 Subscribers can find this table:  \par \par \uc2 -On Telerate pages 4046 and 1087.  \par \par \uc2 -On Dow Jones Newswires by searching the code N/IFX  \par \par \uc2 -On Reuters by entering FRX or INT  \par \par \uc2 -On Bloomberg by entering NI FRX  \par \par \uc2 1074  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031229dzct0003s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
