\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar drifts lower on geopolitical worries.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Gertrude Chavez  \par \uc2 641 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Dezember 2003\par 15:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates prices, adds comment, changes dateline; previous TOKYO)  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Dec 26 (Reuters) - The dollar edged lower on Friday in holiday-thinned trading as market sentiment remained bearish amid concerns that Al Qaeda may try to launch attacks against the United States.  \par \par \uc2 A lack of momentum in thin post-Christmas trade and wariness of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese authorities prevented the dollar from plunging to new lows against the euro and retesting three-year lows set against the yen earlier this month.  \par \par \uc2 "We still have concerns over acts of terrorism after the cancellation of Air France flights to the United States. That of course remains at the fore," said Andrew Delano, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at IDEAglobal in New York.  \par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, Air France canceled Christmas flights from Paris to Los Angeles after U.S. intelligence found the name of at least one person suspected of having links to extremist groups on the passenger list. U.S. officials relayed "credible, reliable" intelligence reports to France regarding plans for "near-term simultaneous attacks that would rival Sept. 11," a U.S. official said.  \par \par \uc2 But Air France said on Friday it will resume its Paris-Los Angeles flights after French intelligence found nothing to substantiate U.S. suspicions about the one passenger having links to terror groups.  \par \par \uc2 In early New York trading, the euro hovered near record highs of $1.2470 against the dollar, trading at $1.2457, and still within sight of the psychologically important $1.2500 mark.  \par \par \uc2 Against the yen, the dollar was down 0.2 percent to 107.00 yen while traders became wary about possible Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen, given the market's low liquidity in post-Christmas trading.  \par \par \uc2 Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was trading nearly flat at 1.2480 francs . The pound, meanwhile, climbed 0.1 percent to $1.7737 . U.K. markets are shut until Monday.  \par \par \uc2 Virtually all traders in London and New York were off for Christmas on Thursday and many in New York were likely to take Friday off as well, keeping global volume subdued.  \par \par \uc2 "For some of our customers, it's the last day of business for the year and they're clearing up any transactions in the backlog," said a trader at a major Japanese bank.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  WORRIES  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the market appeared hesitant to push the dollar below 107 yen for fear it could prompt Japanese authorities, who have been worried that a stronger yen could stall \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's export-led economic recovery, to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and send the dollar sharply back up.  \par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki, repeating his warning against the yen's recent gains against the dollar, told a news conference that Japan would act on exchange rates if the market strayed from economic fundamentals.  \par \par \uc2 The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  also said the Bank of Japan has agreed to a government request for short-term funding to soothe worries it may soon run out of funds to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. The BoJ policy board agreed to issue three-month repo pacts for up to 10 trillion yen, but noted that this was a temporary, precautionary measure.  \par \par \uc2 LITTLE REACTION TO FED'S MCTEER  \par \par \uc2 The dollar reacted little to statements by Federal Reserve Bank of Dallas President Robert McTeer that U.S. inflation was still muted and that only when it becomes a clear danger would the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  considering tightening its monetary policy.  \par \par \uc2 But most analysts were convinced that the dollar is due for a near-term rally fairly soon, given the extent of its losses in the past few sessions. "The market is heavily extended in short dollar positions, so the dollar is due for a pullback. But I'm not sure what that catalyst would be," said IDEAglobal's Delano. (Additional reporting by Shinichi Kishima in Tokyo).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031226dzcq0049u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
