\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar brushes against record lows vs euro.   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Shinichi Kishima   \par \uc2 651 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Dezember 2003\par 07:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Dec 26 (Reuters) - The dollar was softer on Friday, brushing against recent record lows against the euro and probing two-week lows against the yen on persistent worries about geopolitical risks and a bulging U.S. current account deficit.   \par \par \uc2 Still, a lack of momentum in thin post-Christmas trade and wariness of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese authorities prevented the dollar from plunging to new lows against the euro and retesting three-year lows set against the yen earlier this month.   \par \par \uc2 "Basically, you can see that there is no change to the dollar's downtrend," said Junya Tanase, global markets officer at JP Morgan Chase in Tokyo.   \par \par \uc2 "But the market is very thin today and the down move, therefore, wasn't all that aggressive." Virtually all traders in London and New York were off for Christmas on Thursday and many of them were likely to take Friday off as well, keeping global transaction volume subdued.   \par \par \uc2 "For some of our customers, it's the last day of business for the year and they're clearing up any transactions in the backlog. But these are all very small lots and nothing to write home about," said a trader at a major Japanese bank.   \par \par \uc2 At 0540 GMT, the euro hovered around $1.2460 - within sight of record highs set two days earlier at $1.2470. It briefly rose to $1.2469 on electronic dealing systems before running out of steam.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar also slipped toward 107.00 yen down a quarter yen on the day and precariously close to three-year lows around 106.75 yen set in mid-December.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  JITTERS   \par \par \uc2 Traders said the market appeared hesitant to push the dollar below 107 yen in the current thin market for fear that it could prompt the Japanese authorities, who have been worried that a stronger yen could stall \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's export-led economic recovery, to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and send the dollar sharply back up.   \par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki, repeating his warning against the yen's recent rises against the dollar, told a news conference that Japan would act on exchange rates if the market strayed from economic fundamentals.   \par \par \uc2 Japanese government sources said the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF), in a bid to quell speculation that it could soon run out of funds to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, had asked the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy foreign bonds from it under a repurchase agreement to provide short-term funds in yen.   \par \par \uc2 The BOJ policy board met on Friday to discuss the issue and agreed to three-month repo pacts, for up to a total of 10 trillion yen ($93.2 billion), the sources said.   \par \par \uc2 The move comes within a week of the ministry's decision to raise the borrowing ceiling for its \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  account to 140 trillion yen in the draft budget for fiscal 2004/05.   \par \par \uc2 Still, traders said the dollar remained dogged by worries about geopolitical uncertainty, which only increased over the Christmas holidays.   \par \par \uc2 Fresh mortar attacks were reported in Baghdad near the headquarters of the U.S.-led occupation authorities in Iraq and a second attempt in two weeks to assassinate Pakistani President Pervez Musharraf by what he called Islamic extremists.   \par \par \uc2 A Palestinian suicide bombing that killed four people in Tel Aviv and a helicopter strike which killed a top Islamic militant and four other Palestinians also dealt a double blow to hopes of reviving a Middle East peace plan.   \par \par \uc2 In addition, Japanese data generally supported the view that the economy was on a gradual recovery thanks to robust exports.   \par \par \uc2 Industrial output was up 0.8 percent in November from a month earlier, the third straight month of gains.   \par \par \uc2 "The figure came in a bit weaker than expected, but capital goods such as steel are fairly strong, which suggests exports are likely to remain firm," said Takahide Kiuchi, an economist at Nomura Research Institute.    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031226dzcq001us\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
