\par\par \b \uc2 Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 : Dlr Edges Down Vs Yen In Thin Christmas Trade     \b0\par\par\uc2 By Leika Kihara     \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES     \par \uc2 831 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Dezember 2003\par 06:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.     \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The dollar edged slightly lower against the yen after hovering in a very tight range in Asia Thursday, with a few Japanese exporters placing offers in thin Christmas trading.     \par \par \uc2 Trading activity was muted as most non-Japanese players were away for Christmas and Japanese bank dealers were hesitant to go short on the dollar, bearing in mind that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent any sharp rise in the yen.     \par \par \uc2 The euro was also rangebound against the dollar after a brief jump to an intraday high of 1.2461, just below its all-time high of 1.2470 marked in overnight New York trade.     \par \par \uc2 Fears of MOF action kept many market players from trading actively, as the ministry has a record of stepping into the market on Christmas Day.     \par \par \uc2 Most traders are focusing on whether the MOF will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the dollar slips below key Y107 support amid thin year-end trading.     \par \par \uc2 "If the MOF doesn't act to defend that level, the dollar could easily slip to around Y105" toward the end of the year, said Junichi Kumagai, senior economist at NLI Research Institute. "The chance of this happening is not that remote with market liquidity so thin."     \par \par \uc2 Amid a lack of fresh news to trade on in Asia, players had no choice but to keep focusing on overnight dollar-bearish news, such as the release of weaker-than-expected U.S. economic data.     \par \par \uc2 U.S. durable goods orders fell 3.1% in November from the previous month, surprising economists who had forecast an increase of 0.8%. New home sales in the month also fell 2.4%, much weaker than economists' predictions of a 1.2% increase.     \par \par \uc2 Traders fear that such weak readings could put further downward pressure on the dollar, which has failed to stage any major rebound even after a slew of recent positive economic data.     \par \par \uc2 At 0515 GMT, the dollar was quoted at Y107.20, below Y107.30 late Wednesday in New York. The dollar was at Y107.40 late Wednesday in Tokyo.     \par \par \uc2 The euro was at $1.2456, steady from $1.2455 late Wednesday in New York. The euro was at $1.2408 in Tokyo Wednesday.     \par \par \uc2 Against the yen, the euro was quoted at Y133.51, down from Y133.70 in New York late Wednesday. The euro was at Y133.21 in Tokyo late Wednesday.     \par \par \uc2 Sterling was at $1.7746, down from $1.7755 in New York but higher than $1.7641 in Tokyo Wednesday.     \par \par \uc2 Market players' views were mixed on the likely impact on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates of the White House's confirmation of the first suspected case of mad-cow disease in the U.S., which led Japan and several other nations to suspend imports of U.S. beef.     \par \par \uc2 NLI Research Institute's Kumagai expects any negative impact on the dollar to be limited, pointing to the overnight resilience of U.S. stocks after the news.     \par \par \uc2 But Toshiyuki Hara, senior market economist at Mizuho Securities Co., warns that the case may dampen U.S. consumer sentiment and throw cold water on the U.S. economic recovery, thereby putting further downward pressure on the U.S. unit.     \par \par \uc2 "We need more time to see how the case affects personal consumption, but it's certainly not good news for the U.S. economy," Hara said.     \par \par \uc2 The impact of the mad-cow disease case may linger on particularly as the market is already dealing with a slew of dollar-bearish factors, such as geopolitical concerns in Iraq and wariness over the huge U.S. current account deficit.     \par \par \uc2 Mizuho Securities expects the dollar to remain under pressure at least until the first half of next year as market players continue to focus on the widening U.S. current account deficit.     \par \par \uc2 "Dollar buybacks may emerge later in the year if the Bush administration begins to stress its preference for a strong dollar to avoid any turmoil in U.S. capital markets," Mizuho Securities said in a report. The brokerage house expects the dollar to move in a range of Y105-Y120 for 2004.     \par \par \uc2 As for the euro, many market players expect it to test further highs when European players return from their Christmas holidays next week.     \par \par \uc2 "The euro has already risen to excessively high levels," NLI Research Institute's Kumagai said. "But for the time being, we can't find anything that could stem a further euro rise."     \par \par \uc2 -By Leika Kihara, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; leika.kihara@dowjones.com     \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Hugh Lawson     \par \par \uc2 The chart of late Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates that accompanied Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been replaced with an enhanced \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  fixture that is updated hourly.     \par \par \uc2 Subscribers can find this table:     \par \par \uc2 -On Telerate pages 4046 and 1087.     \par \par \uc2 -On Dow Jones Newswires by searching the code N/IFX     \par \par \uc2 -On Reuters by entering FRX or INT     \par \par \uc2 -On Bloomberg by entering NI FRX     \par \par \uc2 1074     \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031225dzcp0002k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
