\par\par \b \uc2 Treasurys Close Lower in Thin Trading  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Michael S. Derby  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 663 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Dezember 2003\par 22:25\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK -- Warnings on terrorist attacks in the U.S. failed to drive anything more than a short-lived safe-harbor bid, as the market closed lower Monday.  \par \par \uc2 The slide largely reflected position adjustments by some traders and investors as the Christmas holiday and end of year approaches, market participants said.  \par \par \uc2 And even with the early gains and then subsequent losses, yields stayed within their current ranges, suggesting that technical factors were playing an important role in the trading action.  \par \par \uc2 There was little fundamental news to drive trading. Compounding the malaise, liquidity was on the light side, as many on Wall Street turned their attention to the holiday, which will see curtailed trading hours on Wednesday and no trading on Thursday, Christmas Day.  \par \par \uc2 The quiet end to trading reversed over night gains, done after the U.S. government increased its terrorism threat assessment from yellow to orange, which indicates a high risk of terrorist activity. Treasurys usually benefit in times of worry, for their risk-free investment status.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. Homeland Security Secretary Tom Ridge said Sunday the "near-term" threat is possibly greater than at any point since the attacks of Sept. 11, 2001.  \par \par \uc2 "It's very thin trading and we had a little bit of safe-haven buying," said Sharon Stark, market strategist with Legg Mason in Baltimore. "We took prices up to what we considered to be resistance levels, or the high end of the range, which gave people a reason to sell," but only by so much, she said.  \par \par \uc2 At 3:45 p.m. EST, the 10-year Treasury note stood at 100 21/32, down 8/32 to yield 4.17%. The 30-year Treasury fell 9/32 to 105 24/32, yielding 4.99%.  \par \par \uc2 The five-year price lost 4/32 to a 3.19% yield, while the three-year fell 2/32, yielding 2.32%, and the two-year price was down 1/32 to yield 1.81%.  \par \par \uc2 The week's economic calendar includes a final revision to third-quarter gross domestic product data, November personal income and spending, and the University of Michigan report on consumer confidence for December, each for release Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 It is likely that Michigan will capture most of the market's attention, as it is often viewed as a popular trading opportunity. Economists surveyed by Dow Jones Newswires and CNBC believe the Michigan sentiment index for December will stand at to 91.0, from 89.6 in the preliminary estimate and 93.7 in November.  \par \par \uc2 Market participants are also already talking about the auction Tuesday of $26 billion in new two-year notes. Traders think the sale should go well. "They look reasonable," said Vinnie Verterano, head of government bond trading at Nomura Securities, in New York. "Year end securities usually do OK," he added.  \par \par \uc2 Traders expect Asian central banks, most notably the Bank of Japan, to be big buyers in the auction, which could keep dealers from bidding too aggressively as talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  circulates in the bond market.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier Monday, Zembei Mizoguchi, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's vice minister for international affairs, said the ministry had asked that its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  borrowing limit be lifted to 140 trillion yen in the fiscal year starting April from 79 trillion yen. This translates into roughly $1.3 trillion in total that the Japanese monetary authorities are prepared to use to defend the dollar against yen strengthening.  \par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan, which acts on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , tends to reinvest the dollar proceeds from its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  into the U.S. government-debt market.  \par \par \uc2 This dynamic has caused Treasury-market participants to view a weaker dollar as bond-market positive since it means the Bank of Japan will be forced to buy dollars and, eventually, buy Treasurys.  \par \par \uc2 -By Michael S. Derby; Dow Jones Newswires, 201-938-4192; michael.derby@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 (Agnes Crane contributed to this report.)  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020031222dzcm0000k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
