\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Tanigaki says  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  to review  \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention \highlight\uc2  funds.  \b0\par\par\uc2 322 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Dezember 2003\par 12:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Dec 19 (Reuters) - Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said on Friday that Japan will consider raising its borrowing ceiling for its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds during further budget discussions.  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki, speaking after revealing the outline of the draft budget for fiscal 2004/05 starting in April, said the ceiling would be considered as the government finalises that budget, as well as a supplementary budget for the current fiscal year.  \par \par \uc2 "At the moment we have enough room to operate but we would like to review this (ceiling) to ensure that we can respond flexibly to any situation on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market," Tanigaki told a news conference.  \par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market has become very sensitive to various geopolitical risks and speculative activity is becoming conspicuous. We must be able to deal flexibly with future developments."  \par \par \uc2 Worried that the yen's strength, which took the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to its highest level against the dollar in more than three years earlier this month, would derail Japan's export-driven economic recovery, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in the exchange market this year.  \par \par \uc2 It has conducted a record 17.8 trillion yen ($165.5 billion) of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the first 11 months of the year.  \par \par \uc2 This has raised concerns that its yen-borrowing was nearing its 79 trillion yen ceiling, and ministry officials have said the limit was likely to be raised by 20 trillion yen in the extra budget and by 40 trillion yen in the 2004/05 budget.  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki reiterated that Japan will take decisive, appropriate action against irregular moves in exchange rates, saying \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be stable and reflect economic fundamentals.  \par \par \uc2 The MOF's draft budget is due to be approved by Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi's cabinet on Saturday. The \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  account borrowing limit can be changed by amending the wording in the budget statement. ($1=107.57 yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031219dzcj001v2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
