\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: Dollar In Line Of Wall Street Big Guns' Fire   \b0\par\par\uc2 1,031 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Dezember 2003\par 18:39\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Jamie McGeever \par    A Dow Jones Newswires Column \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--It's the time of year when Wall Street's big guns train their sights on the year ahead and already the dollar looks like the sitting target.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Bank of America, JP Morgan and Morgan Stanley, all in the top 10 global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  players league, this week released 2004 \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  forecasts, while Merrill Lynch published its latest fund manager survey.   \par \par \uc2 If there's a common thread, it's that sentiment toward the dollar is as bearish as it has been for some time. And that shows no sign of lifting until the second half of the year at the earliest.   \par \par \uc2 Although only a net 4% of fund managers surveyed by Merrill Lynch say the dollar is overvalued compared with an average of 30% over the preceding three months, their exposure to the dollar is at its lowest ever since the survey began in 1989.   \par \par \uc2 This bleak outlook for the dollar is reflected in the first quarter forecasts from JP Morgan, Morgan Stanley and Bank of America, the latter an underlying dollar bull which expects the greenback's losing streak to run it its course in the second half.   \par \par \uc2 But even though the dollar has lost 15% of its value this year and nearly 30% in the last two years, none of these three banks expect the dollar to reverse its downward course against the euro in the first three months of the year.   \par \par \uc2 Bank of America sees the euro at $1.24 at the end of March 2004 while JP Morgan expects $1.25. Morgan Stanley has a more bullish euro call of $1.28, a forecast revised sharply upward from $1.16 previously.   \par \par \uc2 At 12:40 p.m. EST Friday, the euro was trading at $1.2387, coming off its all-time high of $1.2440 reached Thursday, the fourth consecutive session it had made all-time highs.   \par \par \uc2 Underpinning the dollar's weakness is the climate of low U.S. interest rates, which currently stand at 1%.   \par \par \uc2 "With U.S. rates remaining relatively unattractive, foreign investors are unlikely to suddenly come back to U.S. assets for yield," wrote JP Morgan analysts in a note explaining their forecasts.   \par \par \uc2 The Federal Reserve has made repeatedly clear that monetary policy will remain loose for a "considerable period" of time, even though economic growth is surging and most economic indicators are surprising on the upside. Most, that is, except labor market and inflation numbers. And as long as there's no substantial pickup in jobs or inflation, interest rates will stay low.   \par \par \uc2 The U.S. needs foreign capital inflows to plug its bulging current account gap, which is running at around $500 billion a year.   \par \par \uc2 "Stronger U.S. growth has not enabled the dollar to enjoy yield advantages over most of the other G10 \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which makes it more difficult to fund current account deficits on a cyclical basis," wrote Bank of America economists in a note explaining their forecasts.   \par \par \uc2 And the booming economy is likely to spur even higher import demand, thus maintaining the pressure on the current account deficit.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Less Consensus On Yen \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Once again, the invisible hand of U.S. authorities will likely guide the dollar lower in the early part of next year, analysts say. Although officials reaffirm support for a strong dollar policy on a frequent basis, the Bush administration in all likelihood actually welcome a steady fall in the dollar's value.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Friday last week U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow said the dollar's decline has so far been "orderly". Indeed, on a trade-weighted basis, "the dollar is as high as at any time in the last 25 years," Snow said. That relaxed stance on the dollar was echoed this week by European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  chief economist Otmar Issing. The euro's exchange rate, particularly on a trade-weighted basis, is relatively close to historical averages, Issing said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A weaker dollar would help redress some of the huge imbalances currently blighting the global economy, not least the U.S. deficits and anemic domestic demand in Europe and Japan. And it would also help cheapen U.S. goods on world markets and give a shot in the arm to the sickly U.S. manufacturing sector, something President George W. Bush will be mindful of going into election year.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 So as long as there's no howl of protest from either side of the Atlantic, traders will likely continue to sell the dollar lower.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The greenback's slide, however, may be more limited against Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and other Asian central banks regularly \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market selling their own \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for dollars to halt their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ' appreciation from derailing export-led recoveries.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Few would bet against the Bank of Japan continuing its aggressive efforts on behalf of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to curb the yen's rise, at least in the first quarter. But just how effective this policy is in the face of mounting market pressure to sell the dollar is open to question.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In a note accompanying their forecasts, economists at Morgan Stanley said the fall in dollar's fall against the yen will lag its fall against the euro in the first part of the year. "That's not to say that downward pressures on dollar/yen will not be great, but we expect that the M0F will continue to conduct massive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to smooth out the correction (lower) and support dollar/yen above the psychologically important threshold of Y100," they wrote.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 JP Morgan sees the dollar falling to Y102.00 by the end of March, Morgan Stanley forecasts Y105.00, while Bank of America actually expects the dollar to edge up slightly to Y109.00. At 12.40 p.m. EST Friday, the dollar was at Y107.85, around one yen up from the three-year low of Y106.75 hit last week.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Jamie McGeever, who reports on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in New York, has previously written about \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets from London and Rio de Janeiro.)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -Jamie McGeever; Dow Jones Newswires; 201 938 2096; jamie.mcgeever@dowjones.com \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020031219dzcj0002q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
