\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar bounces on positive U.S. job claims data.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Manuela Badawy  \par \uc2 702 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Dezember 2003\par 15:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates prices, adds U.S. jobs data, adds comments, changes byline, dateline, previous LONDON)  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Dec 18 (Reuters) - The dollar bounced up from record lows hit earlier against the euro on Thursday as weekly U.S. jobs data were surprisingly stronger than expected, giving a small jolt to help strengthen the battered greenback.  \par \par \uc2 The euro dropped 0.1 percent against the dollar near $1.2390 per euro, according to Reuters data, after hitting record highs to $1.2436 earlier in the session.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. Labor Department said first-time claims for state unemployment aid, a rough guide to the pace of layoffs, fell 22,000 to 353,000 in the week to Dec. 13 from a revised 375,000 the prior week. Economists had seen claims falling to 365,000 from the 378,000 originally reported for the Dec. 6 week.  \par \par \uc2 "The claims data is fairly volatile but it does provide some positive new insight into the recovery. That should be a positive for the stock market. Any positive news for the stock market is positive for the dollar, but a lot of other factors are dragging on the dollar at this point," Carl Riccadonna, U.S. economist at Deutsche Bank in New York.  \par \par \uc2 The euro rose against the dollar earlier on Thursday to its 13th record high of the past 15 trading sessions. So far this year, the euro has gained more than 18 percent against the dollar and about 50 percent since it hit record lows in 2000.  \par \par \uc2 Marcel Kasumovich, head G10 \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Merrill Lynch, said the claims data were "economy-friendly" but added: "I don't think the figure is a sufficient outlier to get people excited about runaway growth. So I would not expect a very significant market impact."  \par \par \uc2 The dollar has been under heavy pressure due to investor concerns about the widening U.S. current account deficit and expectations benchmark U.S. interest rates will remain steady for some time. Those factors dampen demand for the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Against the yen, the dollar was around 107.60 yen, up 0.15 percent, according to Reuters data. Sterling remained steady, holding at around $1.7653, but below Wednesday's 11-year high near $1.7700.  \par \par \uc2 Against the Canadian dollar, the greenback was up 0.46 percent to C$1.3309, as traders took advantage of a quiet Canadian economic session to mull the Bank of Canada's next interest rate decision, due in January.  \par \par \uc2 IN THE EUROZONE  \par \par \uc2 German data showed business confidence rising for the eighth month running in December in the euro zone's largest economy. The Ifo economic research institute said its business climate index rose more than expected to 96.8, its highest level since the start of 2001 and up from 95.7 in November.  \par \par \uc2 However, the euro zone's economic performance was overshadowed by speculation that comments from European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials signaled little readiness to step into the market to stop the euro's rise just yet.  \par \par \uc2 ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet was quoted as saying he was happy both Europe and the United States were aiming to keep their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  strong and stable.  \par \par \uc2 ECB sources were also reported on Wednesday as saying the ECB was unlikely to consider \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  before the euro reaches $1.35.  \par \par \uc2 The ECB said the readiness of the 10 countries aiming to join the euro in few years will be judged individually. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said countries should make any major policy adjustments needed before joining the Exchange Rate Mechanism (ERM-2) which is required prior to joining the euro.  \par \par \uc2 Investors were watching for any signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with speculation intensifying the government is set to announce a higher borrowing limit for funds for its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Government sources have told Reuters Japan would raise the cap on its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  account to 100 trillion yen ($931.2 billion) for the fiscal year ending next March from the current ceiling of 79 trillion yen, giving \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  more scope to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the yen. (Additional reporting by Daniel Bases in New York and Justyna Pawlak in London).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031218dzci003h3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
