\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Options-Lower euro volatility eases  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  jitters.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Natsuko Waki  \par \uc2 622 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Dezember 2003\par 12:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Dec 18 (Reuters) - Falling euro/dollar implied volatility reflects a steady upmove in the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and is helping reinforce a view euro-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb its strength is unlikely near-term, analysts say.  \par \par \uc2 The euro rose to a record high for a fifth consecutive session on Thursday, reaching $1.2436 . But one-month euro/dollar implied volatility, which reflects expectations of how much a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will fluctuate in the future, fell below its 200-day average to around 10 percent.  \par \par \uc2 Last time the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in 2000, when it enlisted its counterparts in Europe, Japan and the United States to lift the euro from record lows. At that time, one-month euro/dollar volatility shot up to 16 percent.  \par \par \uc2 "The euro's move has been orderly. When the ECB \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  last time one-month volatility was at a historically high level," said Benedikt Germanier, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at UBS.  \par \par \uc2 "(Euro zone policymakers) don't want to give a green light to buy the euro. When the market becomes volatile they would start to verbally \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but we don't expect that yet."  \par \par \uc2 The ECB has shown a relaxed stance so far on the euro's 18-percent gain against the dollar this year. ECB governing council member Nout Wellink said a strong euro was in the interest of the euro zone economy and would not hurt recovery.  \par \par \uc2 ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet was quoted as saying he was happy that both Europe and the United States were aiming to keep their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  strong and stable.  \par \par \uc2 In September 2000, central banks in Europe, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the United States \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to drive the euro higher after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit 85 cents, a loss of nearly 30 percent of its value since its January 1999 launch.  \par \par \uc2 The ECB, in conjunction with national euro zone central banks, then \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  three times in November following the euro's fall to a record low of $0.8225.  \par \par \uc2 STEEP CURVE  \par \par \uc2 Euro/dollar risk reversals, a widely used indicator in the options market which gauges investor sentiment towards a specific \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , are falling at the short end.  \par \par \uc2 This means the market still favours the euro's upside but not to the extreme extent that it favoured its downside when it was falling steeply in 2000.  \par \par \uc2 One-month euro/dollar risk reversals stood at 0.4/0.65 favouring euro calls, or the right to buy euros, compared with this year's average of around 0.47 euro calls. One-year risk reversals were at 0.9/1.15 favouring euro calls.  \par \par \uc2 "The premium for euro calls is falling when the euro is shooting up. It's a very bizarre situation, but it's because the euro has enjoyed a sharp, long uptrend. Investors are stuck with long volatility positions so they are selling it," said Niels Christensen, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Societe Generale.  \par \par \uc2 Back in 2000, one-month euro/dollar risk reversals hit 1.2 percent favouring euro puts just before the September \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , compared with that year's average of around zero.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers also say market makers, who had sold barrier options with strikes at key euro/dollar levels on the upside, are selling volatility at the short end to hedge their positions.  \par \par \uc2 "The risk/reversal is not as bad as you might expect at the moment... It's because of the exotic barriers up top in the euro/dollar," a senior options dealer at a European bank said. At the long end however, volatility has held up, steepening the curve. This indicates markets still expect higher volatility in the long-term but that barriers on the top side could slow the move.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031218dzci002sz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
