\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar Sags Despite Data; Euro Hits Fresh Record High  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Tom Barkley  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 847 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Dezember 2003\par 23:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK -- The dollar once more fell to multiyear lows against European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday, with strong U.S. economic data failing yet again to provide much more than a brief respite from the selling pressure.  \par \par \uc2 The euro hit a fresh all-time high against the dollar around $1.2436 after a larger-than-expected rise in Germany's Ifo business-sentiment survey to 96.8 in December, its highest level since November 1994.  \par \par \uc2 However, weekly employment figures out of the U.S. helped the dollar climb back out of its hole for much of the day, offering some hope that the worst may be over for the greenback.  \par \par \uc2 Initial weekly jobless claims unexpectedly fell 22,000 to 353,000 in the week ended Dec. 13, versus expectations of an 8,000 decline. Other U.S. data were supportive of the dollar as well, especially the Federal Reserve Bank of Philadelphia's business-conditions index, which jumped to a 32.1 reading in December from 25.9 the month before.  \par \par \uc2 But the dollar soon succumbed to a familiar pattern of failing to hold any data-inspired gains.  \par \par \uc2 In afternoon trading, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit an 11-year low against the pound around $1.7720 and fell to its weakest level in seven years against the Swiss franc at around 1.2498 francs. By the end of the New York session, the euro had returned to its new high around $1.2436.  \par \par \uc2 "Certainly the data [Thursday] were extremely positive for the U.S. economy," said Peter Frank, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN-Amro Bank in Chicago. The Philadelphia Fed survey was especially encouraging, he said, but it had "absolutely no positive impact on the dollar."  \par \par \uc2 With the market approaching year end, investors will be unlikely to shift positions and herald in a new trend, Mr. Frank said, which means that the dollar will likely continue to suffer for now.  \par \par \uc2 He said a sharp move up through $1.25 against the euro could spark a pullback, since short-dollar positioning is already approaching extreme levels. But if the euro's move higher continues to be gradual, a correction may have to wait until the new year, he added.  \par \par \uc2 Grant Wilson, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at Mellon Bank, agreed that a correction probably isn't in the cards for the dollar for now. However, he attributes the late-day selloff more to a lack of liquidity than anything else, with London desks closing down and investors getting ready for the holidays.  \par \par \uc2 "[Thursday] is nothing more than what we've seeing," Mr. Wilson added. "The euro comes off, and then it gets low enough to get people interested in buying it again."  \par \par \uc2 Late Thursday, the euro was trading around $1.2436, up from $1.2405 late Wednesday in New York. It was also at 133.68 yen, up from 133.25 yen.  \par \par \uc2 Against the Swiss franc, the dollar rose to 1.2495 francs from 1.2544 francs, while the pound rose to $1.7733 from $1.7658.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was higher against the yen at 107.55 yen, up from 107.41 yen late Wednesday in New York.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar continues to find support against the yen, due in part to concerns about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , especially following reports from international financial sources Wednesday that the Japanese plans to nearly double the amount of funding available for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "They've put the fear into the market," said Tim Mazanec, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Investors Bank & Trust in Boston, referring to the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, apparent complacency by euro-zone officials on the strength of the euro has emboldened investors to take aim at $1.25.  \par \par \uc2 ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet, in an interview in Thursday's Wall Street Journal, said he is "rejoicing" that officials in both the U.S. and the eurozone are pursuing "long-term strategies that are aimed at delivering strong and stable \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."  \par \par \uc2 Mr. Mazanec, noting that other officials have also expressed only modest concern about the euro's rise, said "They all seem to confirm that the euro is moving toward or at its long-term average, and hence are satisfied with the current level. So the market can stay long euro and be pretty comfortable with it."  \par \par \uc2 Still, some traders are pointing to the recent weakness in the so-called commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a sign that the dollar could be poised for a broad-based correction.  \par \par \uc2 At this point, however, it is unclear whether the recent weakness in pullback from an 11-year high in the Canadian dollar, or six-year highs in the Australian and New Zealand dollars, is a meaningful trend or simply profit-taking ahead of the year end.  \par \par \uc2 Mr. Wilson said the modest shift out of commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have more to do with end-of-year liquidity than anything else.  \par \par \uc2 -By Tom Barkley, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-4385  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     tom.barkley@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020031218dzci0001b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\