\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  to boost  \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention \highlight\uc2  war chest - sources.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Satomi Noguchi  \par \uc2 412 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Dezember 2003\par 11:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Dec 17 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is to raise the amount of money it keeps ready for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets as it steps up its attempts to halt the yen's export-damaging rise, government sources said on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 The borrowing limit on its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  account will be raised to 100 trillion yen ($929.8 billion) for the fiscal year ending next March from the current ceiling of 79 trillion yen, the sources told Reuters.  \par \par \uc2 The move comes at a time of sustained selling pressure on the dollar and worries that speculators could be tempted to step up their buying of the yen as repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  puts the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  account ceiling in sight.  \par \par \uc2 The government will push through an extra budget for the year to allow the increase in funds.  \par \par \uc2 Japan will earmark an extra 40 trillion yen in funds in its budget for the 2004/05 fiscal year, bringing the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  account limit to 140 trillion yen, the sources said.  \par \par \uc2 The Japanese authorities spent a record 17.8 trillion yen in the first 11 months of this year to curb the rise in the yen, which some see hurting exporters and choking off an export-led economic recovery.  \par \par \uc2 But the yen has remained near a three-year high of 106.74 per dollar hit last week. It was trading at 107.38 per dollar on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 NEAR CAP  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  finances its \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by issuing finance bills (FBs), which are also used for other short-term funding needs.  \par \par \uc2 According to the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF), the outstanding balance of FBs issued stood at 61.3 trillion yen at the end of June.  \par \par \uc2 Having conducted yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of some 2.0 trillion yen in July and around 8.8 trillion yen in the September-November period, the total spent so far is probably now around 72.1 trillion yen - dangerously near the MOF's legal borrowing cap.  \par \par \uc2 Ministry officials have said that if the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  account gets too close to the ceiling before the extra budget process is completed, the ministry can sell foreign securities - namely U.S. Treasury bills - to the Bank of Japan in a repurchase pact and receive payment in yen.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's top financial diplomat, Zembei Mizoguchi, reiterated on Wednesday that the Japanese authorities would take action against big swings in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as needed. ($1=107.55 Yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031217dzch001po\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
