\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: ECB Complacency On Euro Strength Baffles Mkt  \b0\par\par\uc2 871 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Dezember 2003\par 20:06\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Jamie McGeever \par    A Dow Jones Newswires Column \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--As the dollar sinks to yet another record low against the euro Wednesday, it looks increasingly like the only life jacket capable of buoying the greenback will be an official one.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 That means action - either verbal or direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , probably from the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  - to halt the euro's rampant rise and help reverse the dollar's equally rapid descent.  \par \par \uc2 Yet the catalyst for the latest carnage in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets was a report from Market News quoting unnamed sources at the ECB as saying there will be no \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt the euro's appreciation until it reaches $1.35, as long as the move up is orderly.  \par \par \uc2 This is baffling market participants, who struggle to see the benefits the bank would gain from effectively giving traders a signal to push the euro even higher.  \par \par \uc2 "This surely risks triggering a disorderly rise in the euro, as traders are presented with a 'one way bet'," wrote ING Financial markets analysts in a research note. "Even leaving aside the ECB's complacency about the economic damage caused by the euro's appreciation (which is based on its apparent confidence in the offsetting benefits of the acceleration in global economic growth), this story seems to betray a startling complacency about the behavior of the financial markets. "It is hard to believe that the ECB wants an even faster appreciation in the euro, which is likely to be the story's effect."  \par \par \uc2 This apparent complacency appeared to be backed up later Wednesday by ECB Chief Economist Otmar Issing.  \par \par \uc2 "The current level of the exchange rate corresponds to its long-term average," Issing said in an interview with Bloomberg Television which was also reported on the news agency's Web site. "It is essentially back where it started." The euro was introduced at about $1.17 on Jan. 1, 1999.  \par \par \uc2 Issing did say earlier this week that the ECB is capable of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  if it wants to, but qualified this by repeating his view that there's currently no need for concern.  \par \par \uc2 But all things being equal, a strengthening \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  effectively equates to a tightening of monetary policy, as it damps external demand.  \par \par \uc2 The Market News story had helped push the euro up to a new all-time high of around $1.2390 Wednesday, before the collapse of options-related resistance at $1.2400 prompted another leg up to $1.2417.  \par \par \uc2 Traders say that while position-squaring and profit-taking could well spark a correction lower in the euro at any time, there's very little on the horizon other than firm verbal or direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that will cause a significant reversal in the broader trend.  \par \par \uc2 The euro's strength has been mirrored by the huge gains in sterling, which powered its way up to new 11-year highs of $1.7670, and the slide in dollar/Swiss to a new seven-year low of CHF1.2514.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    U.S. Appears Equally Relaxed \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But the other side of this equation, of course, is the broad and deep weakness of the dollar. And just as the strength of the euro is eliciting very little protest from the ECB and European officials, so U.S. officials are not standing in the way of the dollar's decline either.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Last Week, U.S. Treasury Secretary said the dollar's depreciation has been "orderly", and added that on a trade-weighted basis, the dollar is actually as high as at any time in the past 25 years.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A weaker dollar increases the competitiveness of U.S. firms on world markets, giving a much-needed jobs and growth boost to the country's battered manufacturing sector.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Ian Stannard, senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at BNP Paribas in London, points out that as long as officials on both sides of the Atlantic consider the current rise in the euro and fall in the dollar to be orderly, "We won't see any official action."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The ECB has shown itself to be very noninterventionist, just like the old Bundesbank," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Steven Englander, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist for North America at Barclays Capital, agrees that recent history suggests there will be no action just yet. Unlike the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the Fed and ECB rarely \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But if and when it comes, either verbally or directly, "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would tell us something," about Fed and ECB thinking on exchange rates.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That's because when the Group of Seven Communique calling for more "flexibility" in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets - interpreted as a call for a weaker dollar globally but particularly against Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  - was released in September, the euro was trading around $1.1200.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 So when the verbal protests start to get louder and louder, that could be interpreted as a clear sell signal wherever the euro is trading at the time.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In light of the current silence on the euro's strength, however, that may not be for some time.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Jamie McGeever, who reports on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in New York, has previously written about \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets from London and Rio de Janeiro.)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -Jamie McGeever; Dow Jones Newswires; 201 938 2096; jamie.mcgeever@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020031217dzch0002n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
