\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Seeks Y61T More In  \highlight22\uc2 FX Intervention \highlight\uc2  Funds - Sources  \b0\par\par\uc2 366 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Dezember 2003\par 13:37\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's government is preparing to increase by Y61 trillion the amount of money it can spend on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fight against an unwanted yen rise, international financial sources said Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 Tokyo will seek to boost the borrowing limit on its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  account by Y21 trillion this fiscal year ending March, and by another Y40 trillion next fiscal year, sources said.  \par \par \uc2 The first Y21 trillion will be earmarked in an extra budget for the current fiscal year and the remaining Y40 trillion will be part of the main budget for next fiscal year starting April 2004, the sources said. Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  is putting together drafts for both budgets now.  \par \par \uc2 Finance Ministry officials have declined to comment on the figures, but have said that increasing the borrowing limits for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds is a routine procedure, and that the government is always fully prepared to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if they feel it is necessary to stem excessive and unnatural market movements.  \par \par \uc2 They have also said they still have still have plenty of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds, countering speculation Tokyo is running short of ammunition in its campaign to curb the yen's rise.  \par \par \uc2 Still, the furious pace of Japan's campaign this year to keep the yen from strengthening has brought the government dangerously close to its current cumulative borrowing limit of Y79 trillion, sources say.  \par \par \uc2 Official figures show \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the first 11 months of this year totaled Y17.8 trillion, more than double the record of Y7.64 trillion set in 1999. That would bring the cumulative total to about Y72 trillion as of the end of November, sources said.  \par \par \uc2 Thus the government is seeking to increase the legal borrowing limit to Y100 trillion for this fiscal year, and then to Y140 trillion next fiscal year.  \par \par \uc2 Officials of Japan's Finance Ministry, which formulates \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, are worried that a sharp rise in the yen would hurts exporters by making their products less competitive overseas and reducing the yen value of their overseas earnings.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Miyako Takebe, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; miyako.takebe@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031217dzch000kc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
