\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar struggles near record lows vs euro.   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Hideyuki Sano   \par \uc2 560 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Dezember 2003\par 07:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Dec 16 (Reuters) - The dollar remained under pressure on Tuesday, dangling just above record lows against the euro set a day earlier, as euphoria after the arrest of former Iraqi President Saddam Hussein quickly faded.   \par \par \uc2 The capture of Saddam failed to dispel concerns about geopolitical risks, and entrenched worries over how the United States will finance its growing current account deficit continued to haunt the dollar.   \par \par \uc2 "We had the best dollar-buying factor we could think of but the dollar failed to rise," said Kota Kimura, assistant manager at Shinkin \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 "Basically, funds are not going back to the U.S."   \par \par \uc2 At 0253 GMT, the dollar was at $1.2312/17 per euro , a stone's throw from its record low of $1.2325 per euro set on Monday and off slightly on the day.   \par \par \uc2 "There is much more room for the euro to rise, which can be explained by one thing - the dollar's weakness," said Junya Tanase, global markets officer at JP Morgan Chase.   \par \par \uc2 The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at 132.68 yen , up a little from 132.48 in late U.S. trading.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar managed to eke out small gains against the yen on concerns that the Japanese authorities would step in to stop the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's descent.   \par \par \uc2 The greenback was trading at 107.75/80 yen , up about 0.1 percent from late New York levels.   \par \par \uc2 Given the dollar's position against the yen, some traders say that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may already have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 "That the euro has gone past $1.23 while the dollar is in the mid-107 yen range means there's a good chance that something's going on," said a trader at a Japanese bank.   \par \par \uc2 DEFICIT WEIGHS   \par \par \uc2 With the arrest of Saddam having failed to provide much impetus for the dollar, traders were looking back to the old theme of structural problems in the U.S. economy, particularly the current account deficit.   \par \par \uc2 Current account figures for the third quarter are due at 1330 GMT. A Reuters poll of Wall Street analysts and investment banks shows a median forecast for a deficit of $136.10 billion, compared with $138.67 billion in the previous quarter.   \par \par \uc2 "Basically the U.S. needs about $45 billion a month from abroad to make ends meet. But clearly it is not receiving that much," said Shinkin's Kimura.   \par \par \uc2 Indeed, international capital data from the U.S. Treasury show foreign investors bought a net $27.65 billion of U.S. financial securities in October.   \par \par \uc2 Although that was up from $4.19 billion in September, it was way below the $45 billion the country needs to plug its current account deficit.   \par \par \uc2 Tuesday will also see U.S. consumer prices and housing starts for November, both due at 1330 GMT.   \par \par \uc2 But given the gaping U.S. trade deficit, traders said the market would receive the news in the same way it had responded to recent U.S. economic data.   \par \par \uc2 "If the data is good, the market won't react. If it's bad, then we'll see a sell-off (of dollars)," said Toshihiro Azuma, a manager at Sumitomo Trust and Banking.   \par \par \uc2 "At the moment everyone's just looking for reasons to sell the dollar, so even if the figures are good, few are going to react."    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031216dzcg000p1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
