\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar resumes slide as US trade numbers loom.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Christina Fincher  \par \uc2 642 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Dezember 2003\par 14:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Dec 12 (Reuters) - The dollar slid back towards this week's record low against the euro and hit an 11-year low against the pound on Friday as looming U.S. trade data focused attention on the United States' current account deficit.  \par \par \uc2 Worries the world's largest economy will find it increasingly hard to attract funds to plug its deficit have taken a heavy toll on the dollar, which has shed 17 cents against the euro this year, and seven cents in the past month alone.  \par \par \uc2 "The market is focusing on structural factors, so the U.S. trade numbers are important," said Trevor Dinmore, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Bank.  \par \par \uc2 Figures from the U.S. Commerce Department later in the day are expected to show the U.S. trade deficit widened to $41.5 billion in October as an increase in imports, fuelled by a strong U.S. recovery, swamped exports boosted by a weaker dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The euro rose as high as $1.2257, coming within a quarter-cent of peaks scaled on Tuesday, when the euro notched a record high against the dollar for an eighth straight trading session.  \par \par \uc2 "The shallowness of the euro's pullback after its string of record highs shows real money investors still want to buy, even if speculators are over-extended," Dinmore added.  \par \par \uc2 Sterling rose above $1.75 for the first time since October 1992, the month after Britain's ejection from Europe's Exchange Rate Mechanism.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. FOCUS  \par \par \uc2 U.S. trade data for October is due at 1330 GMT together with November producer price data. The University of Michigan's preliminary consumer sentiment index for December is due at 1445 GMT.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar's lurch lower, coming at a time when U.S. stock markets are pushing higher, reflects a growing conviction that a recovering economy will do little to help the United States plug its current account deficit - currently running at five percent of gross domestic product.  \par \par \uc2 Indeed, analysts note that recovering domestic demand should boost U.S. imports, while a combination of ultra-low U.S. interest rates and rising optimism in the global economy is likely to see capital flow out of the United States into higher yielding assets.  \par \par \uc2 "The trade release has reminded the market of the structural weakness of the dollar," said Paul Mackel, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN Amro, who noted the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was already struggling after Thursday's dovish FOMC minutes.  \par \par \uc2 Minutes of October's Federal Open Market Committee meeting showed the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  thought the jobs market may not fully recover until the latter part of 2005 or even later and inflation would remain tame, indicating U.S. rates were likely to remain low for a while.  \par \par \uc2 UPBEAT TANKAN  \par \par \uc2 The dollar also came under pressure against the yen after a closely-watched survey showed big Japanese firms more confident about business conditions than at any time in the past six years.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's quarterly "tankan" poll showed the key diffusion index for large manufacturers had improved to plus 11 in December - its highest since June 1997 - from plus 1 in September, beating economists' forecast of plus 6.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar eased to 107.81 yen, but wariness of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  prevented a slide back to three-year lows set earlier this week.  \par \par \uc2 Traders reckon the Bank of Japan sold one trillion yen on Wednesday, after throwing almost 18 trillion yen at the foreign exchanges in the first 11 months of this year.  \par \par \uc2 Japan is concerned a rising yen could harm the country's export-led recovery and Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki reiterated on Friday that recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves had been too fast.  \par \par \uc2 The Swiss franc showed little reaction after Switzerland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left interest rates unchanged, as expected.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031212dzcc002kk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
