\par\par \b \uc2 CORRECTED - CORRECTED-Tokyo stocks end up on tankan boost, U.S. advance.   \b0\par\par\uc2 676 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Dezember 2003\par 08:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 In TOKYO story headlined "Tokyo stocks end higher on tankan,U.S. stocks rise" please read family name in 3rd and 9th paragraphs as Iso instead of Ito (correcting spelling).   \par \par \uc2 A corrected story follows.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds byline, quote from market and background)   \par \par \uc2 By Edwina Gibbs   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Dec 12 (Reuters) - Tokyo stocks finished nearly one percent higher and were up for a second successive day on Friday, heartened by improved business confidence in the Bank of Japan's "tankan" survey and advances in U.S. stock markets.   \par \par \uc2 Chip-related issues like Nikon Corp helped lead the rise after an upbeat forecast from a Taiwanese chip maker, but a large-scale equity financing plan by Internet heavyweight Softbank Corp helped limit gains.   \par \par \uc2 "Sentiment seems to have gone up a notch in the past two days with a strong tankan coming on the heels of the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's (\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Masaki Iso, chief investment officer at Yasuda Asset Management.   \par \par \uc2 "The Nasdaq's gains overnight also seem to have eased caution over doubts about the strength of earnings at U.S. high-tech firms," he added.   \par \par \uc2 The Nikkei average ended up 0.94 percent at 10,169.66, tacking on to a 1.66 percent rise on Thursday when suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sent the yen lower and fuelled buying of exporters.   \par \par \uc2 But dollar weakness and yen strength was the week's main theme, and the Nikkei was down 1.96 percent from a week earlier.   \par \par \uc2 The broader TOPIX index, which covers some 1,500 shares on the main board, added 0.78 percent to close at 998.70 on Friday.   \par \par \uc2 Occupying centre stage was the Bank of Japan's quarterly tankan survey issued before trade started, which showed that large manufacturers were more confident about business than at any other time in the past six years.   \par \par \uc2 "The economy is recovering and maybe the market doesn't need to be that cautious," said Yasuda Asset's Iso.   \par \par \uc2 But others were less sure.   \par \par \uc2 "It could be that all positive factors out there have already been factored into the market," said Toyomi Kusano, a director at Credit Agricole Indosuez Securities.   \par \par \uc2 "If the market has factored in an economic recovery continuing into next year, there might be no room for it climb further in the first half of 2004."   \par \par \uc2 NIKON STEPS HIGHER   \par \par \uc2 Among chip-related issues gaining after Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing Co (TSMC) said its fourth-quarter sales would beat forecasts and hit a record high, chip equipment maker Nikon rose 7.18 percent to 1,388 yen.   \par \par \uc2 Semiconductors stocks had been pummelled only two sessions ago on disappointing revenues from TSMC and a peer. Tokyo Electron, the world's second-largest maker of chip manufacturing equipment, rose 3.47 percent to 8,040 yen.   \par \par \uc2 Softbank, the most active issue by turnover, sank 13.85 percent or its daily limit of 500 yen to 3,110 yen after it said on Thursday it would raise up to 230 billion yen ($2.13 billion) by issuing new shares and convertible bonds.   \par \par \uc2 The large-scale fund-raising comes after Softbank posted a record net loss of 77.34 billion yen for April-September as an aggressive sales campaign for its asymmetric digital subscriber line (ADSL) Internet connection service dented profitability.   \par \par \uc2 It was the latest in a slew of large-scale equity and equity-linked financing deals including electronics giants NEC Corp, Sony Corp and Web-based shopping mall operator Rakuten Inc   \par \par \uc2 "Corporate financing deals have increased considerably and there is some concern that this could weigh on share prices, although market turnover has run up to fairly high levels," said Kazuyuki Naito, general manager of equities sales and trading at UFJ Tsubasa Securities.   \par \par \uc2 Trading volume spiked due to the settlement of December futures and options on the Nikkei average, with 1.57 billion shares changing hands on the first section, compared with 1.19 billion on Thursday. Gainers outpaced decliners 938 to 464.   \par \par \uc2 ($1=108.00 yen).    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031212dzcc001f0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
