\par\par \b \uc2 Yen caged by BOJ jitters despite upbeat data.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Hideyuki Sano  \par \uc2 649 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Dezember 2003\par 08:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Dec 12 (Reuters) - The yen hardly flinched on Friday as the prospects of Tokyo's dollar buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  put a lid on the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  despite stronger-than-expected figures in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's "tankan" corporate sentiment survey.   \par \par \uc2  The quarterly poll showed that the key diffusion index for large manufacturers improved to plus 11 in December - its highest since June 1997 - from plus 1 in September, beating economists' forecast of plus 6.   \par \par \uc2  "The tankan... came in much stronger than expected. But the yen is not rising because there is speculation in the market that the Bank of Japan may be bidding the dollar," said Yoshihiro Nomura, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  section manager at Trust & Custody Services Bank.   \par \par \uc2  By 0615 GMT, the dollar was at 107.91/99 yen, little changed from the late U.S. level and stuck in a tight range between 107.90 and 108.06 as dealers hesitated to sell the dollar due to wariness about Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2  Traders estimated that the BOJ sold one trillion yen ($9 billion) on Wednesday, one day after the dollar fell to a three-year low of 106.74 - down nearly 10 percent since early September.   \par \par \uc2  "I think they (the government) were concerned about Japanese shares," said Seiya Nakajima, chief economist at Itochu Corp.   \par \par \uc2  "They carried out large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier this week because the Nikkei share average was falling three percent and looked shaky."   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  conducted almost 18 trillion yen of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the first 11 months of this year, fearing the yen's rise would erode Japanese manufacturers' profits from exports. Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said on Friday that recent movements in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market were a little too fast.   \par \par \uc2  FOCUS ON U.S.   \par \par \uc2  The euro was also little changed at $1.2218/23 with many traders waiting for a series of U.S. data to land later in the day including the trade deficit for October, due at 1330 GMT.   \par \par \uc2  Economists in a Reuters survey forecast a median $41.5 billion deficit compared with a $41.27 billion deficit in September.   \par \par \uc2  A rise in the deficit could spark fresh selling of the dollar, given that the snowballing U.S. current account deficit has been among the biggest factors hurting the greenback.   \par \par \uc2  Worries that the United States may have no choice but to weaken the dollar to reduce its current account deficit drove the euro to record highs versus the dollar for eight straight days until Tuesday, when it marked a high of $1.2276.   \par \par \uc2  Since then, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has declined about a half cent but traders said that was a natural correction.   \par \par \uc2  "The euro needed an adjustment," said Toshihiro Azuma, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Sumitomo Trust and Banking. "I don't expect it to fall to $1.20 and I think it will hit a new high of $1.24 by the end of the year."   \par \par \uc2  Also due later on Friday are the November producer price index at 1330 GMT and the University of Michigan's preliminary consumer sentiment index for December at 1445 GMT.   \par \par \uc2  Data unveiled on Thursday was mixed. U.S. weekly jobless claims unexpectedly rose in the week to December 6, while November retail sales climbed 0.9 percent, beating economists' forecasts for a 0.7 percent rise.   \par \par \uc2  The euro, which has benefited from its yield advantage over the U.S. dollar, received another shot in the arm on Thursday when minutes from the October 28 meeting of the Federal Open Market Committee showed the Fed saw inflation under wraps through 2004 and possibly beyond.   \par \par \uc2  The surprise contents rekindled expectations for a prolonged period of low U.S. interest rates.   \par \par \uc2  "The contents (of the minutes) have assured us that the dollar's bearish trend will not change soon," said Yoshiharu Yanagisawa, vice president of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  at State Street Bank in Tokyo.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031212dzcc001ee\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
