\par\par \b \uc2 Asian Stocks End Mostly Higher, Led by Tokyo, Hong Kong  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,001 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Dezember 2003\par 14:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A Wall Street Journal Online News Roundup   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Most Asian-Pacific markets ended higher Thursday, led by rallies in Tokyo and Hong Kong as traders went on blue-chip buying sprees.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Japan's Nikkei Stock Average of 225 issues rose 164.58 points, or 1.7%, to 10075.14. On Wednesday, the index fell 213.72 points, or 2.1%.  \par \par \uc2 Prices in Tokyo were lifted by optimism that the U.S. dollar might stabilize against the yen, a day after Japanese monetary authorities were believed to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market. A weaker yen makes Japanese goods cheaper abroad.  \par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan might have injected as much as one trillion yen ($9.2 billion) when the dollar fluctuated just above mid-107 yen, pushing the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  above 108 yen, major economic newspaper Nihon Keizai reported Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's Chief Cabinet Secretary Yasuo Fukuda said Thursday it is "natural" for monetary authorities to take action in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if the yen strengthens too rapidly. Mr. Fukuda, however, refused to confirm whether \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 Technology and exporter blue-chip stocks attracted investors thanks to the dollar's stability in New York. Technology issues such as Canon and Tokyo Electron, and auto makers Honda Motor, Nissan and Toyota led Thursday's gains.  \par \par \uc2 Wednesday in New York, Wall Street retreated modestly in light trading. The Dow Jones industrial average closed down 1.56 points at 9,921.86. The Nasdaq composite index lost 3.67 points to 1,904.65.  \par \par \uc2 In Hong Kong, shares closed at a 29-month high Thursday, with the focus shifting to blue-chip laggards on the positive outlook for 2004 and concerns over a potential correction in class H shares.  \par \par \uc2 The blue-chip Hang Seng Index ended up 156.20 points, or 1.3%, at 12544.58, its highest close since July 16, 2001.  \par \par \uc2 Index heavyweight HSBC Holdings, one of the outperforming blue-chip stocks in the second half of the year, fell 0.4%.  \par \par \uc2 Hutchison Whampoa ended 5.7% higher. The ports-to-telecom conglomerate is expected to launch its third-generation mobile service in Hong Kong next week, raising hopes that lingering problems of a 3G handset shortage may have finally been solved. Property developer Cheung Kong, which has a 49.9% stake in Hutchison, rose 4.2%.  \par \par \uc2 Wharf Holdings, another blue-chip laggard, advanced 4%.  \par \par \uc2 Taiwan's shares ended higher, buoyed by a rally in financial stocks after newspapers reported the government would allow more mergers and acquisitions in the insurance sector.  \par \par \uc2 According to the Commercial Times, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  will allow the insurance units of financial holding companies to acquire banks, securities firms or other insurers in the open market.  \par \par \uc2 The financial subindex jumped 1.7%, more than any other main board subindex. E Sun Financial Holding rose 4.5%, while peer Jih Sun Financial Holding surged 6.5%.  \par \par \uc2 Among the sector's heavyweights, Cathay Financial ended 2.9% higher, and Fubon Financial Holding rose 2.5%.  \par \par \uc2 China's shares ended little changed, with investors buying bank stocks after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced plans to allow more flexible lending rates for commercial banks.  \par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of China said late afternoon Wednesday that the floating band for lending rates for all commercial banks will be widened to 1.7 times the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 -determined benchmark rate starting January, a further step in reforming the heavily regulated interest-rate regime.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also plans to reduce the interest rate on above-quota reserves to 1.62% from 1.89% from Dec. 21, a measure some hope will stimulate lending as banks look for better returns on their cash.  \par \par \uc2 China Merchants Bank's class A shares jumped 4%, China Minsheng Bank's class A shares rose 1.8%, and Shanghai Pudong Development Bank's class A shares gained 3%.  \par \par \uc2 South Korean shares closed lower on heavy program selling by domestic institutions due to triple-witching, market participants said.  \par \par \uc2 Shares were in positive territory much of the session but yielded to selling pressure in the last hour from triple-witching, when stock index futures, index options and stock options expire at the same time.  \par \par \uc2 Samsung Electronics fell 0.1%; SK Telecom lost 0.5%; Posco slid 2%; and Korea Electric Power dropped 1.4%.  \par \par \uc2 Singapore shares ended higher, helped by firmer regional markets.  \par \par \uc2 Thanks to new listing Asia Environment Holdings, trading volume jumped to 541 million shares from the 346 million shares traded Wednesday. The China-based water treatment company dominated Thursday's trade, with about 142 million shares exchanging hands.  \par \par \uc2 Asia Environment closed at 45 Singapore cents, almost double its 23.5 cents initial public offering price.  \par \par \uc2 Other water stocks such as Hyflux and Sinomem Technology lost their sparkle as investors switched out of the stocks into Asia Environment. Hyflux slipped two cents, or 1.3%, while Sinomem ended flat.  \par \par \uc2 Datacraft Asia ended higher after Chief Executive Bill Padfield said the system integrator is "pretty confident" of breaking even during the year ending Sept. 30, 2004. The stock rose 2.7%.  \par \par \uc2 Australia's share market posted its fourth straight loss, with pressure on key bank stocks and large miners.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said investors continue to clean up portfolios ahead of Christmas, while a thin market exaggerated share price movements.  \par \par \uc2 National Australia lost 11 cents to A$29.90 and Australia & New Zealand Banking eased 13 cents to A$17.10.  \par \par \uc2 Westpac Banking was the only major to gain, adding three cents to A$15.56 despite Managing Director David Morgan warning that the revenue outlook is less buoyant due to the expected downturn in the housing sector.  \par \par \uc2 Thai prices advanced as traders bought into blue-chip stocks, while Indonesian shares rallied as investors snapped up telecoms and mining issues.  \par \par \uc2 Malaysian shares, meanwhile, dropped on a news report that the government will postpone a major rail project. Philippine shares also fell, and New Zealand shares edged down slightly in a lackluster session.  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020031211dzcb0000d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
