\par\par \b \uc2 EUROPEAN MIDDAY BRIEFING FROM DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \b0\par\par\uc2 770 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Dezember 2003\par 12:52\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 AMERICAN MARKETS OUTLOOK: Markets are expected to open higher, with all eyes on retail sales numbers and weekly jobs data due later in the day. If the data fulfills, or exceeds, market expectations, the Dow is likely to resume its push toward 10,000.  \par \par \uc2 City Index is calling the DJIA up 30 points at 9951 and the Nasdaq up 10 at 1914.  \par \par \uc2 EUROPEAN MARKETS: European markets are mostly trading higher, although the FTSE 100 is down 0.3% at 4324.6 after a profit warning from Matalan pulled retailers lower. The German DAX was up 0.6% at 3,842,78 after briefly breaching resistance at 3845 to reach an intraday high of 3854. In Paris, the CAC was up 0.5% at 3454.24.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar is likely to see limited gains from positive U.S. economic data, says Dresdner Kleinwort Wasserstein. Euro/dollar support is seen at the week's low of $1.215.  \par \par \uc2 Euro-zone government bonds showed little movement following the release of European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  economic projections, which kept the 2004 growth forecast at 1.6% Euro movements are seen as more of an influence on prices, traders said. The CBI's report that manufacturing appears to be staging a recovery is keeping pressure on gilts.  \par \par \uc2 ===========================  \par \par \uc2 TOP STORIES:  \par \par \uc2 ECB DOWNGRADES EURO-ZONE 2003 GDP GROWTH: The euro zone's gross domestic product will grow by 0.4% in 2003, the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in its December monthly bulletin. This is lower than the 0.7% growth the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  forecast six months ago.  \par \par \uc2 JAPAN OFFICIALS SUPPORT ACTION TO REIN IN YEN: Japan's top government spokesman said it is "natural" for monetary authorities to take action in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if the yen strengthens too rapidly.  \par \par \uc2 GERMAN NOVEMBER CPI FALLS: Consumer prices in Germany were down on the month in November, mainly due to lower prices for food, oil products, and leisure and hotel services, data released by the Federal Statistics Office showed.  \par \par \uc2 ============================  \par \par \uc2 INSIGHT & ANALYSIS FROM DOW JONES NEWSWIRES:  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  FOCUS: With the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  standing by to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on its behalf, the dollar has a better chance of resisting selling pressure Thursday. (By Nicholas Hastings)  \par \par \uc2 =THE SKEPTIC: It really shouldn't surprise that Matalan's (MTN.LN) value was rocked by a surprise profit warning Thursday. The U.K. discount retailer is, after all, heavily exposed to a sector which naturally experiences hiccups every time there's a change in the competitive environment or consumer whims. (By Brian Truscott)  \par \par \uc2 =CHARTING EUROPE: The euro is now testing key support which is expected to hold ahead of a move up to fresh all-time highs around $1.2315. (By Axel Rudolph)  \par \par \uc2 ===========================  \par \par \uc2 STILL TO COME  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    ET/GMT \par *0830/1330  U.S. Jobless Claims for Dec. 6 Week (seen -5K) \par *0830/1330  U.S. Nov. Retail and Food Sales (seen +0.6%) \par *0830/1330  U.S. Nov Retail and Food Sales, Ex-autos (seen +0.3%) \par *0830/1330  U.S. Oct. Business Inventories (seen +0.2%) \par *1200/1700  U.S. Oct. Chicago Fed Midwest Manufacturing Index \par *1230/1730  U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Greenspan           speaks on globalization, Dallas \par *1400/1900  U.S. Nov. Federal Open Market Committee Minutes \par *1630/2130  U.S. Money Supply   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ===========================  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 OTHER NEWS:  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Royal Ahold NV (AHO) said that its shareholders had subscribed to more than 90% of an offering of EUR3 billion in new shares, quelling some fears in the market that the rights issue would receive a lukewarm response. (News Snap by Neil Moorhouse)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Troubled U.K. holiday operator MyTravel Group PLC (MT.LN) said that full-year net loss widened to GBP913.2 million, blaming a deteriorating trading environment in the U.K. and structural problems that made it unable to respond to poor sales. (News Snap by Susanna Howard)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 France's gross domestic product is likely to grow by 0.4% in the first quarter of 2004 following an estimated 0.5% rise in the last three months of this year, the Bank of France said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As speculation about possible mergers among Germany's major banks increases, Commerzbank AG's Chief Financial Officer Eric Strutz is convinced that "something is bound to happen in the next one and a half years." (WSJ)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Car rental company Avis Europe PLC (AVE.LN) warned 2003 profits will be driven down by almost EUR100 million in exceptional costs and, with no upturn in demand expected, profits will be flat in 2004. (News Snap by Emma Blake)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Andrea Chipman, Dow Jones Newswires; 44-207-842-9259; andrea.chipman@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 70737  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031211dzcb000ix\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
