\par\par \b \uc2 EUROPEAN MORNING BRIEFING: Markets Seek Direction  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,407 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Dezember 2003\par 07:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US SUMMARY: Investors Take A Step Back \par  \par DJIA          9921.86    loss 1.56         0.0% \par NASDAQ        1904.65    loss 3.67      dn 0.2% \par S&P 500       1059.85    loss 1.13      dn 0.1% \par Dow Future    9945.00    gain 14.00     up 0.1% \par NASDAQ Future 1392.00    gain 2.00      up 0.1% \par S&P Future    1061.00    gain 0.25         0.0% \par 10-Yr US Treasury: 4.32% down 0.03 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Wall Street retreated modestly in light trading Wednesday, as the market searched for direction following the Dow Jones industrials' brief foray above 10,000 a day earlier. Prices of Treasurys rose slightly.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 STOCKS: Investors appeared unwilling to push the market significantly higher in the absence of news Wednesday, and following the milestone touched during the previous session.  \par \par \uc2 "There are two things at play in this market," said Kevin Caron, market strategist with Ryan, Beck & Co., LLC. "You've got fundamental conditions in the economy improving, but on the other hand you have the very human response of money managers, who are facing the year-end and trying to position their portfolios after two or three down-years."  \par \par \uc2 BONDS: Treasurys ended higher Wednesday in a listless session, as the market bounced back a bit from Tuesday's selloff induced by the Federal Reserve's slight tilt toward concern over inflation.  \par \par \uc2 The market has "gotten through the Fed issue," and that's induced some stability in prices, said Sharon Stark, strategist with Legg Mason in Baltimore.  \par \par \uc2 "Fixed income has had a good year, and nobody wants to mess this up," so the market is sliding into its normal holiday period calm, she explained.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ASIAN SUMMARY: Tokyo Shares Up As Yen Weakens \par  \par USD-Yen             108.28       loss  0.12    dn 0.1% \par AUD-USD             0.7366       loss  0.0024  dn 0.3% \par Nikkei 225          10058.92     gain  151.39  up 1.5% \par Hang Seng           12565.35     gain  166.97  up 1.3% \par JGB Yield           1.3150% up 0.0050 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Tokyo stocks edged up Thursday following the U.S. dollar's rise overnight against the Japanese yen.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On the stock market, technology and exporter blue chips attracted investors due to the dollar's stability. Technology issues such as Canon and Tokyo Electron, and automakers Honda Motor, Nissan and Toyota all rose.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar rose against major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Wednesday in New York, aided by suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may have injected as much as 1 trillion yen ($9.2 billion) into the market, pushing the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  above 108 yen, Japan's Nihon Keizai reported Thursday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Elsewhere, the outlook for East Asia's economies has improved considerably over the past five months, thanks to improving global growth, stronger domestic demand and China's emergence as a major player in intra-regional trade, an Asian Development Bank think tank reported Thursday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN OUTLOOK: Quiet Markets Ahead Of U.S. Figures \par  \par Euro-USD            1.2186      loss  0.0032  dn 0.3% \par Stlg-USD            1.7400      loss  0.0029  dn 0.2% \par USD-Franc           1.2700      gain  0.0032  up 0.3% \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ZURICH (Dow Jones)--European shares, government bonds and the euro are expected to open a quiet session little changed.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 STOCKS: European shares may open steady to slightly higher. Although Wall St. ended lower, Europe is likely to take its lead from the modest strength seen toward the New York close.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Spreadbettor IG Index is calling the FTSE to open 10 points up at 4345. It sees the DAX opening 2 points up at 3822 and the CAC 5 up at 3443.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European stocks finished weaker Wednesday, as end-of-year profit-taking continued to grind indexes lower a day after the U.S. Federal Reserve decided to hold key interest rates steady. Thin pre-Christmas newsflow added to the sluggish session.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts continue to debate whether a nine-month winning streak has left European equity valuations looking stretched or whether there is further market momentum left in the run- up to Christmas.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ABN Amro strategists said value plays are likely to come back into favor. "With economies moving out of recovery toward an expansion phase, valuation will be a bigger influence on sector performance," they said.  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  volatility has been another factor weighing on investor sentiment in recent sessions.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HSBC strategists said that for the euro zone, the stronger euro represents tighter monetary conditions. "But provided earnings continue to grow solidly in 2004 - which is our expectation - this needn't mean an end to the equity rally," they said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BONDS: Euro-zone government bonds are likely to drift at the open as the market watches for signs of life in stocks and the euro.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Euro-zone government bond prices finished higher Wednesday on euro gains and statements by Bundesbank President Ernst Welteke, who suggested that interest rates will remain at historically low levels.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Technical trading also played a significant role in pushing prices up, market participants said, as liquidity remained at low levels.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A bounty of economic data this session includes U.S. retail sales and weekly jobless claims.  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : The euro starts the session just under $1.2200, which is now shaping up as an important marker.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Aided by suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from Japanese monetary authorities, the dollar managed to avoid a new low against the euro Wednesday for the first time in nine sessions.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar advanced against its major European rivals - though not before slipping to an 11-year low against sterling.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There were no land mines out there to weaken the dollar," said Lauren Germain, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Bank of America in New York.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Technically, if it (the euro) breaks below $1.22 on a sustained basis and the dollar stays above Y108...that could instill a bit of positive sentiment in the dollar," Germain said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CALENDAR: Thursday, Dec 11: US Retail Sales; Greenspan \par ET/GMT \par  \par 0200/0700 Germany   Final Nov CPI \par 0245/0745 France    3Q Definitive Non-Farm Payrolls \par 0250/0750 France    Nov CPI (Seen +2.3% YY) \par 0400/0900 E.U.      ECB December Monthly Bulletin \par 0500/1000 U.K.      Industrial Trends Survey (seen -2.0) \par 0830/1330 U.S.      Initial Jobless Claims for December 6 \par 0830/1330 U.S.      November Import Prices (seen +0.2%) \par 0830/1330 U.S.      November Retail (seen +0.6%) \par 0830/1330 U.S.      October Business Inventories \par 1000/1500 U.S.      DJ-BTM Business Barometer for November \par 1200/1700 U.S.      Chicago Fed Midwest Manufacturing Index \par 1230/1730 U.S.      Federal Reserve Chairman Greenspan \par 1400/1900 U.S.      Nov FOMC Minutes \par 1630/2130 U.S.      Money Supply \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Dennis Baker; Dow Jones Newswires; 41-1-212-2181; dennis.baker@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Inditex (ITX.MC): 3Q Earnings \par Consensus net profit (DJ, 5 analysts): EUR144M (EUR153M) \par Consensus sales: EUR1.20B (EUR1.10B) \par Note: Gross margin drops on high costs, price cuts in Europe, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  movements, summer heatwave. 9-mo net profit seen +5.8% to EUR290M from EUR274M. Focus on current trading, possible share buyback or dividend increase. \par  \par MyTravel (MT.LN): FY Earnings \par Note: MyTravel Group (MT.LN) seen reporting 03 losses before tax and amortization of more than GBP300 million, Thursday, says an analyst. Exceptionals and write-off costs could come in at just as much. MyTravel reported 1H losses of GBP285M and has warned it will also make a 2H loss. "Whatever is reported, people know it's going to be an extremely long road to recovery and, if it survives, the business model will be very different from the past." \par  \par LogicaCMG (LOG.LN): Trading Update \par Note: LogicaCMG's (LOG.LN) trading statement, due pre-market Thu, will be scrutinized for outlook comments for 04, with cautious optimism expected. Statement hot on the heels of NHS contract success and could drive stock into FTSE-100. Company seen cautious on current trading with UK, telecoms and financial services areas of improvement. Wireless Networks division outlook key swing factor. Aggressive cost cutting could also be a theme. \par  \par  \par OTHER SCHEDULED EVENTS: \par  \par IBEX-35 technical advisory committee meeting \par  \par Standard Chartered (STAN.LN): Trading Update \par  \par Trinity Mirror Group (TNI.LN): Trading Upd