\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar rout stalls in bout of profit-taking.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Daniel Bases  \par \uc2 636 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Dezember 2003\par 18:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Dec 11 (Reuters) - The dollar extended its rebound from recent lows against the euro and yen in thin trade on Thursday, a day after suspected Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  boosted the greenback.  \par \par \uc2 After weeks of persistent dollar selling despite mounting evidence of a strengthening U.S. economy, analysts said investors are buying back dollars in order to lock in profits.  \par \par \uc2 The euro is up more than 16 percent against the greenback year-to-date, while the dollar has lost nearly 10 percent of its value against the yen in the same period.  \par \par \uc2 "This is a round of natural profit-taking since the rally in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the dollar has stalled and momentum type players are taking profits before year-end," said Mike Malpede, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Refco in Chicago.  \par \par \uc2 The euro fell to a session low of $1.2114 before cutting its losses to trade at $1.2152, still down 0.56 percent from Wednesday's New York close, according to Reuters data.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar edged down to 108.24 yen a day after what was thought to have been large-scale yen selling by Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 Finance officials in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  have declined to confirm whether they \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but Chief Cabinet Secretary Yasuo Fukuda, the government's top spokesman, said: "I would like you to understand appropriate steps are being taken."  \par \par \uc2 From January to November, Japan spent almost 18 trillion yen to stop strength in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from choking off an export-led recovery. Traders speculated Wednesday's suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  amounted to one trillion yen ($9 billion), but others said the market cannot know for sure right now.  \par \par \uc2 "The speculative and hedge fund portions of the market are all short of dollars and now they are taking some profits, so I think we've seen a turn for the dollar into the end of the year with a rangebound trading pattern," said Mike King, trader at Commerzbank in New York.  \par \par \uc2 This does not indicate a trend change, rather position-squaring before year-end, he said.  \par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, sterling hit an 11-year high of $1.7477, but has since slipped back to $1.7428 . The Australian dollar recoiled from a six-year high of US$0.7430 on Tuesday to trade at $0.7350 .  \par \par \uc2 The dollar rose more than 1 percent versus the Swiss franc to a high of 1.2818 francs before slipping to 1.2771 francs , still up 0.80 percent on the day.  \par \par \uc2 The greenback hit a one-month high of C$1.33 against the Canadian dollar, a rise of more than 1 percent. However, the Canadian dollar has been rangebound during this period of U.S. dollar weakness and the greenback's move up could be a capitulation by speculative traders, said Refco's Malpede.  \par \par \uc2 MIXED U.S. DATA  \par \par \uc2 Economists said a rise in U.S. weekly jobless claims announced on Thursday was counterbalanced by a rise in November U.S. retail sales. Taken together, they proved modestly positive for the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "Overall, I think it is modestly positive for the dollar, but I think worth pointing out that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not received support from robust data over the past several months. At the margins it is modestly supportive," said Doug Porter, senior economist at BMO Nesbitt Burns.  \par \par \uc2 Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan speaks on Thursday on globalization, and so may avoid talking about monetary policy.  \par \par \uc2 The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised its inflation forecast for 2004 but signaled monetary policy will remain on hold.  \par \par \uc2 It sees inflation between 1.3 percent and 2.3 percent next year, with a mid-point of 1.8 percent, up from a range of 0.7 to 1.9 percent forecast in June and a mid-point of 1.3 percent.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031211dzcb003ii\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
