\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar extends rebound as greenback sell-off wanes.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Daniel Bases  \par \uc2 675 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Dezember 2003\par 16:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates prices, adds U.S. data, comment, changes byline, dateline, previous LONDON)  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Dec 11 (Reuters) - The dollar extended its rebound from recent record lows against the euro in thin trade on Thursday and held steady against the yen a day after suspected Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had boosted the greenback.  \par \par \uc2 A mixed bag of U.S. economic data offered only marginal support for the dollar, with traders and analysts saying the turn upward in the dollar's fortunes had more to do with market positioning than a positive outlook for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "We are seeing some signs of fatigue on the upside for the euro, in particular against the dollar. I think there is some profit taking in large positions," said Robert Sinche, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Citibank in New York.  \par \par \uc2 The euro fell to a session low of $1.2114, off 0.87 percent from Wednesday's New York close according to Reuters data.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar edged up slightly to 108.32 yen a day after what was thought to have been large-scale yen selling by Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 Finance officials in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  have declined to confirm whether they \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  but Chief Cabinet Secretary Yasuo Fukuda, the government's top spokesman, said: "I would like you to understand appropriate steps are being taken."  \par \par \uc2 From January to November, Japan spent almost 18 trillion yen to stop strength in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from choking off an export-led recovery. Traders speculated Wednesday's suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday amounted to one trillion yen ($9 billion), but others said the market cannot know for sure right now.  \par \par \uc2 "The speculative and hedge fund portions of the market are all short of dollars and now they are taking some profits, so I think we've seen a turn for the dollar into the end of the year with a rangebound trading pattern," said Mike King, trader at Commerzbank in New York.  \par \par \uc2 This does not indicate a trend change, rather position squaring before year-end, King said.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar is recovering from its beating at the hands of major rivals. On Wednesday, sterling hit an 11-year high of $1.7477, but has since slipped back to $1.7398. The Australian dollar has recoiled from a six-year high of US$0.7430 touched on Tuesday to trade at $0.7335.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar climbed more than 1 percent against the Swiss franc, in concert with its strength against the euro. The dollar touched a session high 1.2818 Swiss francs before slipping back to 1.2787 francs.  \par \par \uc2 The greenback is at its highest in over a month against the Canadian dollar, up 1.64 percent to C$1.33.  \par \par \uc2 MIXED U.S. DATA  \par \par \uc2 Economists said a rise in U.S. weekly jobless claims announced on Thursday was counterbalanced by a rise in November U.S. retail sales. Taken together they proved modestly positive for the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "Overall I think it is modestly positive for the dollar, but I think worth pointing out that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not received support from robust data over the past several months. At the margins it is modestly supportive," Doug Porter, senior economist at BMO Nesbitt Burns.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. weekly jobless claims rose unexpectedly in the week to Dec. 6 to 378,000 from 365,000 the week before. Economists had expected the Labor Department to report a drop to 360,000.  \par \par \uc2 November retail sales rose 0.9 percent, beating economists' forecasts for a 0.7 percent rise.  \par \par \uc2 Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan is speaking on Thursday on globalization, and so may be able to avoid talking specifically about monetary policy.  \par \par \uc2 The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  increased its inflation forecasts for 2004 but it signaled monetary policy will remain on hold.  \par \par \uc2 It now sees inflation between 1.3 percent and 2.3 percent next year, with a mid-point of 1.8 percent, up from a forecast range of 0.7 to 1.9 percent in June with a 1.3 percent mid-point.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031211dzcb002z1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
