\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar extends rebound, awaits US data.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Burton Frierson  \par \uc2 666 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Dezember 2003\par 13:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Dec 11 (Reuters) - The dollar advanced further off recent record lows against the euro and held steady on the yen ahead of U.S. data later on Thursday, after a rebound a day earlier on suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Japanese authorities.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar's bounce followed what was thought to have been large-scale yen selling by Japan. Traders said the action proved a warning for dollar bears and encouraged them to take profits.  \par \par \uc2 "It is more a squaring of positions than suddenly people seeing anything positive about the dollar," said Niels Christensen, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Societe Generale in Paris.  \par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has a limited and short-lived impact. It has not started an uptrend in dollar/yen."  \par \par \uc2 At 1230 GMT, the dollar stood steady at 108.34 yen , but still near three-year lows set on Tuesday below 107.00.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was more than half a percent firmer on the day at $1.2151 per euro but not far from Tuesday's record low around $1.2276.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. retail sales are due at 1330 GMT, along with weekly jobless claims. Sales are forecast up 0.7 percent on the month for November, compared with a 0.3 percent decline in October.  \par \par \uc2 Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan is due to speak on globalisation later in the day.  \par \par \uc2 "We might have seen the (euro) highs for the year now and we're expecting a good headline for the U.S. retail sales today so we're willing to look for a corrective phase," said Peter Fontaine, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at KBC in Brussels.  \par \par \uc2 "Also because we saw the Bank of Japan stepping quite heavily into the market, everyone will be reluctant to short the U.S. dollar in the short term, and in thin trading conditions."  \par \par \uc2 U.S. FOCUS  \par \par \uc2 But traders were unconvinced the dollar's broad-based downtrend would be reversed by dollar/yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It has been hammered against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the past few weeks, hitting 11-year lows against sterling and six-year lows against the Australian dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Besides the U.S. current account deficit, worries about Washington's handling of post-war Iraq have been cited as among the main reasons for the dollar's weakness.  \par \par \uc2 Other U.S. data includes business inventories for October and the Chicago Fed's midwest manufacturing index, as well as minutes of the Federal Reserve's October monetary policy meeting.  \par \par \uc2 "The U.S. data today will provide a key test of whether sentiment towards the dollar has changed. The dollar had some perverse reactions to strong data when it was sliding so it will be interesting to see whether this is still the case," said Ryan Shea, senior international economist at Bank One.  \par \par \uc2 The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised its forecast of euro zone inflation for 2004 on Thursday but signalled monetary policy was on hold.  \par \par \uc2 Inflation was now seen between 1.3 percent and 2.3 percent on average next year, which gives a mid-point of 1.8 percent. That is up from a range of 0.7 to 1.9 percent forecast in June, or a mid-point of 1.3 percent.  \par \par \uc2 BIG BUT NOT ENOUGH?  \par \par \uc2 Although Japanese monetary authorities have so far declined to confirm whether they \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Chief Cabinet Secretary Yasuo Fukuda - the government's top spokesman - said: "I would like you to understand appropriate steps are being taken."  \par \par \uc2 Some traders estimated the amount of suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be around one trillion yen ($9 billion), although others said the market had no way of knowing for now.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  conducted almost 18 trillion yen of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the first 11 months of this year. December figures will be released at the end of the month.  \par \par \uc2 The next test for dollar/yen will be the Bank of Japan's closely watched "tankan" business survey due on Friday (2350 GMT Thursday). It is likely to show continued improvement in Japan's economy.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031211dzcb002d0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
