\par\par \b \uc2 REPEAT:Dow Jones Asia-Pacific Top Stories Sked Dec 10  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,782 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Dezember 2003\par 10:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Following are the top Asia-Pacific stories featured on Dow Jones Newswires as of 0900 GMT, along with items expected later in the day. Please direct any questions or comments to asianewseds@dowjones.com or by phone on 65-6415-4020.  \par \par \uc2 MACRO/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /FIXED INCOME  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: Robust domestic demand pushed China's forecast-beating industrial output to a nine-month high in November and would likely underpin moderate but sustained growth into 2004, economists said Wednesday. ("=DATA VIEW: China Nov Indus Output Soars On Robust Demand" ran at 0808 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MANILA: A coalition of Philippine opposition parties Wednesday nominated popular movie actor Fernando Poe Jr. as its standard bearer in next year's presidential elections. Former senator Juan Ponce Enrile, who heads the coalition's nomination committee, said the 64-year old Poe was chosen for his "capacity to reunify our shattered and fragmented society." Poe has yet to decide whether or not he will accept the nomination but said he will make up his mind "soon." ("Philippine Opposition/Poe -2: To "Reunify" Society" ran at 0327 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =OUTLOOK 04/ASIA'S ECONOMIES: The outlook for Asia's economies in 2004 is brighter than it has been in years as the region shakes off the effects of SARS and looks ahead to a continued recovery in both exports and domestic spending. China is expected to continue playing a leading role as a hub for trade within Asia, and Japan is finally showing signs of reasserting itself as a regional force after a decade-long economic slump. ("=OUTLOOK 04: Asia Poised For Strongest Growth In 4 Years" ran at 0621 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan's current account surplus expanded for the fourth straight month in October, rising 38.9% from a year earlier, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Wednesday. ("Japan Oct Current Acct -2: Fourth Straight On-Year Rise" ran at 0000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Foreign investors turned net buyers of Japanese bonds in October after net selling for three months on a settlement basis, while continuing to buy Japanese stocks with increasing signs of an economic recovery in Japan, according to data released by the Finance Ministry Wednesday. ("Japan Oct Portfolio Invest Fund Outflow Totals Y464.5 Bln" ran at 0154 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Australians maintained their strong appetite for home loans in October, supporting the Reserve Bank's decision to recommence its interest rate tightening cycle the following month. ("=WRAP:Australia Housing Fin Resilient Ahead Of Rate Hikes" ran at 0121 GMT; note "CORRECTION: WRAP/Australia Housing Fin -2: Rate Rises" ran at 0418 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =AUSTRALIA WATCH: The next move for interest rates in Australia is still likely up, but when they will be hiked has become less certain. ("=AUSTRALIA WATCH: Timing Of Next Rate Move Less Certain" ran at 0308 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: The New Zealand dollar is showing all the hallmarks of reaching its post-float peaks just above US$0.70 within the next six months - but strength at those levels will put a heavy strain on the economy and thus, will likely be brief. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Asia: Kiwi Reaching For Post-Float Highs" ran at 0616 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA: Indonesia expects the International Monetary Fund to approve Dec. 19 a final $400 million tranche of loans before the country pulls out of the program at the end of the year, said Finance Minister Boediono. ("IMF To Vote On Last Indonesia Loan Tranche Dec 19-Fin Min" ran at 0840 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: China's banking regulator said it is formalizing an agreement to exchange information with its U.S. counterpart, almost two years after Beijing and Washington cooperated to investigate a major financial scandal involving Bank of China's New York \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ("US, China Bank Regulators Plan Information Sharing" ran at 0141 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA: Inflation in Indonesia is likely to fall to 4.5% this year from over 10% last year thanks to ample food supply and the rupiah's appreciation against the dollar, Bank Indonesia said Wednesday. ("Bank Indonesia/Inflation -2: Benchmark Rate Cut This Year" ran at 0134 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: New Zealand retail sales surged in October from September, driven by a very large increase in food sales, according to official figures released Wednesday. ("DATA SNAP: Food Feeds Big Rise In NZ's Oct Retail Sales" ran at 2149 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIA DEBT: Talk of a sovereign bond issue, or indeed two, from Malaysia has been teasing the international debt markets for some time. The speculation may be just wishful thinking right now, but going forward the prospect of cheap funds might be too tempting for the Southeast Asian nation to pass up. ("=ASIA DEBT:Malaysia Bond Talk Persists Amid Strong Demand" ran at 0557 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 KUALA LUMPUR: Telekom Malaysia, the country's largest telecommunications company, late Tuesday set the indicative coupons on its 10- and 15-year redeemable bonds. The 10-year bonds, which will be issued as a single tranche of 2 billion ringgit, will carry a coupon of 6.20%. The 15-year up to MYR2 billion bonds will be issued with a coupon of 5.25% and will be split into two tranches. ("Telekom Malaysia 10-Yr Bond Indicative 6.20%, 5-Yr 5.25%" ran at 0230 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI: The Indian government's recent curbs on corporate borrowing overseas are likely to deter a new crop of foreign investors who have been showing an interest in India's syndicated foreign loan market, an investment banker at Credit Lyonnais said. ("=INTERVIEW:India's Foreign Loan Curbs May Deter Investors" ran at 0810 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  MARKET ROUNDUP: The dollar rose against the yen in Asia Wednesday on suspected yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese authorities and short-covering by U.S. and European funds and Japanese banks, traders said. ("Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 : Dlr/Yen Rises On Suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 " ran at 0549 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 EQUITIES  \par \par \uc2 =ASIAN IPO FOCUS: Strong demand for Chinese auto maker Great Wall Automobile Holding's initial public offering, as H shares reach a peak last seen six years ago, suggests a happy return of IPO mania in Hong Kong.("=Asian IPO Focus: HK Investors Pile Into Great Wall Auto" ran at 0743 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =OUTLOOK 04/JAPAN'S BANKS: Signs that Japan's biggest banks are regaining their health might relieve investors, but the November bailout of a regional bank is a reminder that Tokyo still has its work cut out to cure the sector's ills. How Japanese policy-makers take care of the country's troubled regional banking sector, after the nationalization of Ashikaga Bank last month, will be a key theme to watch next year for those interested in the bank industry of the world's second-largest economy. ("=OUTLOOK 04: Japan Bank Comeback May Be Up To Local Banks" ran at 0131 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =OUTLOOK 04/JAPAN'S ELECTRONICS MAKERS: Japanese electronics makers got the jump on their global rivals by dazzling consumers with the latest in digital cameras and mobile phones, and they look set to leverage their expertise in 2004 as the market for digital products explodes. (=OUTLOOK O4/JAPAN'S ELECTRONICS MAKERS will run by 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MELBOURNE: Thermal coal producer Coal & Allied Industries, a unit of global miner Rio Tinto, said Wednesday it faces a calendar 2003 loss due to a resurgent Australian dollar. Analysts said the earnings downgrade reinforces expectations that Australian producers will seek large price increases from utilities for the 2004-05 Japanese fiscal year that begins April 1. ("=WRAP: Rio Tinto Unit Warns A$ Surge Will Wipe Out Profit" ran at 0823 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MELBOURNE: Shares in Australian blood products company CSL Ltd. surged Wednesday as investors bet its acquisition of Aventis SA's Aventis Behring unit will drive a profit and stock price turnaround. ("=WRAP: Thirsty Investors Rush Australia's CSL Issue" ran at 0542 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 KUALA LUMPUR: The impending departure of Kumpulan Guthrie's chief executive and the appointment of Golden Hope Plantations' group CEO as his replacement - announced Tuesday - signals the government wants the two firms to merge soon, analysts said Wednesday. (WRAP will run around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: Taiwan-backed air conditioner maker Zhejiang King Refrigeration Industry said Wednesday it intends to raise 292 million yuan in China's first initial public offering by a foreign-invested enterprise. ("Taiwan-Backed Co Plans China's First IPO By Foreign Firm" ran at 0043 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korea's largest card issuer, LG Card, says it has set the price of new shares to be offered later this month at 5,400 won apiece. The company hopes to raise about KRW199.8 billion from offering 37 million new shares from Dec. 15, part of its plan to boost its capital by KRW1 trillion by first-quarter 2004. ("S Korea's LG Card Sets New Share Price At KRW5,400 Each" ran at 0832 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: Former China Construction Bank President Wang Xuebing has been jailed for 12 years for taking bribes, state media reported Wednesday. ("China Banker Wang -2: Ex-China Construction Bank Chief" ran at 0745 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Singapore's DBS Bank plans to issue covered warrants on shares of Singapore Telecommunications Thursday, according to a preliminary issue document seen by Dow Jones Newswires Wednesday. Each warrant will be priced at 15% of SingTel's share price, with the strike price at 90% of the share price, the document said. ("=EXCLUSIVE: Singapore DBS/SingTel -2: SingTel Up 1.1%" ran at 0836 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Guangzhou-based China Southern Airlines said it carried 2.15 million passengers in November, up 22.6% from a year earlier. ("China Southern Nov Passenger Numbers Up 22.6% On Year" ran at 0202 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Panva Gas said it has entered into a memorandum of understanding with a state-owned asset management company to form a piped-gas joint venture in Benxi city in China's Liaoning Province. The move will bring Panva Gas's business to cover 10 provinces in China. ("HK Panva Gas Signs MOU For Liaoning Gas Joint Venture" ran at 0131 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 ENERGY/COMMODITIES  \par \par \uc2 =OUTLOOK 04/CHINA GRAINS: Since joining the World Trade Organization in December 2001, China has disappointed global suppliers by not only importing less grains, but pushing its own corn exports to a record high and emerging a net wheat exporter for the first time in 2002.In 2004, however, Chinese grain imports are likely to increase, although not to levels desired by the world market.Exports may be sharply curtailed, which will also be good news for other major suppliers like the U.S., which are trying to recapture market share in Asia. ("=OUTLOOK 04:China's Grain Imports To Rise As Output Drops" ran at 0730 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 62550  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031210dzca000c2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
