\par\par \b \uc2 Europe,US policymakers seem relaxed on dollar fall.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Stella Dawson, Chief ECB Correspondent  \par \uc2 689 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Dezember 2003\par 18:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 FRANKFURT, Dec 10 (Reuters) - Top policymakers in Europe and the United States have taken a hands-off approach in public to the tumbling dollar, putting no brake in its path in the past week.  \par \par \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governing Council member Ernst Welteke was the latest, expressing calm on Wednesday over the dollar's sharp retreat that has pushed the euro up 5.3 percent in one month to fresh record highs for eight straight days.  \par \par \uc2 "The euro's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate is not far removed from its long-term average and it should not harm competitiveness," Welteke said at the Bundesbank's annual news conference.  \par \par \uc2 Certainly, both sides of the Atlantic can reap benefits from the dollar's decline.  \par \par \uc2 For the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the sharply rising euro, which was trading at $1.2213 late Wednesday, squeezes inflationary pressures out of an economy where prices are proving uncomfortably sticky to downward pressures.  \par \par \uc2 That could allow the ECB, mandated to achieve stable prices, to duck the uncomfortable question of whether to tighten credit early when growth is still weak and politicians are squirming.  \par \par \uc2 Little wonder then that Welteke appeared relaxed about the euro rise, even though German business lobbies have warned a rate of $1.23 to $1.25 would cause pain and even though new trade data showed exports in retreat.  \par \par \uc2 In fact, Welteke said that ECB rates, now at a record low of 2.00 percent, could stay low for some time - clearly an easier decision if a strong euro tightens monetary conditions as the economy recovers.  \par \par \uc2 The European Commission also expressed no distress over the euro on Wednesday. It simply repeated the July formulation from Eurogroup finance ministers that a strong and stable euro was in the interests of the euro zone.  \par \par \uc2 The same message has come from ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet both at his news conference on December 4 after leaving interest rates unchanged and before the European Parliament three days earlier.  \par \par \uc2 "It is very fortunate that each \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is pursuing a policy of strong and stable \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is certainly the case in the UK, certainly the case in the U.S. and certainly the case in Europe," he told parliament.  \par \par \uc2 HANDS OFF  \par \par \uc2 For the United States, a weaker dollar helps its export industries and quietens manufacturers whom President George Bush is courting in his re-election campaign.  \par \par \uc2 Hence analysts doubt that the U.S. Treasury, which must decide on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , would seek to halt the slide unless it turned turbulent and threatened global recovery.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow showed no sign in a Reuters interview on Monday that he might shift course. Instead he hammered home U.S. backing for a strong dollar, even as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  plumbed new depths against the euro and hit three-year lows against the Japanese yen.  \par \par \uc2 "We favour a strong dollar and we feel that \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' exchange value is best set in open, competitive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets," Snow told Reuters.  \par \par \uc2 Another reason that top policymakers may prefer a hands-off approach is that when market momentum is so strongly in one direction - as now with dollar selling - throwing a few billion dollars here and there at a multi-trillion dollar \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market can be a waste of time.  \par \par \uc2 "The possibility for central banks to be effective in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets has decreased in recent years," International Monetary Fund Chief Economist Raghuram Rajan was quoted as saying in a German newspaper interview on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  frequently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to smooth the yen's rise against the dollar, anxious it will stifle export-led recovery, as it was rumoured to have done on Wednesday. But the action this year has failed to halt the dollar juggernaut.  \par \par \uc2 Some political rumblings are beginning though.  \par \par \uc2 "Why do the two most powerful economic regions in the world abandon their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the free fluctuations of markets?" said Italian Deputy Economy Minister Maria Baldassarri in a newspaper interview published on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 The euro at $1.21 to $1.22 penalises European recovery, he said.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031210dzca003ip\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
