\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar jumps higher with suspected BOJ help.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Daniel Bases  \par \uc2 700 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Dezember 2003\par 18:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Dec 10 (Reuters) - The dollar ricocheted up from three-year lows against the yen and a record low against the euro on Wednesday, snapping out of its losing streak on yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said.  \par \par \uc2 "Overall, general dollar strength is a little bit of a knock-on from the Bank of Japan taking the froth off the top ... given session after session with nothing standing in the way of dollar weakness," said David Durrant, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank Julius Baer.  \par \par \uc2 "Then you see a little something from the BOJ and that raises the flag of caution up to the dollar bears," he added.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar surged over 1 percent to a session high around $108.75 yen in a matter of minutes and dealers estimated the BOJ may have bought up to $5 billion, or roughly 540 billion yen, in covert action, even though there was no official confirmation from Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 The Nikkei News service said in a report that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could have amounted to as much as 1 trillion yen.  \par \par \uc2 However, the dollar slipped back to trade near 108.36 yen, a gain of 1.36 percent from Tuesday's New York close. The dollar had touched a three-year low below 107 yen on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 "But the rally is fading and the risk is that they may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again later," said Paul Mackel, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN AMRO in London.  \par \par \uc2 The euro climbed more than 1 percent to a high of 132.69 yen.  \par \par \uc2 The greenback had been edging higher in Asian trade after the U.S. Federal Reserve stuck with its pledge to keep monetary policy easy "for a considerable period" on Tuesday but dropped its concern about falling prices.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar rose slightly against the Swiss franc and Canadian and Australian dollars and rebounded from a fresh 11-year low against sterling of $1.7477. This is cable's best showing since October 1992, a few weeks after Black Wednesday, when Britain left Europe's Exchange Rate Mechanism.  \par \par \uc2 AND ON THE NINTH DAY...  \par \par \uc2 After eight straight sessions of record highs against the dollar, the euro rally paused. It fell to $1.2206, a loss of 0.43 percent on the day after reaching a record $1.2276 on Tuesday, according to Reuters data.  \par \par \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governing Council member Ernst Welteke said the euro is near its long-term average value and this should not harm the competitiveness of European firms.  \par \par \uc2 "We know markets tend to over-shoot, but I do not see this in the current situation," Welteke told reporters.  \par \par \uc2 Welteke also said interest rates in the euro zone - currently 2 percent - are likely to stay at their record lows for a considerable time, mirroring the language of the Fed.  \par \par \uc2 Saudi Arabian Oil Minister Ali al-Naimi told reporters on Wednesday he remained concerned over the low level of the dollar, but said fresh gains in oil prices were justified given the greenback's weakness.  \par \par \uc2 DOWNWARD SPIRAL  \par \par \uc2 Before Wednesday, Japan was thought to have thrown almost 18 trillion yen ($168 billion) at the market this year to try to prevent a strengthening yen from choking off the country's export-led recovery.  \par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki declined comment on whether the authorities had acted but dealers said Tokyo might have wanted to calm foreign exchanges after the dollar's string of lows threatened to turn its fall into a rout.  \par \par \uc2 "I think Tokyo knows that when market conditions look threateningly like they are moving into disorderly conditions that the scope for coming in more aggressively is there," said Derek Halpenny, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Bank of Tokyo Mitsubishi.  \par \par \uc2 An advisory panel to Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi said the economy was still in a mild deflationary state but there were some signs of change.  \par \par \uc2 There has been market speculation \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is close to its allotted ceiling for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds. But a top \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official told Reuters there had been no problem with raising funds via three-month finance bills and he saw no need to use longer maturity debt. (Additional reporting by Charlotte Cooper in London).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031210dzca003hb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
