\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar ricochets up from lows - BOJ help suspected.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Daniel Bases  \par \uc2 655 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Dezember 2003\par 16:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates prices, adds Welteke and analyst comments, changes byline and dateline, previous LONDON)  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Dec 10 (Reuters) - The dollar ricocheted up from three-year lows against the yen and a record low against the euro on Wednesday, snapping out of its losing streak on yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said.  \par \par \uc2 "Overall, general dollar strength is a little bit of a knock-on from the Bank of Japan taking the froth off the top ... given session after session with nothing standing in the way of dollar weakness," said David Durrant, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank Julius Baer.  \par \par \uc2 "Then you see a little something from the BOJ and that raises the flag of caution up to the dollar bears," he added.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar surged over 1 percent to a session high around $108.75 yen in a matter of minutes and dealers estimated the BOJ may have bought up to $5 billion in covert action, even though there was no official confirmation from Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 However, it subsequently lost roughly half of that surge in the course of European and early North American trading hours to 108 yen, still a gain of 1 percent from Tuesday's New York close. The dollar had touched a three-year low below 107 yen on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 "But the rally is fading and the risk is that they may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again later," said Paul Mackel, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN AMRO in London.  \par \par \uc2 The greenback had been edging higher in Asian trade after the U.S. Federal Reserve stuck with its pledge to keep monetary policy easy "for a considerable period" on Tuesday but dropped its concern about falling prices.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar rose slightly against the Swiss franc and Canadian and Australian dollars and rebounded from a fresh 11-year low against sterling of $1.7477. This is cable's best showing since October 1992, a few weeks after Black Wednesday, when Britain left Europe's Exchange Rate Mechanism.  \par \par \uc2 AND ON THE NINTH DAY...  \par \par \uc2 After eight straight sessions of record highs against the dollar, the euro rally paused, slipping slightly on the day to trade at $1.2250. On Tuesday the euro rose to a record high $1.2276, according to Reuters data.  \par \par \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governing Council member Ernst Welteke said the euro is near its long-term average value and this should not harm the competitiveness of European firms.  \par \par \uc2 "We know markets tend to over-shoot, but I do not see this in the current situation," Welteke told reporters.  \par \par \uc2 Welteke also said interest rates in the euro zone - currently 2 percent - are likely to stay at their record lows for a considerable time, mirroring the language of the Fed.  \par \par \uc2 DOWNWARD SPIRAL  \par \par \uc2 Before Wednesday, Japan was thought to have thrown almost 18 trillion yen ($168 billion) at the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets this year in a bid to prevent a strengthening yen from choking off the country's export-led recovery.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki declined comment on whether the authorities had acted but dealers said Tokyo might have wanted to calm foreign exchanges after the dollar's string of lows threatened to turn its fall into a rout.  \par \par \uc2 "I think Tokyo knows that when market conditions look threateningly like they are moving into disorderly conditions that the scope for coming in more aggressively is there," said Derek Halpenny, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Bank of Tokyo Mitsubishi.  \par \par \uc2 An advisory panel to Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi said the economy was still in a mild deflationary state but there were some signs of change.  \par \par \uc2 There has been market speculation \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is close to its allotted ceiling for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds. But a top \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official told Reuters there had been no problem with raising funds via three-month finance bills and he saw no need to use longer maturity debt. (Additional reporting by Charlotte Cooper in London).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031210dzca0034t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
