\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar fights back vs yen, BOJ help suspected.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Charlotte Cooper  \par \uc2 645 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Dezember 2003\par 13:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Dec 10 (Reuters) - The dollar staged a sharp rebound from recent three-year lows on the yen and life lows on the euro on Wednesday and traders said they strongly suspected Japanese yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was behind the move.  \par \par \uc2 The greenback had already got a lift in Asian trade after the U.S. Federal Reserve promised to keep monetary policy easy "for a considerable period" on Tuesday but dropped its concern about deflation.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar then surged over one percent to around 108.70 yen in a matter of minutes and dealers estimated the Bank of Japan may have bought up to $5 billion in covert action, even though there was no official confirmation from Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 "It's very, very likely that they (the Bank of Japan) were very active this morning. That triggered a lot of other accounts to start buying dollar/yen and that saw the dollar recover against European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as well," said Paul Mackel, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN Amro in London.  \par \par \uc2 "But the rally is fading and the risk is that they may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again later."  \par \par \uc2 By 1230 GMT, the dollar was still one percent up on the day at 108.10 yen. It had touched a three-year low below 107.00 on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 It was a third of a percent stronger at $1.2216 per euro , having hit a record low beyond $1.2270 in the previous session.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was also up 0.3 percent on Swiss franc and Australian dollar but holding close to the previous day's 11-year low on sterling as UK markets awaited a pre-budget report from Chancellor of the Exchequer Gordon Brown.  \par \par \uc2 DOWNWARD SPIRAL  \par \par \uc2 Before Wednesday, Japan was thought to have thrown almost 18 trillion yen ($168 billion) at the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets this year in a bid to prevent a strengthening yen from choking off the country's export-led recovery.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki declined comment on whether the authorities had acted but dealers said Tokyo might have wanted to calm foreign exchanges after the dollar's string of lows threatened to turn its fall into a rout.  \par \par \uc2 "I think Tokyo knows that when market conditions look threateningly like they are moving into disorderly conditions that the scope for coming in more aggressively is there," said Derek Halpenny, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Bank of Tokyo Mitsubishi.  \par \par \uc2 "The last week to 10 days has clearly been one of those situations. In those circumstances I think privately Washington is more than happy to see Japanese authorities come in in a more aggressive way."  \par \par \uc2 An advisory panel to Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi said the economy was still in a mild deflationary state but there were some signs of change.  \par \par \uc2 There has been market speculation \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is close to its allotted ceiling for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds. But a top \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official told Reuters there had been no problem with raising funds via three-month finance bills and he saw no need to use longer maturity debt.  \par \par \uc2 EYES ON U.S.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts were also watching the progress of talks between the U.S. and China. President George W. Bush met Chinese Premier Wen Jiabao on Tuesday and was reported to have told him Washington wanted to see concrete progress regarding adopting a more flexible exchange rate regime.  \par \par \uc2 China later said it was pressing ahead with reforms but poured cold water on expectations loosening the exchange rates would definitely lead to yuan appreciation.  \par \par \uc2 The Fed held rates at one percent on Tuesday and in its accompanying statement reiterated its intention to keep benchmark borrowing costs low for a "considerable period".  \par \par \uc2 That wording disappointed dollar bulls, who had speculated the Fed could drop the phrase, which would have sparked expectation of a rise in U.S. interest rates and boost the greenback.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031210dzca002ia\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
