\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Stocks dip after Fed, dollar rebounds.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Nigel Stephenson  \par \uc2 741 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Dezember 2003\par 14:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Dec 10 (Reuters) - European stocks slid on Wednesday after the U.S. Federal Reserve held interest rates steady the previous session but hinted at an eventual hike while the dollar was boosted by suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Euro zone government bond prices also fell, in line with U.S. Treasuries, after the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  upgraded its view of the economy and said the threat of falling prices had eased.  \par \par \uc2 The fall in bond prices came despite the Fed renewing its commitment to keep rates low for a lengthy period.  \par \par \uc2 "This is a modest step towards tightening somewhere in the first half of next year," said Michael Rottmann, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  and fixed income research at Hypovereinsbank.  \par \par \uc2 "All in all it sounds a little bit more hawkish with the price assessment reaching more neutral wording...To me the odds that we will see the first rate hike at the meeting in March are clearly increasing."  \par \par \uc2 European shares fell as investors used worries about growth-crimping interest rate cuts to lock in gains from a nine-month rally in equity markets.  \par \par \uc2 The FTSE Eurotop 300 index of pan-European blue chips was down 1.1 percent and the narrower DJ Euro STOXX 50 index was off 0.89 percent.  \par \par \uc2 In the U.S., weakness in semiconductor stocks hit the tech-laden Nasdaq, which recorded its biggest percentage fall in seven weeks, pushed down by lower-than-expected earnings from Taiwanese chip companies.  \par \par \uc2 Stocks initially rose after the Fed decision to keep rates on hold, but then gave up gains. The Dow Jones Industrial average, which earlier topped 10,000 points, closed down 0.42 percent to 9,923.42. The tech-heavy Nasdaq closed 2.08 percent lower at 1,908.32 points.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. stock index futures pointed to a weaker opening on Wall Street.  \par \par \uc2 JAPANESE STOCKS DROP  \par \par \uc2 In Tokyo, the Nikkei tumbled two percent to its lowest close in more than two weeks, with chip-related shares hit by disappointing earnings in the industry and the strong yen.  \par \par \uc2 Chip stocks such as Advantest, Tokyo Electron and Oki Electric Industry led the decline.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's decision to send troops to Iraq also weighed.  \par \par \uc2 The Nikkei fell 2.11 percent to 9,910.56 points and the broader TOPIX index ended down 1.69 percent.  \par \par \uc2 "The drop in U.S. semiconductor stock has played a big role in pushing the Nikkei below 10,000," said Hiroaki Kuramochi, head of global equities at Credit Lyonnais.  \par \par \uc2 "And when you see the yen moving into the 106 to the dollar territory, even with wariness about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it creates a lot of uncertainty regarding \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  and that is really pressuring techs and other exporters."  \par \par \uc2 Suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , however, saw the dollar stage a rebound from recent three-year lows on the yen and life lows against the euro.  \par \par \uc2 The greenback had already got a lift in Asian trade after the U.S. Federal Reserve promised to keep monetary policy easy "for a considerable period" on Tuesday but dropped its concern about deflation.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar then surged over one percent to around 108.70 yen in a matter of minutes and dealers estimated the Bank of Japan may have bought up to $5 billion in covert action, even though there was no official confirmation from Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 "It's very, very likely that they (the Bank of Japan) were very active this morning. That triggered a lot of other accounts to start buying dollar/yen and that saw the dollar recover against European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as well," said Paul Mackel, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN Amro in London.  \par \par \uc2 "But the rally is fading and the risk is that they may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again later."  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was last trading around one percent up on the day at 108.02 yen. It had touched a three-year low below 107.00 on Tuesday. The dollar was at $1.2240 per euro, having hit a record low beyond $1.2270 in the previous session.  \par \par \uc2 Euro zone government yields, which move inversely to prices, climbed sharply on fears a U.S. interest rate rise will come sooner rather than later.  \par \par \uc2 The interest rate sensitive two year Schatz yield was 3.6 basis points higher at 2.67 percent. The benchmark 10-year Bund yield was up 1.3 basis points at 4.36 percent.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031210dzca0024f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
