\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-INTERVIEW-MOF confident of increasing long-dated JGBs.   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Shinichi Kishima   \par \uc2 777 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Dezember 2003\par 11:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Dec 10 (Reuters) - Investors in the Japanese government bond (JGB) market are in favour of increased issuance of 20-and 30-year bonds despite concerns about a possible rise in yields, a top \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said on Wednesday.   \par \par \uc2 Jiro Makino, in charge of the ministry's bond issuance and debt management, told Reuters in an interview that he expected the soon-to-be unveiled bond issuance plan for the 2004/05 fiscal year would be in line with such views in the market.   \par \par \uc2 He declined to comment, however, on talk that 20-year JGB issuance would grow to 600 billion yen ($5.60 billion) per month in the new year, which starts next April, from 500 billion.   \par \par \uc2 "At the forum of market participants, there are people voicing concerns about rising yields but the majority view is that long-term yields are attractive in what is overall a very low interest rate environment," said Makino, the director-general of the ministry's Financial Bureau.   \par \par \uc2 "We have held constant conversations with the market, so you'll probably see something that is close to expectations."   \par \par \uc2 The yield on 20-year JGBs currently trades at around 1.8 percent. But it had shot up above 2.0 percent in September after hitting record lows below 0.75 percent in June.   \par \par \uc2 Behind the volatility is concern that the long bonds, whose prices are more sensitive to yield changes, could become highly unpopular as chronic deflationary pressures that have plagued Japan's economy abate. Core consumer prices rose in October from year-ago levels, the first rise in 5-= years.   \par \par \uc2 But Makino said the higher yields at the long end, compared with short-term interest rates near zero, continue to attract some investors, such as life insurers.   \par \par \uc2 He added that the Bank of Japan's recent efforts to clarify that it would not end its ultra-loose monetary policy anytime soon just because of a temporary bounce in prices should keep the market stable.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) is in the final stages of drafting the 2004/05 budget, in which tax revenue is seen barely managing to fund half of the general account - a threshold Makino said Japan must defend.   \par \par \uc2 The market is expecting fresh JGB issuance to rise to 38-39 trillion yen in 2004/05 from 36.4 trillion yen this year, when bond issuance accounted for 40 percent of the budget. Japan's public-sector debt already totals nearly 700 trillion yen, or about 140 percent of gross domestic product - the highest ratio in the industrial world.   \par \par \uc2 COST OF \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 At the short end of the yield curve, Makino said he was not particularly worried about the bulging issuance of three-month finance bills (FBs) for the MOF's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  account to fund \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's massive yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 "At this stage, there has been no problem in funding through FBs and I don't see the need for longer maturity debt," Makino said when asked about exploring different types of funding for \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 In the first 11 months of this year \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  conducted a record 17.8 trillion yen of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , aimed at preventing the yen - which hit three-year highs against the dollar earlier this week - from stalling an export-led economic recovery.   \par \par \uc2 The current 79 trillion yen ceiling for the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  account's borrowing limit is expected to be raised to almost 100 trillion yen when the government compiles an extra budget in the coming weeks to adjust its spending for the current fiscal year.   \par \par \uc2 Some MOF officials have said the ceiling could be boosted to 150 trillion yen or so in fiscal 2004/05 to forestall any speculation about the ministry running out of funding room for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 While short-term interest rates in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been virtually zero for more than two years, rolling over the short-dated FBs could become an expensive proposition when rates start to rise.   \par \par \uc2 To deal with interest rate risks, the MOF last week unveiled the outline of its debt management policy that included the introduction of an underwriting system akin to primary dealers in the U.S. Treasuries market and the use of swaps to hedge interest rate risks.   \par \par \uc2 Makino declined to comment on the specifics, for instance who the primary dealers or the swap counterparties would be.   \par \par \uc2 He said the MOF had to be cautious in entering the swaps market, given the relative size of its potential transactions and its role as the government bond issuer. (Additional reporting by Sumio Ito and Mari Terawaki).    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031210dzca0024d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
