\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar rises as euro and yen hit by profit-taking.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Kazunori Takada  \par \uc2 511 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Dezember 2003\par 07:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Dec 10 (Reuters) - The dollar rose against the yen on Wednesday to move further away from three-year lows as Tokyo stock prices skidded, but many traders doubted a major recovery for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at hand.   \par \par \uc2  "As long as the market focuses on U.S. trade and current account deficits, the dollar will be sold," said Katsunori Kitakura, manager of the treasury department at Chuo Mitsui Trust and Banking.   \par \par \uc2  For now, however, the greenback was finding support on commercial demand from Japanese importers and a steep fall in the Nikkei average, which ended trade down 2.11 percent at 9,910.56, below 10,000 for the first time since December 1.   \par \par \uc2  As of 0620 GMT, the dollar had pushed up to 107.38/43 yen compared with 107.00/08 yen in late U.S. trade.   \par \par \uc2  It fell to a three-year low of 106.74 yen in New York trade before bouncing back up on what traders suspected was Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has thrown almost 18 trillion yen ($168 billion) at the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets this year in a bid to prevent a strengthening yen from choking off the country's export-led recovery.   \par \par \uc2  The euro was easier at $1.2205/10 versus $1.2254/60. Traders said the euro's fall was likely due to profit-taking after the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit a record high - for the eighth consecutive day - of $1.2276 in London on Tuesday.   \par \par \uc2  "I don't think the long-term trend has changed. But for now...there is some sense of achievement in the market," said a trader at a U.S. bank.   \par \par \uc2  MORE DOLLAR FALLS SEEN   \par \par \uc2  A case for a sustained turnaround in the dollar's fortunes wasn't helped by the U.S. Federal Reserve's decision on Tuesday to keep interest rates at 45-year lows, leaving the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yield disadvantage over other units intact, traders said.   \par \par \uc2  "Speculators keep buying the euro," said Takashi Toyahara, a \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Nomura Securities.   \par \par \uc2  "I don't see an end to this momentum. But how far the euro will go from here depends on how aggressively speculators behave in thin trade leading up to the Christmas holidays."   \par \par \uc2  The Fed held rates at one percent and in its accompanying statement reiterated its intent on keeping benchmark borrowing costs low for a "considerable period".   \par \par \uc2  That wording disappointed dollar bulls, who had speculated the Fed could drop the phrase, which would have sparked expectation of a rise in U.S. interest rates and boost the greenback.   \par \par \uc2  Low interest rates have put a damper on capital inflows to the United States, which the world's largest economy desperately needs as its current account deficit has grown to some 5 percent of gross domestic product.   \par \par \uc2  Still, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  did provide a positive sign, saying the probability of an unwelcome fall in inflation had diminished and was now equal to the possibility inflation could rise. ($1=107.11 yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031210dzca001bi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
