\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar wobbles near recent lows after Fed.  \b0\par\par\uc2 412 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Dezember 2003\par 02:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Dec 10 (Reuters) - The dollar wobbled near recent lows on Wednesday as the U.S. Federal Reserve's decision the previous day to keep interest rates at 45-year lows left the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yield disadvantage over other units intact.  \par \par \uc2 Having seen the dollar hit record lows versus the euro for eight consecutive days by Tuesday, most traders think the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's plight is unlikely to go away any time soon,  \par \par \uc2 "Speculators keep buying the euro," said Takashi Toyahara, a \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Nomura Securities.  \par \par \uc2 "I don't see an end to this momentum. But how far the euro will go from here depends on how aggressively speculators behave in thin trade leading up to the Christmas holidays."  \par \par \uc2 A widening current account deficit, worries about Washington's commitment to a strong dollar and simmering violence in Iraq have also been weighing on the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 As of 0030 GMT, the euro was fetching about $1.2250 virtually unchanged from late U.S. levels and only a quarter of a cent below the record high of $1.2276 set in London on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 Against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the euro was at 131.35 yen.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar slipped slightly to 107.15 yen from 107.25 yen in late U.S. trade. But it managed to keep some distance from its three-year low of 106.74 yen, hit in New York trade, thanks largely to wariness about dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has thrown almost 18 trillion yen ($168 billion) at the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets this year in a bid to prevent a strengthening yen from choking off the country's export-led recovery.  \par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, the Fed held rates at one percent and in its accompanying statement reiterated its intent on keeping benchmark borrowing costs low for a "considerable period".  \par \par \uc2 That wording disappointed dollar bulls, who had speculated the Fed could drop the phrase, which would have sparked expectation of a rise in U.S. interest rates and boost the greenback.  \par \par \uc2 Low interest rates have put a damper on capital inflows to the United States, which the world's largest economy desperately needs as its current account deficit has grown to some 5 percent of gross domestic product.  \par \par \uc2 Still, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  did provide a positive sign, saying the probability of an unwelcome fall in inflation had diminished and was now equal to the possibility inflation could rise. ($1=107.11 yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031210dzca00066\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
