\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. Rate Concerns Pressure Dollar; Euro Hits Fresh High  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Agnes T. Crane  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 884 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Dezember 2003\par 22:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK -- The dollar sank against its major rivals Tuesday after the Federal Reserve affirmed its reluctance to raise interest rates any time soon, although it managed to claw back from yet another record low against the euro.  \par \par \uc2 Apparent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  late in the day also shored up the dollar against the yen after it had fallen through a key support level to its worst showing in three years, although for many dealers, the reprieve seemed temporary.  \par \par \uc2 The Federal Open Market Committee, the policy-making arm of the Fed, issued yet another nuanced official statement on its monetary policy, but clearly signaled it isn't prepared to raise its federal-funds target of 1% in the near future.  \par \par \uc2 "The things that changed weren't necessarily what people thought would change," said Jason Bonanca, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Credit Suisse First Boston in New York.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders had expected the Fed to drop the phrase "considerable period" when describing how long the Fed would keep rates low for, as a nod to the improving economic landscape. Instead, the Fed kept that language, but instead de-emphasized the threat of falling inflation, a risk first flagged by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  back in May. In its official statement, the Fed said "the probability of an unwelcome fall in inflation has diminished in recent months and now appears almost equal to that of a rise in inflation."  \par \par \uc2 Low inflation and slack in the economy allow the Fed to keep short-term interest rates low for "a considerable period," the FOMC said.  \par \par \uc2 "We're left with a slightly more hawkish Fed," said Mr. Bonanca. But it is "still much move dovish than what we see on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  landscape," given recent tightening of monetary policy in Australia and the United Kingdom. This should continue to weigh on the dollar, he said.  \par \par \uc2 Lower interest rates in the U.S. have weighed heavily on the dollar this year and are likely to continue to encourage investors to park funds elsewhere, which would help erode the value of the greenback further. Some analysts believe the Fed would have to raise rates by one-half percentage point before the dollar can find its footing against its major rivals.  \par \par \uc2 "Persistently low interest rates erode the greenback's reward potential," noted Ashraf Laidi, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at MG Financial in New York.  \par \par \uc2 In late trading Tuesday, the euro was fetching $1.2254, down from a fresh record high of $1.2278 hit earlier in the session, but above Monday's $1.2224. The dollar, meanwhile, recovered from its worst levels of 106.82 yen touched soon after the FOMC statement, to trade at 107.02 yen, down from 107.34 yen on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said the Tokyo branches of large Japanese banks - familiar agent banks for the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  - aggressively bought dollars when the greenback traded as low as 106.74 yen. "They quickly started bidding it there," said the senior dealer at a Japanese bank in New York.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese authorities have sold over 16 trillion yen so far this year in an attempt to stem the yen's rapid appreciation, which they contend undermines the competitiveness of Japanese goods on global markets and threatens the country's export-led recovery.  \par \par \uc2 Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was trading at 1.2622 francs, up from the new seven-year low of 1.2598 francs established earlier in the session, but down from Monday's 1.2667 francs.  \par \par \uc2 Sterling also hit an 11-year high of $1.7466 in the aftermath of the Fed meeting as that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  looked particularly attractive with the Bank of England already raising rates in the U.K. In late trading, sterling eased back to $1.7458, up from $1.7331 late Monday.  \par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar also benefited from the Fed's view that interest rates in the U.S. can remain low for a while longer. The Australian dollar fetched 74.14 U.S. cents late in the day, rebounding from a low of 73.73 U.S. cents, and up from Monday's 74.03 U.S. cents.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier in the global session, the euro spiked to a new all-time high of $1.2278 for the eighth consecutive trading day. The new highs in the euro and the pound, however, convinced some hedge funds to book some hefty profits, a move that began in the higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and then spilled into the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 President George W. Bush's meeting with Chinese Premier Wen Jiabao yielded little new on the deepening trade tensions between the two nations. Mr. Wen only reiterated the Chinese government's view that the U.S. trade deficit is a problem and that China takes U.S. trade concerns seriously.  \par \par \uc2 Mr. Bush brought up the issue of China's practice of pegging the yuan to the dollar, but White House spokesman Scott McClellan noted that getting China to float the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a long-term process.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par   -By Agnes T. Crane, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2122; \par    agnes.crane@dowjones.com   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Jamie McGeever contributed to this report.)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020031209dzc900091\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
