\par\par \b \uc2 OUTLOOK 04: Dlr To Flirt With Y100 On US C/A, Japan Econ  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Ron Harui  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 1,067 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Dezember 2003\par 10:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The yen, already around three-year highs against the dollar, is expected to push higher in 2004 as a yawning U.S. trade deficit and a recovering Japanese economy push the U.S. unit down near Y100.  \par \par \uc2 Although the U.S. economy is sizzling with its strongest growth in 20 years, financial markets are seizing on the massive current-account gap, which requires about $2 billion of foreign money every business day to finance. Moreover, despite the rebound, the Federal Reserve is widely expected to keep interest rates near their 45-year lows, further damping the incentive to own U.S. assets.  \par \par \uc2 And critically for the dollar's exchange rate against the yen, a growing Japanese economy may temper some of Tokyo's zeal in curbing the unruly yen, allowing the dollar to slide in the coming year, analysts say.  \par \par \uc2 "The weak-dollar trend is unlikely to change, and Japan's economic growth could beat market expectations" in 2004, said Tohru Sasaki, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at J.P. Morgan Chase Bank in Tokyo. He expects the greenback will fall to Y95 by the end of next year.  \par \par \uc2 Six banks canvassed by Dow Jones Newswires see the dollar falling, on average, to Y101 by late 2004, with forecast lows ranging from Y95 to Y105. The dollar, which started 2003 at Y118.82, tumbled to Y107.06 on Tuesday, its lowest since November 2000.  \par \par \uc2 Around 0930 it was at Y107.26.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't flirted with yen "parity" since November 1995, when it was recovering from that year's modern-era low of Y79. As Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  crumbled in the region's financial crisis, the dollar climbed as Y147.61 in 1998. This year the dollar traded up to Y121.88 in March before falling against the yen \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as part of its global weakness.  \par \par \uc2 The Japanese Finance Ministry successfully defended the dollar at Y115 from midyear until the U.S. convinced Japan and the rest of the Group of Seven to call for greater \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility - code for China, Japan and other Asian export powerhouses to stop weakening their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    U.S. Structural Problems To Haunt Dollar In 2004 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At first blush it seems odd for the dollar to fall when the U.S. economy is growing at developing-country rates. The world's biggest economy expanded at an eye-popping annual clip of 8.2% in the third quarter.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But that incandescent growth isn't being met at home - the U.S. is sucking in exports at a torrid pace, with the current-account deficit running at more than 5% of gross domestic product. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market is ignoring good news about U.S. growth and 18-month highs for share prices and focusing instead on America's precarious reliance on foreigners to make up for its lack of savings.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Structural problems, chiefly the massive current-account and budget deficits, are expected to continue haunting the dollar next year.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Huge foreign-investment flows have plugged the current-account gap so far, but concerns are growing that foreign investors will have less appetite for dollar-denominated securities with U.S. interest rates expected to remain low. The Bank of England and the Australian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have started what appears to be a global cycle of higher rates, but market participants expect the Fed to remain accommodative despite strong U.S. growth.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Although the economy is growing, inflation is quiescent, with plenty of slack left in the economy and the labor market, in particular, still weak. Sasaki and other analysts think the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may keep its key policy rate around 1% well into 2004 and perhaps even for the entire year after a disappointing rise in non-farm payrolls.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar was pummeled Friday when the government reported non-farm payrolls rose by only 57,000 in November, well below economists' forecasts for a 150,000 gain. That has pushed back expectations for when the Fed will begin raising rates.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "A Fed rate hike in June had been expected, but this may no longer be possible," said Koji Fukaya, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  analyst at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi. He forecasts the dollar at Y100 at the end of next year.  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Econ Recovery May Curtail MOF's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 On the other side of the dollar/yen equation, the Japanese economy is picking up, local stocks are in demand, and those factors may curb Tokyo's unprecedented campaign of yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to support the dollar.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The government has upgraded its economic assessment three times in the past four months, saying in November the economy is "showing an incipient recovery" on the back of an improving global economy and higher production and consumer spending. Japan's GDP grew for the seventh straight quarter in the July-September period, at a real annual rate of 1.4%. And although it's off its recent highs, the benchmark Nikkei 225 Stock Average is up 33% from April's 20-year lows.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Continued strength in exports and corporate capital spending will likely sustain the economy and rising corporate profits are expected to keep Japanese equities on an even keel. Welcome factors such as those may make it harder for the Finance Ministry to justify \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to prevent "excessive" rises in the yen.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ministry sold Y17.8 trillion, mostly for dollars, through the Bank of Japan in the first 11 months of the year, well above its previous full-year record.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Assuming the nation's economy shows further improvements, acceptance of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could decrease," said Toru Umemoto, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Morgan Stanley, in a report. "Maintaining the political consensus on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is becoming more and more important for the MOF to be able to continue \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the yen."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. and other trading partners have made it clear they will keep up the pressure on Asian authorities not to cheapen their own \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in a "beggar-thy-neighbor" policy, aimed at boosting exports, especially when economies are improving.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A stronger yen makes Japanese goods more expensive or less profitable overseas and crimps the yen value of Japanese companies' foreign earnings. It also inflames deflationary pressures at home.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Ron Harui, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2942; ron.harui@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031209dzc9000gf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
