\par\par \b \uc2 Japan Govt Bonds End Higher After Strong 5-Year Auction  \b0\par\par\uc2 787 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Dezember 2003\par 07:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japanese government bonds ended higher Tuesday after strong results at an auction of five-year JGBs and helped by a rise in the yen against the dollar, which boosts deflationary pressures.  \par \par \uc2 But JGB prices ended below their peak after much stronger-than-expected machinery order data showed Japan's economic recovery remains on track.  \par \par \uc2 December 10-year JGB futures closed 0.16 higher at 138.36 while the yield on benchmark 10-year bonds fell 3.5 basis points to 1.325%.  \par \par \uc2 Looking ahead, JGB prices Wednesday will be strongly influenced by the impact of a U.S. Federal Reserve policy meeting decision on U.S. Treasurys prices later today.  \par \par \uc2 Still, some analysts say they expect further gains for JGBs in the rest of December as pension funds and life insurers catch up on planned investment after staying on the sidelines in October and November.  \par \par \uc2 "Real money investors - life insurers, pension funds - all of them are underweight in superlong bonds and lag behind their investment plans for this fiscal year," said Akihiko Yokoyama, chief strategist at J.P. Morgan in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 Yokoyama expects the yield on 10-year JGBs could fall as low as 1.2% by the end of December.  \par \par \uc2 Still, it was buying of five-year bonds by banks Tuesday that pushed futures prices to their intraday high of 138.46 after surprisingly strong demand at an auction of Y1.9 trillion of five-year JGBs. The finance ministry sold Y1.887 trillion of the bonds with a 0.6% coupon, with the lowest price of 99.88 yielding 0.624%.  \par \par \uc2 UFJ Tsubasa Securities, the securities arm of UFJ Holdings Inc., was the largest buyer of bonds at the auction, scooping up Y545 billion.  \par \par \uc2 "I think (UFJ) went too far at this level," said a senior trader at another major Japanese bank. Still, other major banks want to buy short- and medium-term JGBs on any price dips, he added.  \par \par \uc2 Banks got that price-dip buying opportunity a little later in the session when data showed core machinery orders jumped 17.4% in October from September, ending a three-month slide.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Prices At Around 0600 GMT. \par  \par    Tiffe Sep 3-Mos Euroyen Price:    99.850   Up 0.005 \par    SGX Sep 3-Mos Euroyen Price:      99.850   Up 0.010 \par    TSE Dec 10-Yr JGB Future Price:  138.36    Up 0.16 \par    SGX Dec 10-Yr JGB Future Price:  138.24    Up 0.12 \par    10-Yr 1.4% JGB No. 256 Yield:      1.325%  Dn 0.035 \par  \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Despite more signs of a continuing recovery, Bank of Japan Governor Toshihiko Fukui reiterated his familiar comment that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  won't end its easy policy anytime soon and will keep monetary settings loose even if the economy improves as ending deflation will take some time.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In fact, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's battle against deflation remains tough with the yen setting fresh three-year highs against the dollar this week, prompting \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to directly \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets to stem the yen's rise, according to traders.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The rise in JGB prices for the third successive session shows market participants have little concern about the finance ministry's issuance plan for next fiscal year, which the ministry is set to unveil next week.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 J.P. Morgan's Yokoyama said the plan, which has been foreshadowed by ministry officials in a series of meetings with JGB market participants, "has been completely priced in."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ministry is expected to unveil a plan that requires it to place a record Y120 trillion to Y122 trillion of JGBs in the market next fiscal year, although Yokoyama said the ministry may announce a slightly smaller amount "to create positive sentiment."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Overall, the ministry's issuance plan along with its recently announced debt management plan aim to keep a lid on a price volatility, he added.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the swap market, aggressive receiving by major Japanese banks pushed rates lower in the three- and four-year zone, in a sign that major banks may be boosting their interest in the swap and bond market. In the five-year zone, receiving was replaced by paying after the machinery order data. The three-year swap rate fell to 0.365% from 0.3875% and the four-year rate dropped to 0.52625% from 0.55%. Further along the curve, trading was less active. In the super-long zone, the 10-20-year spread widened slightly by 0.25 basis points, trading at 55 basis points.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Iain McDonald, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2941; iain.mcdonald@dowjones.com (Hiroshi Miwa contributed to this story).  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Hugh Lawson and Bennett Richardson  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1037 4153  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020031209dzc90000m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
