\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Options:Dlr/Yen Vols Up; \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Reportedly  \highlight22\uc2 Intervenes \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 475 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Dezember 2003\par 21:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Volatilities implied by dollar/yen \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options remain well bid in New York midafternoon Monday a short while after Japanese monetary authorities reportedly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market selling yen for dollars.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers say the Tokyo branch of a large U.S. investment bank stepped in on behalf of Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying dollars when the greenback was languishing around three-year lows at Y107.15-20. Other Japanese financial institutions came in with bids of their own, raising the total \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  amount Monday to as much as $800 million, one dealer estimated.  \par \par \uc2 And with the euro hovering around record highs printed earlier Monday, volatilities are well bid across the board.  \par \par \uc2 "Yen vols are well bid as the dollar looked to probe the downside and euro vols are bid too," said an options dealer at a hedge fund in New York, noting that the euro is within 10 pips of its all-time high of $1.2240.  \par \par \uc2 In the over-the-counter market, one-month at-the-money dollar/yen options implied volatility of 9.20%/9.60%, up from 9.00%/9.40% in Tokyo earlier Monday and sharply up from 8.40%/8.80% in New York Friday.  \par \par \uc2 Volatilities implied by one-month ATM euro/dollar options are coming off slightly from overnight highs but could well spike up if options barriers at $1.2250 are taken out, dealers said. A short while ago they were quoted at 10.20%/10.60%, against 10.50%/10.75% in Tokyo Monday and 10.30%/10.60% in New York Friday.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers reported rising corporate interest in hedging as the year-end approaches and the dollar's slide shows no sign of reversing.  \par \par \uc2 In addition, market participants say there is a big end of year euro call option bought at $1.18 with a double-no-touch strike at $1.24. A large Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is suspected of holding this option.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, one-month ATM euro/yen options implied volatility of 9.10%/9.35%, up from 8.95%/9.25% in Tokyo and 8.90%/9.10% in New York Friday.  \par \par \uc2 Around 2:30 EST Monday, the dollar was at Y107.37, down from Y107.67 late Friday in New York but up from its lows Monday of around Y107.15. The euro was at $1.2232, up from $1.2162 Friday in New York and nudging its record high of $1.2240. It was also at Y131.35, up from Y130.98 Friday in New York.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was also hovering near multiyear lows plumbed earlier Monday against the Swiss franc, sterling, South African rand and Australian and New Zealand dollars.  \par \par \uc2 - By Jamie McGeever, Dow Jones Newswires; 201 938 2096; jamie.mcgeever@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 1077 4127 44427  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031208dzc8000s6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
