\par\par \b \uc2 Dow Jones Asia-Pacific Top Stories Sked For Dec 8  \b0\par\par\uc2 2,357 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Dezember 2003\par 10:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Following are the top Asia-Pacific stories featured on Dow Jones Newswires as of 0900 GMT, along with items expected later in the day. Please direct any questions or comments to asianewseds@dowjones.com or by phone on 65-6415-4020.  \par \par \uc2 MACRO/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /FIXED INCOME  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Australia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Monday defended its decision to raise interest rates twice since early November, signaling more tightenings are likely to offset inflationary pressures. ("=WRAP: Australia's RBA Hoses Down Rate Speculation" ran at 0809 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 CANBERRA: The Australian government Monday more than doubled its expected budget surplus for 2003-04, ensuring it will go to a general election with bulging coffers. ("=WRAP: Budget Review Boosts Australian Election Coffers" ran at 0447 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: The Australian dollar leapt to a fresh 6-year high on Monday and appears likely to keep climbing in European trading after the Reserve Bank painted an optimistic picture for the economy in 2004. ("Australian Dlr Rallies To New 6-yr High On RBA Rhetoric" ran at 0555 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 FUKUOKA, Japan: Bank of Japan Governor Toshihiko Fukui said Monday that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains ready to alter monetary policy quickly if needed, although the risks for Japan's economy are weighted evenly. ("BOJ Fukui -2: Risks For Economy Evenly Balanced" ran at 0602 GMT; WRAP will run around 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Lending by Japanese banks, excluding that by shinkin banks, or credit unions, fell 5.0% in November from a year earlier, a sharper pace of decline than the 4.8% drop in October, the Bank of Japan said Monday. ("Japan Bank Lending -2: Lending Falls On Yr For 71st Month" ran at 2355 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan's money supply, or M2 plus certificate of deposits, grew 1.6% on year in November, slightly faster than October's 1.5% but still slow compared to levels posted a year ago, indicating the expansion of money remains weak as banks continue to hold back on lending. ("Japan Nov Money Supply -2: Oct Money Supply Unrevised" ran at 0013 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =OUTLOOK 04/India Bonds: India's biggest bond market bull-run in recent memory is probably history. With the roaring economy poised to be one of Asia's star performers in 2004, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  appears to have ended its longest campaign of interest-rate cuts in decades, and bond yields look set to rise in the months ahead, analysts say. ("=OUTLOOK 04: India Econ Boom Spells End To Bond Bull-Run" ran at 0841 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: The Asian Development Bank said Monday it has lifted its forecast for economic growth this year in developing Asian countries to 5.7% from 5.3%, partly reflecting strong growth in domestic consumption in the region. The ADB also expects the area, which includes all Asian countries other than Japan, to see economic growth of 6.2% next year, a slight upward revision from the bank's prior 6.1% forecast. It anticipates growth will pick up further to 6.3% in 2005. ("ADB -2: Cites Strong Consumption Growth In Region" ran at 0348 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Euro-yen options prices have been bouncing lately even as spot trading has calmed a bit in recent sessions. But as the pair isn't likely to remain subdued for long in the spot market, it makes sense to position for more volatility. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Option Views:EUR/JPY Straddles Attractive Pre-Yearend" ran at 0334 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA: The World Bank Monday raised concerns about growing trade protectionism in Indonesia following the failure of World Trade Organization talks earlier this year. ("World Bk Raises Concerns On Indonesia Trade Protectionism" ran at 0711 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: China International Trust & Investment Corp. said Monday it will sell 10 billion yuan in bonds this week, an issue that will help recapitalize a bank owned by the Beijing financial group and otherwise pay down debt. ("China Citic Bond -2: Two Tranches Of Debt" ran at 0203 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Amid expectations the yuan may be revalued, a new report says Chinese banks are repatriating their massive overseas deposits - undermining U.S. Treasurys and the dollar in the process. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Asia: China Banks Repatriate Funds, Hurt Dollar - BIS" ran at 0133 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: The People's Bank of China said Monday it will offer 30 billion yuan of short-term \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bills this week, at yields that are unchanged or below levels that have failed to entice buyers in recent money market \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ("China PBOC To Issue CNY30 Bln Bills In This Week's Op" ran at 0723 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan's Government Pension Investment Fund, one of the world's largest pension funds, plans to soon allocate money to hedge funds, the fund's executive investment officer, Noboru Terada, said Monday. ("Japan Govt Pension Invest Fund May Use Hedge Funds Soon" ran at 0533 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: Taiwan's exports rose 16.2% in November to a record high of US$13.83 billion, while imports surged 32.6% to US$12.39 billion, the third-highest monthly figure on record - fueled by robust trade with Hong Kong, China, South Korea, Europe and South East Asia, data issued Monday showed. ("DATA SNAP: Taiwan's November Exports Reach Record High" ran at 0814 GMT; DATA VIEW will run around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Retail sales leaped in October as increasing consumer confidence and a sharp increase in tourist arrivals kept shopkeepers busier than economists expected, the government said Monday. Retail sales rose 3.8% in value to HK$14.6 billion from a year earlier, according to provisional data. They expanded 4.6% by volume after adjusting for price changes over the year to October, the Census and Statistics Department said. ("DATA SNAP: HK Oct Retail Sales Rise Faster Than Expected" ran at 0838 GMT; DATA VIEW will run around 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: Moody's Investors Service has affirmed New Zealand's AAA foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  credit rating, Finance Minister Michael Cullen said Monday. A report by Moody's says that structural reforms to the New Zealand economy in the 1980s have made it more resilient to external shocks, Cullen said. ("NZ Fin Min/Moody's -2: More Resilient To External Shocks" ran at 2035 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MANILA: The Philippines plans to return to the international bond market early next year to raise at least $750 million through the reopening of existing sovereign bonds, National Treasurer Sergio Edeza told Dow Jones Newswires Monday.("=EXCLUSIVE: Philippines/Bonds -2: 1Q Best Time To Issue" first take ran at 0641 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: China Huaneng Group Monday confirmed plans to issue 10-year bonds to raise 4 billion yuan. ("China Huaneng Plans CNY4 Bln 10-Year Bond Sale This Week" ran at 0010 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korea's economic agenda isn't likely to be affected by a forthcoming Cabinet reshuffle, which will be limited in size, according to a presidential spokeswoman Monday. ("S Korea Cabinet Reshuffle Won't Affect Economic Agenda" ran at 0141 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korea's service sector output rose 1.5% on year in October, slowing sharply from a 9.9% growth in the same month of 2002, according to the National Statistical Office in a statement Monday. ("Korea Oct Svc Sector Output +1.5% On Yr; +9.9% Oct '02" first take ran at 0300 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI: The Indian government is betting on Special Economic Zones to attract more foreign investment, but acknowledges that it needs at least a decade to catch up with China in that respect. ("India Plans To Set Up Export Zones To Attract More Invest" ran at 0810 GMT; WRAP will run around 1100 GMT or later)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: New Zealand electricity lines concern Powerco said it has appointed an arranger to bring a medium-term bond issue with a likely size of around NZ$200 million to the domestic market early next year. ("=INTERVIEW: Powerco Aims To Issue NZ Bond In Early 2004" ran at 0523 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HANOI: Vietnam's newly pledged foreign direct investment rose 23% on year to $1.44 billion in the first 11 months of 2003, with the bulk going to the industrial and construction sectors, the government's Statistics Office said Monday. ("Vietnam Jan-Nov Pledged FDI +23% On Year To $1.44B" ran at 0458 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 KARACHI: A slowdown in Pakistan's exports in November was just a cyclical lull following a rush of Christmas orders from Europe and the U.S. in the previous months, a senior export official said, adding that he would only be concerned if the slower export growth continued into the months ahead. ("INTERVIEW:Pakistan's Slow Nov Exports Were Just Cyclical" ran at 0543 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  MARKET ROUNDUP: The dollar dipped to a fresh three-year low against the yen in Asia Monday, but sellers refrained from pushing the greenback even lower amid worries \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen. ("Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 :Dlr Dips To New Low Vs Yen, But Bears Fear MOF" ran at 0552 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 EQUITIES  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Virgin Blue made a solid debut Monday on the Australian Stock Exchange, with founder Richard Branson promising to turn up the heat on staunch rival Qantas Airways and its domestic low-cost carrier Jetstar. The shares opened at A$2.40 each, compared with the initial public offer price of A$2.25, and rose to an intraday high of A$2.49 before closing at A$2.43. ("REPEAT: WRAP: Australia's Virgin Blue Makes Solid Debut" ran at 0833 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: CSK Corp. said Monday it will sell almost its entire stake in video game software maker Sega Corp. to pachinko gaming equipment maker Sammy Corp., a move that may trigger realignment in the game industry. ("CSK/Sega -2: May Spur Realignment In Game Industry" ran at 0643 GMT; WRAP will run around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =OUTLOOK 04/DRAM: After tasting some profitability this year, most makers of dynamic random access memory (DRAM) chips should gain in 2004 as businesses begin to replace old computers, but it's still too early to bring out the champagne since a number of factors, including oversupply, could stifle a DRAM rebound in 2004. (Asia DRAM Report/OUTLOOK 04 will run around 1030 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Micromotor maker Johnson Electric Monday reported a first-half net profit of US$81.5 million, up slightly from US$80.4 million in the year-ago period. ("Johnson Electric/Earnings" will run around 0915 GMT; WRAP will run around 1100 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: State-run Korea Development Bank may take over cash-strapped LG Card "as a last resort" if the sale of the sale of the company doesn't proceed smoothly, said a KDB spokesman Monday. ("Korea Devt Bk May Take Over LG Card If Sale Not Smooth" ran at 0045 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: A top executive of Netherlands' ASML Holdings, the world's biggest manufacturer of semiconductor lithography machines, says technology that the company has developed may help chipmakers reduce the cost of buying machines to make powerful new chips. (INTERVIEW will run after 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: New Zealand's Telecom Corp. is considering a merger of its Australian consumer and corporate businesses in a bid to boost their overall financial performance. ("=INTERVIEW: Telecom NZ Looks To Merge Australian Units" ran at 0101 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA: State-owned Bank Negara Indonesia hopes to recover IDR1.05 trillion to offset potential losses from a lending fraud amounting to IDR1.7 trillion, BNI's president Saifuddien Hasan told reporters Monday. ("Bk Negara Indonesia May Recover IDR1.05T From Loan Fraud" ran at 0522 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA: The Indonesian Bank Restructuring Agency said Monday it will relaunch the sale of Asia Pulp & Paper Co.'s debt as it's unhappy with the bids received last week. IBRA will relaunch the debt sale later this week and expects to conclude the sale by the end of this year, IBRA's Chairman Syafruddin Temenggung said. ("REPEAT: Indonesia IBRA To Relaunch APP Debt Sale This Wk" ran at 0424 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: Seoul Guarantee Insurance is planning an overseas sale of its shares in Samsung Life Insurance, said a spokesman at Seoul Guarantee Monday. ("Seoul Guarantee Plans Samsung Life Share Sale Overseas" ran at 0022 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: Kookmin Bank, South Korea's largest lender, has submitted a non-binding letter of intent to take over insolvent Hanil Life Insurance, said a bank spokesman Monday. ("Kookmin Bk/Hanil Life -2: No Other Bidder For Hanil" ran at 0238 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korea's Samsung Electronics is expected to retain its dominance in the dynamic random access memory (DRAM) market in 2003, with a 28.1% share of the market revenue, according to a report published by U.S. market research firm iSuppli Corp. ("Samsung Elec To Retain Dominance In DRAM Mkt '03 -iSuppli" ran at 0233 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: Chinese share market regulators have ordered the closure of Newchina Securities for unspecified violation of regulations, their second brokerage shutdown order in recent weeks, according to a report Monday. It said the company, based in Changchun, the capital city of Jilin province, violated regulations, which weren't specified. ("China Regulators Order 2nd Brokerage Closed In Recent Wks" ran at 0131 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: U.S.-based PalmSource Inc., developer of the Palm operating system used in handheld devices like personal digital assistants, says it sees huge potential in the wireless market. ("PalmSource Sees "Enormous Potential" In Wireless Market" ran at 0506 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: Fletcher Challenge Forests, a New Zealand forestry company, is hoping to have finished the sale of its forest estate by Christmas and is aiming to make the highest possible cash return to shareholders, Chairman Dryden Spring said Monday. ("=INTERVIEW: NZ Fletcher Forests Targets Sale By Christmas" ran at 2343 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 ENERGY/COMMODITIES  \par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI: A slew of new players, both domestic and foreign, will shake up India's protected gasoline and diesel market in 2004, and one consequence could be lower pump prices for motorists. ("=REPEAT: OUTLOOK 04: Competition Shakes Up India Gasoline" ran at 0515 GMT; note "=CORRECT: OUTLOOK 04:Competition Shakes Up India Gasoline" that ran at 0715 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 62550  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031208dzc8000cl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
