\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ.COM:US Stocks Mixed As Market Awaits Tues Fed Meeting  \b0\par\par\uc2 717 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Dezember 2003\par 17:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par   By Erin Schulte \par   Of THE WALL STREET JOURNAL ONLINE \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--A sinking dollar and hesitation ahead of Tuesday's decision by the Federal Reserve about interest rates kept stocks in check Monday, with major indexes hardly budging.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average rose 37 points to 9899 by late morning, while the Nasdaq Composite Index edged down 6 to 1932. The Standard & Poor's 500-stock index rose a point to 1063.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar fell to a new all-time low against the euro and a three-year low against the yen in overseas trading Monday, but sellers refrained from pushing the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  too much lower in Asia amid concern that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen.  \par \par \uc2 The euro rose against the dollar for the seventh consecutive session, extending a rally that has pushed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  up 16% against the U.S. counterpart this year. The euro passed the previous high of $1.2169 it set Friday after disappointing U.S. job figures. The dollar has sagged on concerns about the U.S. trade and budget deficits. Worries over terrorism and the U.S. occupation of Iraq also have played a role.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese authorities, worried that a sharp yen rise could derail the country's economic recovery by hurting exports, sold an unprecedented Y17.8 trillion in the first 11 months of the year in an effort to rein in the surging \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Analysts believe a further decline in the dollar could trigger even more selling by Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, Federal Reserve policy makers gather in Washington on Tuesday for their Open Market Committee meeting. Though no change in interest rates is expected, the market will be watching closely for any signs that the Fed may be more inclined to tighten monetary policy given some recent encouraging readings on the health of the economy. Investors said the Fed's move and inflation, as well as the dollar's slide, were major factors affecting equities right now.  \par \par \uc2 "Market participants have begun their flight to assets not affected by inflation, i.e., gold," which hit a new eight-year high Monday before pulling back, said Brian Williamson, vice president, equity trading, for The Boston Company Asset Management. "Investors expect no change in rates tomorrow but they will be looking to see if the Fed changes their tune to a more tightening bias."  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the dollar's slide could prompt foreign investors to reduce their exposure to U.S. equities and repatriate their money. "The dollar debacle is intensifying so watch for foreign investors to move out of U.S. denominated assets," Williamson said.  \par \par \uc2 Among stocks to watch, U.K. telecommunications company Cable & Wireless announced early Monday the sale of its U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , exiting the U.S. market sooner than it had expected to and for about half the forecast cost. The units will be sold to Gores Technology Group, a private investment firm that focuses on investments in the technology and telecommunications sectors, for $125 million. Cable & Wireless shares climbed 4.8%.  \par \par \uc2 JetBlue climbed 7% after Merrill Lynch upgraded the stock to neutral from buy and set a 12-month price target of $32. Merrill analyst Michael Linenberg said the stock's 45% decline in the past two months more than accounts for concerns about the company's lowered margin expectations.  \par \par \uc2 In major U.S. market action:  \par \par \uc2 Stocks were mixed. On the Big Board, where 321.3 million shares traded, 1,695 stocks rose, while 1,299 fell. On the Nasdaq, where about 521.3 million shares changed hands, 1,708 declined and 1,156 advanced.  \par \par \uc2 Bonds slipped. The 10-year Treasury note lost 1/4 point, or about $2.50 for each $1,000 invested. The yield, which moves inversely to price, rose to 4.27%. The 30-year bond was down 3/4 point to yield 5.12%.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar dropped. It traded at 107.29, down from \u165\'00\'A5107.70 late Friday in New York, while the euro rose against the dollar to $1.2211 from $1.2167 in the previous session.  \par \par \uc2 For continuously updated news from The Wall Street Journal, see WSJ.com at  {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://wsj.com}}}\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 20716 4116 5042  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020031208dzc80009z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
