\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ.COM: US Stocks Seen Lower On Dollar, Fed Meeting  \b0\par\par\uc2 781 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Dezember 2003\par 15:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par   A WALL STREET JOURNAL ONLINE NEWS ROUNDUP \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Stocks were likely to see marginal losses Monday morning, as investors look ahead to a key announcement on interest rates and keep a wary eye on the continuing decline of the dollar.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Futures on the Standard & Poor's 500 Index were down 2.70 to 1059.50 while Dow Jones Industrial Average futures were lower by 21 to 9838. Nasdaq futures were off 5.50 to 1404.50.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar fell to new all-time low against the euro and a three-year low against the yen in overseas trading Monday, but sellers refrained from pushing the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  too much lower in Asia amid concern that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen.  \par \par \uc2 The euro surged against the dollar for the seventh consecutive session, extending a rally that has pushed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  up 16% against the U.S. counterpart this year. The euro passed the previous high of $1.2169 it set Friday after disappointing U.S. job figures. The dollar has sagged on concerns about the U.S. trade and budget deficits. Worries over terrorism and the U.S. occupation of Iraq also have played a role.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese authorities, worried that a sharp yen rise could derail the country's economic recovery by hurting exports, sold an unprecedented Y17.8 trillion in the first 11 months of the year in an effort to rein in the surging \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Analysts believe a further decline in the dollar could trigger even more selling by Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 No major economic data was due for release on Monday. Tuesday, Federal Reserve policy makers gather in Washington for their Open Market Committee meeting. Though no change in interest rates is expected, the market will be watching closely for any signs that the Fed may be more inclined to tighten monetary policy given some recent encouraging readings on the health of the economy. Among stocks to watch, Freddie Mac named Richard Syron as its new chairman and chief executive. The former head of the American Stock Exchange replaces Greg Parseghian, who was asked to step down after federal regulators determined he played a role in a series of accounting misdeeds.  \par \par \uc2 U.K. telecommunications company Cable & Wireless announced early Monday the sale of its U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , exiting the U.S. market sooner than it had expected to and for about half the forecast cost. The units will be sold to Gores Technology Group, a private investment firm that focuses on investments in the technology and telecommunications sectors, for $125 million.  \par \par \uc2 Overseas, Japan's benchmark Nikkei 225 Index saw a 3.2% skid, as investors were prompted to head for the exits by the stumbling dollar. Technology shares were especially weak. In London, shares fell, but more modestly, as investors largely shrugged off a better-than-expected report on October industrial production in the U.K.  \par \par \uc2 The bullish cues investors had been waiting for from Intel failed to materialize Friday, and the latest news on job growth disappointed as well, sending major stock indexes lower. The Dow Jones Industrial Average fell 68.14 points to 9862.68, while the Nasdaq Composite Index declined 30.98, or 1.6%, to 1937.82 and the S&P 500-stock index shed 8.22 to 1061.50.  \par \par \uc2 For the week, the Dow industrials ended up 0.8% and the S&P 500 gained 0.3%. The Nasdaq lost 1.1%. On Thursday, the Dow industrials hit an 18-month high, and on Monday, the Nasdaq hit a 22-month high. Gold closed at $406.40 on Friday, its highest level in nearly eight years; prices were up 2.4% for the week.  \par \par \uc2 In major U.S. market action: Major stock indexes fell. On the Big Board Friday, where 1.23 billion shares traded, 1,787 stocks fell, while 1,437 rose. On the Nasdaq, where about 1.66 billion shares changed hands, 2,063 declined and 1,080 advanced.  \par \par \uc2 Bonds were mixed. The 10-year Treasury note rose about 1/16 point, or about 62 cents for each $1,000 invested. The yield, which moves inversely to price, rose to 4.229%. The 30-year bond was down 1/32 point to yield 5.066%.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar dropped. It traded at Y107.58, down from Y107.70 late Friday in New York, while the euro rose against the dollar to $1.12205 from $1.2167 in the previous session.  \par \par \uc2 For continuously updated news from The Wall Street Journal, see WSJ.com at  {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://wsj.com}}}\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 20716 4116 5042  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020031208dzc80004f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
