\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar slips after weaker than expected U.S. payrolls.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By John Parry  \par \uc2 590 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Dezember 2003\par 15:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates prices, adds comment, changes byline, changes dateline, previous LONDON)  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Dec 5 (Reuters) - The dollar slipped on Friday on a weaker than expected U.S. payrolls report, but the greenback trimmed losses in a second wave of reaction as analysts noted some positive signs in the closely watched data.  \par \par \uc2 The number of workers on U.S. payrolls outside the farm sector edged up by 57,000 in November, undershooting the market's consensus forecast for creation of 150,000 jobs. The jobless rate edged down to 5.9 percent from 6.0 in October.  \par \par \uc2 "The headline non-farm payrolls number is not great, so it looks like there has been a negative dollar knee-jerk reaction, but actually I think the underlying data are brighter than the headline," said Lara Rhame, senior economist with Brown Brothers Harriman in New York.  \par \par \uc2 The euro surged up to the $1.2130 area immediately after the report but then pared some of these initial gains.  \par \par \uc2 "The headline is certainly negative but it is consistent with employers starting to increase activity," Rhame added.  \par \par \uc2 Early on Friday morning in New York, the euro rose 0.3 percent on the day to $1.2115. The dollar was down 0.2 percent to 108.01 yen. Against the Swiss franc the dollar was at 1.2841 francs, down 0.4 percent on the day. The pound was up 0.2 percent to $1.7245.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar has been beaten to record lows against the euro for five consecutive days and is trading not far from the record trough plumbed on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 Doubts remain about the dollar's ability to benefit from robust U.S. economic data, with many in the market convinced that a recovering economy will do little to help the United States fund its deteriorating current account deficit.  \par \par \uc2 "The dollar has reacted poorly to recent strong U.S. data," said David Mann, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Standard Chartered.  \par \par \uc2 "The bear trend is still intact, although market positioning may be becoming extended," he added.  \par \par \uc2 "If the data is good, bad or indifferent, it really comes down to the market preference and that has been dominated by dollar bears," said David Leaver, senior trader with Gain Capital in Warren, New Jersey.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar has tumbled around 6 percent against the euro in the past month and some 15 percent since the start of the year.  \par \par \uc2 JAPAN KEEPS PRESSURE UP  \par \par \uc2 The greenback held firm around 108 yen, buoyed by speculation \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , seeking to limit damage to its exporters, would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen if the dollar fell much further.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has sold a record 17.8 trillion yen this year in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop any rise in the yen from killing off the country's export-led recovery.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's Nihon Keizai business daily reported the government planned to raise its ceiling on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds by 20 trillion yen to nearly 100 trillion yen when it compiles an extra budget for the fiscal year which ends in March.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Friday Japan's external reserves hit a record $644.569 billion at the end of November, up $18.3 billion from October.  \par \par \uc2 Following the announcement, Japan's top financial diplomat, Zembei Mizoguchi, said there was no change in the government's stance of acting in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets as needed.  \par \par \uc2 "We will closely monitor markets and take measures as appropriate," he told reporters. (Additional reporting by Christina Fincher in London).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031205dzc5003ab\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
