\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar bears pause for breath before US jobs data.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Christina Fincher  \par \uc2 594 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Dezember 2003\par 13:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Dec 5 (Reuters) - The dollar recovered its balance ahead of a key U.S. jobs report on Friday after being beaten to record lows against the euro for five consecutive trading days.  \par \par \uc2 But doubts remained over the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's ability to benefit from strong payrolls figures due at 1330 GMT, with many in the market convinced a recovering economy will do little to help the United States fund its growing current account deficit.  \par \par \uc2 "The dollar has reacted poorly to recent strong U.S. data and we could see a similar reaction today," said David Mann, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Standard Chartered.  \par \par \uc2 "The bear trend is still intact, although market positioning may be becoming extended."  \par \par \uc2 In a subdued trading session, the dollar hugged a tight range around $1.2075 to the euro , up almost a cent from the previous day's record low. The dollar has tumbled around six percent against the euro in the past month and 15 percent since the start of the year.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. payrolls figures - a key indicator of how sustainable the U.S. and global recovery will be - are expected to show 150,000 new jobs were created in November, more than in the previous month. The unemployment rate is expected to remain steady at six percent.  \par \par \uc2 "How the dollar reacts to the payrolls is an open question. It's really 'roll your dice' time today," said Ryan Shea, senior international economist at Bank One.  \par \par \uc2 JAPAN KEEPS PRESSURE UP  \par \par \uc2 The greenback also held a firm footing above 108 yen , buoyed by speculation that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , seeking to limit damage to its exporters, would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen if the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell much further.  \par \par \uc2 Japan has sold a record 17.8 trillion yen this year in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop any rise in the yen from killing off the country's export-led recovery.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar has fallen some nine percent against the yen this year.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's Nihon Keizai business daily reported the Japanese government planned to raise its ceiling on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds by 20 trillion yen to nearly 100 trillion yen when it compiles an extra budget for the fiscal year which ends in March.  \par \par \uc2 A top \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official told Reuters the ministry was considering an ever bigger increase in the ceiling to ensure the cap never became an issue.  \par \par \uc2 RESERVES IN FOCUS  \par \par \uc2 Japan, the world's largest holder of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, said on Friday that its external reserves had risen by more than $18 billion in the month of November to hit a record $644.569 billion.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's top financial diplomat, Zembei Mizoguchi, said there was no change in the government's stance of acting in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets as needed.  \par \par \uc2 "We will closely monitor markets and take measures as appropriate," he told reporters.  \par \par \uc2 The latest Japanese figures mean \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in Asia have risen to a record high of $1.85 trillion, as regional authorities continue to buy dollar assets to prevent local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciating to levels that would hurt exporters.  \par \par \uc2 Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves have risen by $415 billion, or close to 30 percent, since the end of 2002.  \par \par \uc2 In September, the Group of Seven industrialised nations called for greater flexibility in exchange rates - interpreted as a criticism of Asia's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  management.  \par \par \uc2 Just ahead of that call, the International Monetary Fund had warned that Asian reserves were excessive and there was a risk that too quick a diversification could unsettle financial markets.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031205dzc500315\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
