\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar bears hold fire ahead of US jobs report.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Christina Fincher  \par \uc2 463 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Dezember 2003\par 11:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Dec 5 (Reuters) - The dollar recovered its poise ahead of a key U.S. jobs report on Friday, winning some respite after being beaten to record lows against the euro for five consecutive days.  \par \par \uc2 But doubts remained about the dollar's ability to benefit from strong payrolls figures due at 1330 GMT with many in the market convinced that a recovering economy will do little to help the United States fund its deteriorating current account deficit.  \par \par \uc2 "The dollar has reacted poorly to recent strong U.S. data and we could see a similar reaction today," said David Mann, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Standard Chartered.  \par \par \uc2 "The bear trend is still intact, although market positioning may be becoming extended."  \par \par \uc2 The dollar inched up to session peaks around $1.2065 to the euro, up almost a cent from the previous session's record low. The dollar has tumbled around six percent against the euro in the past month and 15 percent since the start of the year.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. figures are expected to show 150,000 new jobs were created in November, more than the 126,000 new positions created the previous month.  \par \par \uc2 "How the dollar reacts to the payrolls is an open question. It's really 'roll your dice' time today," said Ryan Shea, senior international economist at Bank One.  \par \par \uc2 JAPAN KEEPS PRESSURE UP  \par \par \uc2 The greenback also held firm above 108 yen, buoyed by speculation that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , seeking to limit damage to its exporters, would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen if the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell much further.  \par \par \uc2 Japan has sold a record 17.8 trillion yen this year in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop any rise in the yen from killing off the country's export-led recovery.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's Nihon Keizai business daily reported the Japanese government planned to raise its ceiling on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds by 20 trillion yen to nearly 100 trillion yen when it compiles an extra budget for the fiscal year which ends in March.  \par \par \uc2 A top \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official told Reuters the ministry was considering an ever bigger increase in the ceiling to ensure the cap never became an issue.  \par \par \uc2 The MOF said on Friday Japan's external reserves hit a record $644.569 billion at the end of November, up $18.3 billion from October.  \par \par \uc2 Following the announcement, Japan's top financial diplomat, Zembei Mizoguchi, said there was no change in the government's stance of acting in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets as needed.  \par \par \uc2 "We will closely monitor markets and take measures as appropriate," he told reporters.  \par \par \uc2 Asked about any impact on the government's dwindling funds for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Mizoguchi said: "I don't think it is necessary to comment on that. Our stance is unchanged."   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031205dzc5002pn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
