\par\par \b \uc2 Dow Jones Asia-Pacific Top Stories Sked For Dec 5  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,688 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Dezember 2003\par 10:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Following are the top Asia-Pacific stories featured on Dow Jones Newswires as of 0900 GMT, along with items expected later in the day. Please direct any questions or comments to asianewseds@dowjones.com or by phone on 65-6415-4020.  \par \par \uc2 MACRO/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /FIXED INCOME  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Asian governments and steelmakers Friday welcomed President George W. Bush's scrapping of U.S. tariffs on steel imports, a move that signals Washington wants to avoid a damaging global trade war. (WRAP will run around 0915 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: China Friday abandoned its threat to impose retaliatory trade sanctions on the U.S. after President George W. Bush announced a decision to scrap 20 month-old steel tariffs. But the country's major steelmakers remained cautious about the possible further use of anti-trade measures to hinder their exports to the U.S. ("=WRAP: China Welcomes US Decision To Scrap Steel Tariffs" ran at 0806 GMT; second WRAP will run by 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: China's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  plans to inject 130 billion yuan in capital into two of the country's four major state-run commercial banks as part of a new rescue package for the ailing sector, a banking official said Friday. ("=EXCLUSIVE:China/Banks-2: Construction Bk To Withhold Pft" ran at 0513 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =JAPAN WATCH: Japanese Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi's allies in his Liberal Democratic Party fared surprisingly well in national elections last month. The snag, though, is they don't all share his zealous desire to reform the economy. ("=Japan Watch: PM Allies' Gains May Not Help Econ Reform" ran at 0229 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =JAPAN CREDIT: Strong demand from investors and a buoyant stock market mean conditions remain ripe for more large offerings of convertible bonds by Japanese firms in the wake of Sony Corp.'s Y250 billion offering this week. (Japan Credit column will run around 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =OUTLOOK 04/Elections: Political turmoil and social unrest have long made investing in Asia a risky business. But a swath of elections across the region in 2004 has even longtime Asia hands worrying that politics could destabilize economies and markets. From India to South Korea, voters in many of Asia's major economies will chose new presidents or parliaments in the coming year. ("=OUTLOOK 04: Asian Elections Raise Political Temperature" ran at 0824 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves rose $18.3 billion from a month earlier to $644.57 billion in November, Japan's Finance Ministry said Friday, marking a new record high. The data also mark the third straight month of increases, highlighting the massive yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary authorities in November. ("\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Foreign Reserves -2:New High Reflects \b \highlight22\uc2 Interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 " ran at 0010 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's index of leading economic indicators in October stood at 88.9, above the boom-or-bust threshold of 50.0, the government said Friday, suggesting Japan remains on track for a steady economic recovery in the near future. Overall household spending fell 0.8% in October in real terms from a year earlier, indicating that lingering concerns over high unemployment continue to weigh on consumer spending. ("Japan Oct Indicators" ran at 0516 GMT; "Japan Oct Household Spending -2: Job Concerns Still Weigh" ran at 0513 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: The Hong Kong dollar has been acting in a decidedly unpegged-like manner lately: namely it has been strengthening. This newfound strength certainly has proved costly for the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has spent more than US$1 billion since September in repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  designed to staunch the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weakening. ("\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Asia: \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strength Proves Costly For Hong Kong" ran at 0351 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: As the city's largest pro-Beijing party reconsiders its support for the administration of Chief Executive Tung Chee-hwa, a political realignment is taking shape that could leave his government with much less room to maneuver on major policy issues. ("=HK Govt's Policy Options Narrow As Pro-Govt Party Wavers" ran at 0838 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korea's credit bust continues to weigh on domestic demand, hurting business sentiment and undermining confidence in an economy that so far has relied on exports for its recovery. ("=WRAP: Weak Demand Continues To Plague S Korea's Economy" ran at 0641 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BALI, Indonesia: Bank Rakyat Indonesia Friday said recent revelations of a lending fraud at three of its Jakarta branches won't hurt its plan to issue 500 billion rupiah in bonds. ("=EXCLUSIVE: Bank Rakyat/Bond -2: Issue Likely By End Dec" ran at 0120 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: Taiwan's consumer price index fell 0.45% in November, its sixth successive month of decline, the island's official statistics agency said Friday. The wholesale price index rose 1.06% in November versus a 0.06% decline in the previous month. ("DATA SNAP: Taiwan CPI Declines More Than Market Expected" ran at 0833 GMT; DATA VIEW will run around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 KUALA LUMPUR: Malaysia's preliminary October trade data and revised data for August and September released Friday show the country's export manufacturing sector is more robust than earlier supposed. ("=DATA VIEW: Malaysia Trade Data Show Robust Exports, Mfg" ran at 0536 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MANILA: Food prices unexpectedly propelled Philippine inflation higher in November, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  insists consumer prices remain benign, posing no immediate threat to its neutral monetary policy. ("=WRAP: Philippine Inflation Accelerates In November" ran at 0817 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: Proposed amendments to New Zealand's labor laws will boost the power of trade unions and disadvantage the majority of workers who aren't union members, according to business critics of the legislation. ("NZ Labor Law Will Give Unions More Power, Critics Say" ran at 2251 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: International Commercial Bank of China, a unit of Taiwan's Mega Financial Holding, said Friday it plans to do more debt issuance in Australia next year to fund its Australian \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  as it talks with potential merger partners back home. ("=INTERVIEW: Taiwan ICBC Plans More A$ Debt, Talks Mergers" ran at 0052 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  MARKET ROUNDUP: The dollar traded narrowly against the yen and the euro in Asia Friday, with many players content to sit out the session ahead of highly anticipated U.S. jobs data due later in the day. ("Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 : Dollar Steady Vs Majors Ahead Of US Jobs Data" ran at 0539 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 EQUITIES  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: Formosa Petrochemical Corp.'s initial public offering was nearly two times subscribed, ensuring the success of the NT$7.72 billion IPO for Taiwan's only privately owned oil refiner. Subscription for the retail portion of the share offer totaled 259.1 million shares, 92% higher than the 134.7 million shares offered for this segment. ("Taiwan Formosa Petrochemical Shr Offer 92% Oversubscribed" ran at 0312 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Companies offering investors exposure to China's rapid economic growth are sparking an IPO frenzy in Hong Kong, as the stock market's H-share index scales six-year highs. ("=China Companies Cash In On IPO Appetite As H Shares Soar" ran at 0736 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Australia's APN News & Media Ltd. said Friday that it remains on track to achieve a net profit of at least A$100 million for 2003. ("Australia's APN News -2: Benchmarks Vs Oct Mkt Consensus" ran at 0116 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Shares in IOOF Holdings debuted on the Australian Stock Exchange Friday at A$3.80 each, a 21% premium to the A$3.15 price set under the mid-sized fund manager's share offer facility. ("Australia's IOOF -2: Touched A$3.82 High" ran at 0108 GMT; "=INTERVIEW: Australia IOOF Chmn Confirms To Retire End-04" ran at 0301  \par \par \uc2 =TECH STOCK FOCUS: Pioneer Corp. helped blaze the trail in the DVD recorder and flat-screen TV business, but with bigger rivals scrambling to catch up, the Japanese company's lofty share price looks threatened. ("=Tech Stock Focus:Pioneer Blazed Trail, Rivals On Heels" ran at 0002 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: Despite its major global thrust into the digital world, Eastman Kodak intends to remain aggressive in developing its traditional film photography business in China, its second-largest market in terms of sales. ("=INTERVIEW: Kodak Eyes Traditional, Digital Mkts In China" ran at 0528 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: The South Korean government has nominated 170 local and overseas institutional investors as participants in the auction to buy the government's 9.1% stake in Kookmin Bank. ("S Korea Names 170 Candidates To Bid For Kookmin Bk Shrs" ran at 0226 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI: Hongkong & Shanghai Banking Corp.'s move to purchase a stake in India's UTI Bank Ltd. (532215.BY) earlier this week will likely spur rival foreign banks to speed up plans to expand their \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in India via the acquisition route. ("=HSBC's Stake Purchase Of UTI Bk May Spur More Such Deals," may run after 0900 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Panva Gas has increased the size of its share placement to US$80 million from US$60 million, people involved in the deal said. The placement price was fixed at HK$4 per share, at the low end of an indicative price range of HK$4 to HK$4.30. ("HK Panva Gas Ups Placement Size To US$80M From US$60M" ran at 0127 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korean car maker Kia Motors said it will produce its mini van model Carnival in its Chinese factory from 2004, earlier than planned, to meet higher demand for recreational vehicles in China. ("S Korea Kia To Make Carnival Mini Van In China From '04" ran at 0344 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 ENERGY/COMMODITIES  \par \par \uc2 =OUTLOOK 04/Gold: Gold could face pressure in 2004 thanks to a fall-off in the amount of producer dehedging, analysts say. ("=OUTLOOK 04: Lack Of "Dehedging" Could Hurt Gold In 2004" ran at 0657 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 CANBERRA: The issuing of export licenses for barley and canola to private operators is destroying an export monopoly that previously dominated the trade in Western Australia, said Andy Crane, general manager of monopoly operator Grain Pool. ("West Australia Export Barley Monopoly Breaking Down" ran at 0430 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 62550  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031205dzc5000co\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
