\par\par \b \uc2 Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 : Dollar Steady Vs Majors Ahead Of US Jobs Data  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Jonathan Soble  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 664 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Dezember 2003\par 06:39\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The dollar traded narrowly against the yen and the euro in Asia Friday, with many players content to sit out the session ahead of highly anticipated U.S. jobs data due later in the day.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets settled down after choppy trading Thursday, which saw the euro rattled by the expiry of a major options barrier and by a report hinting at possible European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the single unit's rise.  \par \par \uc2 "Buyers bought, sellers sold, and now we're back in the middle," said the head dealer at a European brokerage in Tokyo. "People are waiting for the U.S. data, although by now it's clear that even strong figures aren't helping the dollar."  \par \par \uc2 Friday's November employment report is expected to show another healthy rise in payrolls, despite higher-than-expected weekly U.S. jobless claims released Thursday. Economists estimate U.S. firms hired about 150,000 new workers last month, and some forecasts run as high as 200,000.  \par \par \uc2 But given the dollar's muted response to good economic news out of the U.S. in recent weeks, few traders or analysts expect the data to give the greenback much of a boost.  \par \par \uc2 "The market's focus is changing," said Masamichi Koike, head spot dealer at Sumitomo Mitsui Bank. "People are taking a longer-term view and are worrying that the U.S. won't be able to finance its deficits."  \par \par \uc2 Koike predicted the dollar would stay below Y109 "even if the jobs data are strong."  \par \par \uc2 At 0515 GMT, the dollar was quoted at Y108.31, just above Y108.27 late Thursday in New York. The dollar was at Y108.28 late Thursday in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 The euro was at $1.2081, slightly higher than $1.2073 late Thursday in New York. The euro was at $1.2061 in Tokyo Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 Against the yen, the euro was quoted at Y130.88, up from Y130.78 in New York late Thursday. The euro was at Y130.61 in Tokyo late Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 Sterling was at $1.7216, steady from $1.7217 in New York and lower than $1.7243 in Tokyo Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 The yen also brushed off strong economic data Friday. Japan's index of leading economic indicators stood at 88.9 for October, above the boom-or-bust threshold of 50.0 for the sixth straight month and better than economists' average forecast for a reading of 77.8.  \par \par \uc2 A level above 50 points to economic expansion over the short term.  \par \par \uc2 Expectations that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities are poised to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to keep the dollar above Y108 - as they are suspected to have done earlier this week - have kept traders from selling dollars for yen aggressively.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese exporters - among the biggest dollar sellers - are now shifting their focus to the euro, selling the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for yen on rallies, said Daiwa Securities SMBC manager Mitsuo Imaizumi.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's vice finance minister for international affairs, Zembei Mizoguchi, expressed his government's determination to curb excessive yen strength Friday. Repeating the standard Finance Ministry line, he said Tokyo was watching the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market closely and was ready to take action to tame volatility if needed.  \par \par \uc2 Fearing a sharp yen rise could snuff out Japan's economic recovery by hurting exports, the ministry sold Y17.8 trillion in market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the first 11 months of the year.  \par \par \uc2 -By Jonathan Soble, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; jonathan.soble@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by John Gibson and Alastair Gale  \par \par \uc2 The chart of late Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates that accompanied Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been replaced with an enhanced \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  fixture that is updated hourly.  \par \par \uc2 Subscribers can find this table:  \par \par \uc2 -On Telerate pages 4046 and 1087.  \par \par \uc2 -On Dow Jones Newswires by searching the code N/IFX  \par \par \uc2 -On Reuters by entering FRX or INT  \par \par \uc2 -On Bloomberg by entering NI FRX  \par \par \uc2 1074  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031205dzc500053\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
