\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar up from record low vs euro - focus on jobs.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Daniel Bases  \par \uc2 725 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Dezember 2003\par 22:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Dec 4 (Reuters) - The dollar rebounded from a fifth straight record low versus the euro on Thursday as investors grew transfixed on Friday's U.S. jobs report and shrugged off the scrapping of United States steel tariffs.  \par \par \uc2 Rising expectations for good news on the employment front, fueled by a wave of strong economic reports that prompted economists to raise November job-creation forecasts, gave investors reason to limit their dollar sales.  \par \par \uc2 Forewarned the controversial tariffs would be lifted, the news was largely priced into the market and produced only a fleeting upside benefit to the dollar, analysts said.  \par \par \uc2 "Even though we have seen the White House lift the steel tariffs, I think the perception in the market is that the Bush administration remains amenable to an orderly dollar decline," said Alex Beuzelin, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Ruesch International in Washington  \par \par \uc2 The brief respite from dollar selling occurred after the Bush administration said the tariffs had achieved their purpose of helping domestic producers and could now be lifted.  \par \par \uc2 But caution pervaded the marketplace ahead of Friday's jobs data - expected to show 150,000 new non-farm jobs and an unchanged unemployment rate of 6.0 percent - leaving the dollar little changed on the day.  \par \par \uc2 A strong report could spark a big round of profit-taking against the dollar's rivals, who have benefited from a myriad of factors such as 45-year low U.S. benchmark interest rates, an inability to rally on good economic data, a record current account deficit, and concerns over terrorism and Iraq.  \par \par \uc2 "We've seen such a sharp move down by the dollar that we do need to have somewhat of a correction. I think the market would view a strong payrolls number as an excuse to initiate this correction, but I don't think it will change the overall bearish sentiment that pervades the greenback," said Beuzelin.  \par \par \uc2 The euro climbed to a record $1.2155 according to Reuters data, but then slipped to $1.2084, a loss of 0.23 percent on the day.  \par \par \uc2 The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  held its minimum bid rate steady at 2 percent as expected, and the Bank of England left its key interest rate steady at 3.75 percent. A quarter-percentage-point rise in UK rates is expected early in 2004.  \par \par \uc2 The pound was down 0.28 percent on the day at $1.7224.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar nudged ahead versus the Japanese yen to 108.20 yen according to Reuters data. Earlier, the dollar fell below 108 yen, raising expectations the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Traders expect the BOJ to start buying dollars if the the greenback falls below the 107.50 yen area.  \par \par \uc2 Against the Swiss franc the dollar was at 1.2886 francs, up 0.17 percent on the day.  \par \par \uc2 The greenback reached a high of C$1.3145 against the Canadian dollar, up more than 1 percent on the day.  \par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar hit a six-year high of US$0.7381, according to Reuters data, but has since slipped back to US$0.7357, a loss of 0.07 percent on the day.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier, the dollar fell after the weekly jobless claims report showed claims for new benefits rose to 365,000 in the week to Nov. 29, above economists' forecasts for 350,000.  \par \par \uc2 "I think that (the dollar's decline after the jobless claims report) is just underscoring the really bearish dollar tone in the environment. There is this asymmetric response that positive news isn't helping the dollar but negative news can push it lower," said Lauren Germain, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Banc of America Securities in New York.  \par \par \uc2 HOW MUCH TOLERANCE FOR A STRONGER EURO?  \par \par \uc2 Worries that euro zone policy-makers may not put up with a strong euro emerged in Asia after a report in the British Daily Telegraph, which the European Commission dismissed, said the EU was looking at controls to stop a surge in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Analysts were skeptical Brussels would impose the 1970s-style \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls suggested in the report but said the story prompted profit-taking on euro positions built up during the single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent rally. (Additional reporting by Gertrude Chavez and John Parry in New York and Carolyn Cohn in London).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031204dzc4003yb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
