\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar rebounds on steel after record low vs euro.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By John Parry  \par \uc2 728 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Dezember 2003\par 17:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Dec 4 (Reuters) - The dollar rebounded on traders' hopes that controversial U.S. steel tariffs would be scrapped on Thursday, after it had plumbed a record low against the euro for the fifth straight day.  \par \par \uc2 The greenback fell to record troughs against the euro as traders digested a weekly report on U.S. jobless claims that hinted the labor market might be less robust than expected.  \par \par \uc2 But the dollar turned higher on the day after President George W. Bush said the administration would shortly announce his decision on steel tariffs, remarking that "the American economy is better off with a world that trades freely."  \par \par \uc2 "The dollar got a big lift from news headlines that President Bush may lift steel tariffs. This had a huge impact. There was also a bit of stop-loss selling on the euro," said Philip Capone, a trader at Fortis Bank in New York.  \par \par \uc2 Midmorning on Thursday in New York, the euro was down 0.4 percent to $1.2062, well below its earlier record highs around $1.2155, according to Reuters data.  \par \par \uc2 Against the yen, the dollar was nearly flat on the day at 108.23 yen, near an area where traders become more wary of the possibility that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  might undertake yen-weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Against the Swiss franc the dollar was at 1.2924 francs, up 0.5 percent on the day.  \par \par \uc2 The pound was down 0.4 percent on the day at $1.7196.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar climbed about 1 percent against the Canadian dollar, rising to session highs around C$1.3133.  \par \par \uc2 Among U.S. economic reports, weekly jobless claims rose to 365,000 in the week to Nov. 29, overshooting economists' consensus forecasts for 350,000 new filings.  \par \par \uc2 Friday's U.S. employment report for November is now the dollar's main data focus, analysts said. A Reuters poll shows a median forecast for 150,000 jobs added to nonfarm payrolls.  \par \par \uc2 "I think that (the dollar's decline after the jobless claims report) is just underscoring the really bearish dollar tone in the environment. There is this asymmetric response that positive news isn't helping the dollar but negative news can push it lower," said Lauren Germain, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Banc of America Securities in New York.  \par \par \uc2 "I don't think the number itself was that bad, but just being higher than expectations was all that was needed," Germain added.  \par \par \uc2 In Europe, the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  held its minimum bid rate steady at 2 percent as expected, and the Bank of England left its key interest rate steady at 3.75 percent. A quarter percentage point rise in U.K. rates is expected early in 2004.  \par \par \uc2 HOW MUCH TOLERANCE FOR A STRONGER EURO?  \par \par \uc2 Worries that euro zone policymakers may not put up with a strong euro emerged in Asia after a report in the British Daily Telegraph, which the European Commission dismissed, said the EU was looking at controls to stop a surge in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Analysts were skeptical Brussels would impose the 1970s-style \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls suggested in the report but said the story prompted profit-taking on euro positions built up during the single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent rally.  \par \par \uc2 "The latest move in the euro has really been a euro move because euro/yen is higher as well, which probably doesn't please European officials.... I don't think they are prepared to do anything about it. It does feel like a technical move," said Marcel Kasumovich, head of G10 \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy with Merrill Lynch in New York.  \par \par \uc2 He cited barrier options at $1.2050 and near $1.22 for the euro. Barrier options, which give holders the right to trade a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a set price for a specified time, become worthless if the market hits an agreed-upon price, so holders may buy or sell a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep their option from being triggered.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier, the dollar hit two-week lows against the yen around 107.98 due to persistent worries about the U.S. current account deficit and geopolitical tensions.  \par \par \uc2 Also earlier, the Australian dollar climbed to fresh six-year highs against the U.S. dollar around US$0.7381, according to Reuters data. (Additional reporting by Gertrude Chavez in New York and Carolyn Cohn in London).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031204dzc40037g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
