\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar at record low vs euro despite bullish data.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Daniel Bases  \par \uc2 787 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Dezember 2003\par 23:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Dec 3 (Reuters) - The dollar slumped to a record low against the euro for a fourth straight day on Wednesday as investors focus on the dominant sell-off trend and not on the strong U.S. data that herald an economy regaining its stride.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar sell-off has for weeks overwhelmed a string of strong economic data, including Wednesday's government report showing U.S. workers operated more efficiently in the third quarter than at any time in the last 20 years.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar, after reaching significant lows against its major rivals in the last week, hardly stirred despite the strong productivity data. Likewise, reports showing disappointing growth in the vast U.S. services sector last month and a dip in U.S. mortgage applications last week were no help, but not seen as major hindrances either.  \par \par \uc2 "Despite all this data, there is a counterbalance of terrorism, and maybe the economic data overextending itself in that it is rebounding from depressed levels," said Joe Francomano, vice president of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Erste Bank in New York.  \par \par \uc2 "At least in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets they have definitely discounted the data as having a greater impact on the economy when compared to terrorism and the growing threat of an economic drain from Iraq," he added.  \par \par \uc2 Additionally, the inability of the U.S. financial markets to attract enough capital to offset the U.S. current account deficit given benchmark interest rates are at a 45-year low of 1 percent is another reason for looking for investment growth elsewhere.  \par \par \uc2 Signs that Germany was not worried by the euro's persistent climb gave added encouragement to euro bulls who bid the euro up to a record high $1.2128 according to Reuters data. It then retreated to $1.2103, still a small gain of 0.22 percent on the day.  \par \par \uc2 The euro is now trading about 3-1/2 cents higher than its launch level where the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  first traded around $1.1747 in January 1999. It reached a low near $0.8225 in October 2000.  \par \par \uc2 "The dollar is looking more at the structural imbalances in the United States rather than economic data," said Michael Cairns, trading manager with \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Solutions in Ridgewood, New Jersey.  \par \par \uc2 "Dollar weakness is a massive underlying trend at the minute," he added. The next resistance levels for the euro lie at $1.2149, then $1.2168, above which it could rise as high as $1.2400, Cairns added.  \par \par \uc2 Fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hold the yen down against the dollar, kept the greenback from sliding too much against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The dollar traded near its two-week low around 108.15 yen, off roughly a third of a percent on the day according to Reuters data.  \par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar leapt to a new six-year high above US$0.7370 after the Reserve Bank of Australia raised rates for a second straight month on Wednesday to 5.25 percent. That helped bring its gains against the greenback to more than 31 percent in 2003.  \par \par \uc2 The New Zealand dollar fell after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left interest rates unchanged at 5.0 percent on Wednesday. After hitting a six-year high of US$0.6507, the kiwi dropped to the US$0.6450 area.  \par \par \uc2 Sterling hit a fresh five-year high for the sixth straight session, touching $1.7317, according to Reuters data.  \par \par \uc2 ECONOMIC UPSWING  \par \par \uc2 Early in the U.S. session, the dollar made only muted gains against the euro in response to third-quarter U.S. productivity data that was revised up to show growth of 9.4 percent.  \par \par \uc2 It traded skittishly after the Institute for Supply Management non-manufacturing index, a measure of the U.S. service sector economy, came in at 60.1 in November, below economists' consensus forecasts for a reading of 64.3 and down from 64.7 in October.  \par \par \uc2 The market's main focus remains the U.S. November non-farm employment report due on Friday, analysts said. Economists' consensus forecasts are for a rise of 150,000 in payrolls, according to a Reuters poll on Wednesday. The original estimate was for a gain of 135,000 jobs.  \par \par \uc2 "We have a low degree of confidence among investors that strong U.S. economic growth is going to translate to (a) stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  simply because the stronger data has not produced yield advantages and/or significant equity market outperformance in the U.S," said Sean Callow, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at IDEAglobal in New York. (Additional reporting by Manuela Badawy, Gertrude Chavez and John Parry in New York and Christina Fincher and Carolyn Cohn in London).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031203dzc30052s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
