\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW-Ex-MOF Utsumi sees need for more yen  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 .   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Natsuko Waki   \par \uc2 694 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Dezember 2003\par 15:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Dec 3 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's repeated yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year means the effect might be fading but even greater action may be needed to underpin the economy's recovery, a former top Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policymaker said on Wednesday.   \par \par \uc2 Makoto Utsumi, vice minister of finance for international affairs between 1989-1991 and now a director at Japan's fourth biggest bank UFJ Holdings (8307.T), also said he expected dollar/yen to trade in a 10-yen range centring on 115 yen towards the end of 2004.   \par \par \uc2 "Basically, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is like antibiotics. Excessive use means the effect would fade. I feel \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  too much. However, the dollar/yen is below 110 yen at this critical time when the Japanese economy is beginning to recover, so they might duly need to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  further," Utsumi told Reuters on the sidelines of the European Finance Convention in London.   \par \par \uc2 "It depends on the situation but it's possible the amount (of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) will go up. Towards the end of next year I expect dollar/yen to trade in a range centring on 115 yen, plus/minus five yen," added Utsumi, who is also a president at research institute Japan Center For International Finance.   \par \par \uc2 Japan spent 1.5996 trillion yen ($14.75 billion) in yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in November alone to curb export-damaging strength of the yen. It rose to a three-year high against the dollar of about 107.50 last month.   \par \par \uc2 UGLY SISTERS   \par \par \uc2 The dollar has been under heavy downward pressure in the past weeks as concerns about the U.S. current account deficit and fears of intensifying trade disputes outweighed evidence that the world's biggest economy is recovering strongly.   \par \par \uc2 "The market has this 'un-beauty contest' - 'which \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  zone is the weakest?' The market is ignoring the fact the U.S. economy is recovering, instead focusing on the weak factors of the U.S. economy which include the current account deficit and questions over its ability to finance the deficit," Utsumi said.   \par \par \uc2 "I think (dollar selling) has gone too far and there should be a rebound at some point. When the U.S. economy improves it can attract enough inflows."   \par \par \uc2 The greenback fell to a record low against the euro on Wednesday, hitting $1.2128 , according to Reuters data, to bring its losses to more than 15 percent this year.   \par \par \uc2 Despite across-the-board dollar selling, the yen has kept a low profile, holding below 108.00 in the past few weeks, after suspected large-scale Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in mid-November when the yen rose to the three-year peak.   \par \par \uc2 Calculated from both Bank of Japan and \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  data, Japanese authorities have spent around 17.8 trillion yen in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  so far this year.   \par \par \uc2 The heavy selling by Japanese authorities reflects their fear that a strong yen could hurt exports, hindering the economy's tentative recovery and weakening share prices - key to the health of the banking system.   \par \par \uc2 The authorities are also keen to dispel speculation that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  might be running out of funds for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 The government borrows yen from the markets by issuing financing bills to fund yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 Analysts say as of mid-November the government had only two to six trillion yen before it hits a borrowing limit. Parliament needs to approve an increase in the borrowing limit and that could take months.   \par \par \uc2 To help the government get around the ceiling in the short-term, the MOF could sell some of its huge holdings of U.S. Treasury bills and notes - acquired from years of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  - to the BOJ via a repurchase agreement and receive the initial payment in yen.   \par \par \uc2 That would effectively look like a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 , buying some time for the MOF if the borrowing ceiling became a serious issue.   \par \par \uc2 Utsumi said fund shortage has never been a problem and it was unlikely to discourage the authorities from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 "The BOJ can just sign a swap deal. It has happened in the past and it's not abnormal at all. They will do it if necessary," he said.    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031203dzc3002zs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
