\par\par \b \uc2 OECD: Japan Should Be Selective In Public Funds For Banks  \b0\par\par\uc2 609 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Dezember 2003\par 11:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan needs to be selective and strict in its use of public funds to shore up banks' capital, the Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development said in an annual survey released Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 "Injections of public funds should be made selectively, subject to strict conditions related to the implementation of restructuring programs," the OECD said in its December 2003 Economic Survey on Japan. "In addition properly carrying out due diligence is essential to ensure the accountability of the new management at restructured banks."  \par \par \uc2 The Paris-based group's Economic and Development Review Committee approved the survey on Nov. 19.  \par \par \uc2 Ten days later, the Japanese government declared insolvent and nationalized regional lender Ashikaga Bank.  \par \par \uc2 In its report, the OECD said Japan's upturn appears to be continuing, with output growth likely to exceed 2.5% in 2003, supported by buoyant exports, particularly to China.  \par \par \uc2 Japan has made progress in tackling banking-sector reform and corporate restructuring has advanced, the agency said, but it cited "considerable uncertainty regarding Japan's economic outlook," which depends on further reforms and effective monetary policy.  \par \par \uc2 Last week, the OECD predicted Japan's gross domestic product would grow 1.8% a year in 2004 and 2005. That would mark a slowdown from projected 2.7% growth this year, but would still beat the average 1% growth over the past decade.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    BOJ Needs A Deflation "Exit Strategy" \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Japan's monetary policy should continue to give priority to ending deflation, which has a negative effect on growth prospects, the OECD said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Monetary policy, while "very expansionary, has proven incapable of bringing an end to the deflation that has persisted since the mid-1990's," the OECD said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The agency welcomed the BOJ's recent clarification of its commitment to continue its quantitative easing policy, which keeps short-term interest rates at virtually zero and keeps the banking system flush with liquidity. It recommended the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strengthen the effectiveness of its easing by broadening the range of assets it purchases.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Further ahead, the OECD said monetary policy should also include a "coherent exit strategy" to cope with the end of deflation.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's commitment to continue large purchases of government bonds has attracted private-sector purchases in this market," the OECD said. "The resulting run-up in prices has created the risk of latent losses for financial institutions once deflation ends and the yield curve becomes steeper."  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, which totaled a record Y17.8 trillion in the first 11 months of 2003, provide an opportunity to limit the impact of the easing policy on the JGB market, the OECD said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    End Of Deflation Would Exacerbate Fiscal Woes \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Low interest rates on government bonds reflect both investors' risk aversion and deflationary expectations, and have also limited the impact of Japan's burgeoning public debt, the OECD report said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With a deficit estimated at nearly 8% of gross domestic product in 2003, public debt is likely to exceed 150% of GDP, but interest payments in absolute terms are actually lower than a decade ago.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If interest rates had remained at their level of the early 1990's, the OECD estimated that government interest payments would be higher by nearly 5% of GDP.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The transition to positive rates of inflation, even if there is no rise in the real interest rate, will boost interest payments on public debt over time, creating a risk of finance strains," the OECD said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Lisa Twaronite, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; lisa.twaronite@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Hugh Lawson  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031202dzc2000h5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
