\par\par \b \uc2 OUTLOOK 04: Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  To Rise As US Econ Fires Exports  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Benjamin Pedley  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 1,285 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Dezember 2003\par 01:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Asian central banks, which kept their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  weak for much of the past year, will likely let them rise in 2004 as a U.S.-led global recovery helps the region's economic growth accelerate.  \par \par \uc2 As Asia bounces back strongly from the economic ravages of SARS and the Iraq war, with the U.S. pulling in greater amounts of the region's exports, Asian monetary authorities will have less rationale for weakening their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts said.  \par \par \uc2 On the face of it, the news out of the U.S. looks dollar-positive - the world's biggest economy sizzled with the fastest growth in nearly 20 years in the third quarter, and the labor market is finally recovering. But the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains plagued by the economy's wide current-account and budget gaps.  \par \par \uc2 Such structural problems for the dollar provide added room for \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of export-reliant economies including Singapore, Taiwan, and to a lesser extent Thailand and South Korea, to appreciate gradually.  \par \par \uc2 "Investors' judgment on the dollar in 2004 is unlikely to be favorable," said Lindsay Coburn, head of G7 research at IDEA. "Equity valuations in the U.S. appear rich despite confidence in the recovery, and prices of corporate paper and Treasury debt will be undermined by rising real interest rates that the recovery will foster."  \par \par \uc2 In the past few months, other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have underperformed the yen against the dollar, so most analysts said they now provide better investment opportunities as they play catch-up with the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Ctrl Bks To Tolerate \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Rise As Econs Improve  \par \par \uc2 Tokyo sold more yen for dollars in the first seven months of 2003 than in any previous full year, in a desperate attempt to keep a strong local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from derailing an export-reliant recovery. But the government has let the yen rise 5% against the dollar since finance ministers from the Group of Seven industrial nations agreed Sept. 20 to a U.S. call for greater \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility.  \par \par \uc2 Though the G7's message was aimed mainly at China's fixed-rate regime, U.S. and European officials have also criticized \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by other Asian central banks, especially \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , to rein in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Since the G7 meeting, the yen has outstripped other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Japanese authorities - who signed onto the U.S.-drafted communique as Asia's only G7 member - have been less aggressive than other Asian countries in fighting the tide of a falling dollar. After defending the U.S. unit at Y115 for much of the year, Tokyo has let it slide to a string of three-year lows, skidding as far as November's Y107.57.  \par \par \uc2 Partly because other Asian central banks have been more actively selling their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Taiwanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is flat over the same period, that of Thailand is up just 0.9% and that of Singapore is up 1.2%. In Korea, a combination of aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and some relatively disappointing economic data has seen the won fall 1.3% since the September G7.  \par \par \uc2 But this relative weakness of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in rebounding Asia likely will change as local economies pick up.  \par \par \uc2 IDEA forecasts a 5% rise in the New Taiwan dollar by the end of the first quarter, pushing the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down to NT$32.50. It projects a 5.6% rise in the South Korean won, a 3.7% rise in the Thai baht and forecasts the Singapore dollar to be barely changed.  \par \par \uc2 The big exception, however, is the Philippine peso, which was hurt for much of 2003 by political turmoil, including an abortive army mutiny and an attempt to suspend the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor. IDEA tips further losses next year, with the dollar rising to a record high PHP57.50.  \par \par \uc2 Elsewhere in Asia, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength is likely to accompany accelerating economic growth.  \par \par \uc2 Take the case of Taiwan, which reported gross domestic product expanded 4.2% in the third quarter from a year earlier after contracting 0.1% in the second quarter.  \par \par \uc2 "The current account will continue to benefit from the strong export performance, providing strong support for the local dollar," said DBS Bank economists Daniel Chan and Grace Cheung. "As domestic activity improves and deflation pressure eases, we expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to accept a gradual appreciation of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."  \par \par \uc2 To be sure, tensions with China are always a risk to the Taiwan dollar, but the voting public appear comfortable with the currently ambiguous status quo regarding the island's sovereignty.  \par \par \uc2 China regards Taiwan as a renegade province, but Taiwanese President Chen Shui-bian has made increasingly provocative vote-getting moves in a tough re-election campaign ahead of a poll in March. He threatens to hold a referendum on sovereignty for the island on election day.  \par \par \uc2 In Korea the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , like other authorities in Asia, worries that unbridled \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength against the dollar will derail economic recovery from this year's SARS outbreak and the war in Iraq. Korea, still trying to overcome the effects of the Sept. 11, 2001 attacks, fell into recession in the first half of 2003 for the first time since the 1997-98 Asia financial crisis.  \par \par \uc2 Though Korean exports have been strong - they posted on-year gains of 23% and 26% in September and October, respectively - domestic demand is weak, crimping the potential upside for the won and making it a less attractive proposition than other regional units, said some analysts.  \par \par \uc2 Geopolitics also detracts from the won's allure as a vehicle to capitalize on the pickup in global demand.  \par \par \uc2 "There's always still the unfavorable background of the North Korean nuclear standoff, which carries an escalation risk the market hasn't fully discounted," said Jan Lambregts, strategist at Rabobank.  \par \par \uc2 Politics will affect some exchange rates in Southeast Asia next year. "The 2004 presidential elections in both Indonesia and the Philippines bring along plenty of risks to the perceived political stability of these countries," said Lambregts.  \par \par \uc2 Asian Units May Guide Global \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 In Thailand, the authorities are worried about the contractionary effects of a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but real-money flows likely will ensure the baht appreciates into 2004.  \par \par \uc2 Indeed, with Bangkok expecting 2003 economic growth to top 6% aided by both domestic consumption and external demand, it is little surprise that analysts expect more foreign capital will flow into local stocks.  \par \par \uc2 While domestic economic strength in Malaysia, the Philippines and Indonesia has been modest at best, "Thailand has been an absolute star performer in 2003," said Lambregts. "Though recent signs suggest growth may ease some into 2004, the overall performance of the Thai economy should remain strong, which in turn should see ongoing equity inflows and an appreciating Thai baht."  \par \par \uc2 An export-led recovery in an Asia economy is probably best illustrated by gross domestic product numbers out of Singapore. The island city-state's economy grew 1.7% on year in the second quarter, or an annualized on-quarter rate of 17%. That followed a 3.8% on-year contraction in the second quarter, when SARS hammered travel and trade.  \par \par \uc2 Appreciation of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of export-reliant economies is consistent with previous global recoveries. Export-leveraged \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are typically the second tier to benefit after a rise in commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which have been in a bull trend now for many months.  \par \par \uc2 Just as a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the Australian dollar rose against the Singapore dollar in the initial recovery stages after the Asian crisis and 9/11 before falling back, a number of these Asian units could assume a leadership role in 2004.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Benjamin Pedley, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4044; ben.pedleydowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031202dzc20000e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
