\par\par \b \uc2 ODJ OUTLOOK 04: Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  To Rise As US Econ Fires Exports  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,173 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Dezember 2003\par 16:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones Commodities Service\par \uc2 DJCS\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par (Repeating from Monday) \par  \par    By Benjamin Pedley \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \par  \par   SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Asian central banks, which kept their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  weak \par for much of the past year, will likely let them rise in 2004 as a U.S.-led \par global recovery helps the region's economic growth accelerate. \par  \par   As Asia bounces back strongly from the economic ravages of SARS and the Iraq \par war, with the U.S. pulling in greater amounts of the region's exports, Asian \par monetary authorities will have less rationale for weakening their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , \par analysts said. \par  \par   On the face of it, the news out of the U.S. looks dollar-positive - the \par world's biggest economy sizzled with the fastest growth in nearly 20 years in \par the third quarter, and the labor market is finally recovering. But the \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  remains plagued by the economy's wide current-account and budget \par gaps. \par  \par   Such structural problems for the dollar provide added room for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of \par export-reliant economies including Singapore, Taiwan, and to a lesser extent \par Thailand and South Korea, to appreciate gradually. \par  \par   "Investors' judgment on the dollar in 2004 is unlikely to be favorable," \par said Lindsay Coburn, head of G7 research at IDEA. "Equity valuations in the \par U.S. appear rich despite confidence in the recovery, and prices of corporate \par paper and Treasury debt will be undermined by rising real interest rates that \par the recovery will foster." \par  \par   In the past few months, other Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have underperformed the yen \par against the dollar, so most analysts said they now provide better investment \par opportunities as they play catch-up with the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \par  \par  \par  \par   Ctrl Bks To Tolerate \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Rise As Econs Improve \par  \par  \par  \par   Tokyo sold more yen for dollars in the first seven months of 2003 than in \par any previous full year, in a desperate attempt to keep a strong local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par from derailing an export-reliant recovery. But the government has let the yen \par rise 5% against the dollar since finance ministers from the Group of Seven \par industrial nations agreed Sept. 20 to a U.S. call for greater \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par flexibility. \par  \par   Though the G7's message was aimed mainly at China's fixed-rate regime, U.S. \par and European officials have also criticized \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by other Asian \par central banks, especially \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , to rein in their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \par  \par   Since the G7 meeting, the yen has outstripped other Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \par Japanese authorities - who signed onto the U.S.-drafted communique as Asia's \par only G7 member - have been less aggressive than other Asian countries in \par fighting the tide of a falling dollar. After defending the U.S. unit at Y115 \par for much of the year, Tokyo has let it slide to a string of three-year lows, \par skidding as far as November's Y107.57. \par  \par   Partly because other Asian central banks have been more actively selling \par their own \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the Taiwanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is flat over the same period, \par that of Thailand is up just 0.9% and that of Singapore is up 1.2%. In Korea, a \par combination of aggressive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and some relatively disappointing \par economic data has seen the won fall 1.3% since the September G7. \par  \par   But this relative weakness of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in rebounding Asia likely will \par change as local economies pick up. \par  \par   IDEA forecasts a 5% rise in the New Taiwan dollar by the end of the first \par quarter, pushing the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  down to NT$32.50. It projects a 5.6% rise \par in the South Korean won, a 3.7% rise in the Thai baht and forecasts the \par Singapore dollar to be barely changed. \par  \par   The big exception, however, is the Philippine peso, which was hurt for much \par of 2003 by political turmoil, including an abortive army mutiny and an attempt \par to suspend the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governor. IDEA tips further losses next year, with \par the dollar rising to a record high PHP57.50. \par  \par   Elsewhere in Asia, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strength is likely to accompany accelerating \par economic growth. \par  \par   Take the case of Taiwan, which reported gross domestic product expanded 4.2% \par in the third quarter from a year earlier after contracting 0.1% in the second \par quarter. \par  \par   "The current account will continue to benefit from the strong export \par performance, providing strong support for the local dollar," said DBS Bank \par economists Daniel Chan and Grace Cheung. "As domestic activity improves and \par deflation pressure eases, we expect the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to accept a gradual \par appreciation of the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ." \par  \par   To be sure, tensions with China are always a risk to the Taiwan dollar, but \par the voting public appear comfortable with the currently ambiguous status quo \par regarding the island's sovereignty. \par  \par   China regards Taiwan as a renegade province, but Taiwanese President Chen \par Shui-bian has made increasingly provocative vote-getting moves in a tough re- \par election campaign ahead of a poll in March. He threatens to hold a referendum \par on sovereignty for the island on election day. \par  \par   In Korea the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , like other authorities in Asia, worries that \par unbridled \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strength against the dollar will derail economic recovery \par from this year's SARS outbreak and the war in Iraq. Korea, still trying to \par overcome the effects of the Sept. 11, 2001 attacks, fell into recession in the \par first half of 2003 for the first time since the 1997-98 Asia financial crisis. \par  \par   Though Korean exports have been strong - they posted on-year gains of 23% \par and 26% in September and October, respectively - domestic demand is weak, \par crimping the potential upside for the won and making it a less attractive \par proposition than other regional units, said some analysts. \par  \par   Geopolitics also detracts from the won's allure as a vehicle to capitalize \par on the pickup in global demand. \par  \par   "There's always still the unfavorable background of the North Korean nuclear \par standoff, which carries an escalation risk the market hasn't fully \par discounted," said Jan Lambregts, strategist at Rabobank. \par  \par   Politics will affect some exchange rates in Southeast Asia next year. "The \par 2004 presidential elections in both Indonesia and the Philippines bring along \par plenty of risks to the perceived political stability of these countries," said \par Lambregts. \par  \par  \par  \par   Asian Units May Guide Global \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par  \par  \par  \par   In Thailand, the authorities are worried about the contractionary effects of \par a stronger \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , but real-money flows likely will ensure the baht \par appreciates into 2004. \par  \par   Indeed, with Bangkok expecting 2003 economic growth to top 6% aided by both \par domestic consumption and external demand, it is little surprise that analysts \par expect more foreign capital will flow into local stocks. \par  \par   While domestic economic strength in Malaysia, the Philippines and Indonesia \par has been modest at best, "Thailand has been an absolute star performer in \par 2003," said Lambregts. "Though recent signs suggest growth may ease some into \par 2004, the overall performance of the Thai economy should remain strong, which \par in turn should see ongoing equity inflows and an appreciating Thai baht." \par  \par   An export-led recovery in an Asia economy is probably best illustrated by \par gross domestic product numbers out of Singapore. The island city-state's \par economy grew 1.7% on year in the second quarter, or an ann  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCS000020031202dzc20009c\par }\par \b \uc2 Zusammenfassung der Suche\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalph